{"total_count":25332,"incomplete_results":false,"items":[{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cibakhun/sc-patch-archive/issues/333","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cibakhun/sc-patch-archive","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cibakhun/sc-patch-archive/issues/333/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cibakhun/sc-patch-archive/issues/333/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cibakhun/sc-patch-archive/issues/333/events","html_url":"https://github.com/cibakhun/sc-patch-archive/pull/333","id":6312964188,"node_id":"PR_kwDOTAg6Fc8AAAABOLaeEQ","number":333,"title":"Hangar: Ursa Medivac ohne zweite Hülle aus dem Innenraum-Container","user":{"login":"cibakhun","id":72469315,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjcyNDY5MzE1","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/72469315?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun","html_url":"https://github.com/cibakhun","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[{"login":"cibakhun","id":72469315,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjcyNDY5MzE1","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/72469315?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun","html_url":"https://github.com/cibakhun","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false}],"milestone":null,"comments":0,"created_at":"2026-11-10T18:12:54Z","updated_at":"2026-11-10T18:22:05Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":{"login":"cibakhun","id":72469315,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjcyNDY5MzE1","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/72469315?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun","html_url":"https://github.com/cibakhun","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cibakhun/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"author_association":"OWNER","active_lock_reason":null,"draft":false,"pull_request":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cibakhun/sc-patch-archive/pulls/333","html_url":"https://github.com/cibakhun/sc-patch-archive/pull/333","diff_url":"https://github.com/cibakhun/sc-patch-archive/pull/333.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/cibakhun/sc-patch-archive/pull/333.patch","merged_at":null},"body":"<!-- claude-projects-attribution: {\"github_login\":\"cibakhun\"} -->\n_Requested by **Krisz** · [project thread](https://claude.ai/code/project/chan_01VMhoKYVi2nto1gBezHXNQ1?thread=cmsg_01VMhoKYVi2nto1gBezHXNQ1A9iS8Kb17xqQCpPZKJsiAy)_\n\n## Worum es geht\n\nKrisz, 10.11.2026, Screenshot des Ursa Medivac in der Halle: „madivac ist komplett broken“. Über der roten Hülle lag eine braune zweite, versetzt, und ein Rad schien zu schweben.\n\n**Ursache:** Der Container `medivac_int` setzt unter seinen 25 Teilen auch `RSI_Ursa_Rover.cga`, die Rumpfdatei des Fahrzeugs selbst. Der Rohexport legt dafür die ganze Hülle in den Container. Durch den Versatz des Containers stand sie als zweite Karosserie 1,5 m vor und 0,6 m neben der echten, in der Palette des Containers (braun). Im Spiel gibt es sie nicht.\n\nÜber alle 231 Rohexporte tragen neun Schiffe eine solche Kopie (Ursa Medivac, Argo SRV, Crusader Intrepid, Drake Corsair, Guardian, Guardian QI, Guardian MX, Aurora Mk II, Zeus CL). Sichtbar war nur der Medivac. Bei den anderen fiel der Innenraum im Rumpf schon weg.\n\n## Änderungen\n\n- **Bauregel** `dropBodyCopies` in `scripts/build-hangar-assets.mjs`, Rechnung in `scripts/lib/hangar-huellkopie.mjs`. Sie greift vor `placeContainers`: Ist das Netz eines Knotens unter einem `interior_*`-Container eine Fahrzeug-Rumpfdatei, nimmt sie es heraus. Das gilt für die eigene Datei und für die eines anderen Fahrzeugs aus `ships-dec`. `trisRaw` zählt weiter mit, damit `dropInterior` gleich entscheidet. Das Manifest trägt `bodyCopies`. Die Regel wurde am PC gegen alle 231 Rohexporte geprüft und trifft genau die neun.\n- **Medivac-Modell korrigiert:** `interior_part_23` ist entfernt (13 271 Dreiecke), ebenso die Netze, Materialien und Bilder, die nur sie nutzte. Jetzt 201 698 statt 214 969 Dreiecke und 219 statt 223 Bilder. Manifest, Dock-Bild und Draufsicht sind neu. Die Maße betragen jetzt 5,5 × 7,8 × 3,5 m statt 5,5 × 9,3 × 4,7 m, so viel wie beim Ursa Rover.\n- **Tor** `verify:hangar-lage` [5]: Ein Innenraumteil im Rumpf (`extras.interior`) darf höchstens 0,5 m über den Hüllquader des übrigen Rumpfs ragen. Mehr geht nur mit benannter Ausnahme. Es gibt eine Klinke je Schiff und `mitRagt` gesamt, dazu einen Zombie-Wächter. Die Rechnung `overhang` steht in `scripts/lib/hangar-lage.mjs`, Fassung `hangar-lage 3`. Die Kopie hing an der echten Hülle und war deshalb für [4] keine Insel. [2] misst nur den begehbaren Innenraum.\n  - Benannte Ausnahmen: vier Avenger (Feuerlöscherschrank über dem Dach, 1,52 m) und die 325a (Teil 32 m seitlich). Beide stehen schon als Ausnahme der losen Teile [4].\n  - **Vorgeführt rot** mit dem alten Medivac in `dist/`:\n    ```\n    [5] rsi-ursa-medivac: 1 Innenraumteile ragen ueber die Huelle (interior_part_23 13271 Dr. 1.5 m, rsi_medivac_mtl_Painted_Metal_Tiled_Grey_021) ohne benannte Ausnahme: …\n    [5] 6 Schiffe mit Innenraumteilen ueber der Huelle, Klinke 5\n    ```\n- Notiz `.planning/notes/hangar-huellkopie.md`, WINDOWS #150 (Sichturteil Krisz).\n\n## Nicht in diesem PR\n\nBeim Medivac fehlen weiter zwei der sechs Reifen, an ihrer Stelle sitzt ein Querlenker. Der Rohexport verschiebt bei mehrfach gesetzten Teilen die Netze um eins. Betroffen sind 217 von 231 Schiffen. Die Reparatur braucht eine eigene Bauregel und einen Neubau am PC, darüber entscheidet Krisz.\n\n## Geprüft\n\n- `STAGING=1 npm run build` und `npm run gate`: grün (43/43)\n- `npm run build` und `npm run gate`: grün (37/37)\n- `verify:hangar-lage`: 231 Schiffe gemessen und Klinke gezogen, danach ohne Schalter grün. Rot vorgeführt wie oben.\n- `check:hangar-shader --ship rsi-ursa-medivac` gegen den Vorschau-Bau: höchstens 16 Texturen ✓. Halle mit dem Medivac vorher und nachher aufgenommen und angesehen.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01McMXkjwZdjNisdMdaP2PmW)_\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated description by cubic. -->\n---\n## Summary by cubic\nFixes the Ursa Medivac showing a second, offset hull in the hangar by removing vehicle hull files from interior containers.\n\n- Adds a build rule (`dropBodyCopies`) that strips hull files from `interior_*` containers before placement; nine ships carried such copies though only the Medivac showed one visibly.\n- Removes `interior_part_23` from the Medivac model and regenerates its manifest, dock image, and top-down card; dimensions now match the Ursa Rover (5.5 × 7.8 × 3.5 m).\n- Extends `verify:hangar-lage` with check [5]: interior parts may protrude at most 0.5 m past the hull, with named exceptions for four Avengers and the 325a.\n\nKnown and out of scope: two of the Medivac's six wheels are still missing due to swapped meshes for repeated parts (217 ships affected), which needs a separate build rule and PC rebuild.\n\n<sup>Written for commit 9364552eb6b7fd1262617e201c713a40f2202101. Summary will update on new commits.</sup>\n\n<a href=\"https://cubic.dev/pr/cibakhun/sc-patch-archive/pull/333?utm_source=github\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-no-image-dialog=\"true\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-dark.svg\"><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-light.svg\"><img alt=\"View guided diff\" src=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/review-in-cubic-light.svg\"></picture></a> <a href=\"https://www.cubic.dev/action/auto-fix/pr/cibakhun/sc-patch-archive/333?returnTo=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fcibakhun%2Fsc-patch-archive%2Fpull%2F333&source=description\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-no-image-dialog=\"true\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/turn-on-auto-fix-dark.svg\"><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/turn-on-auto-fix-light.svg\"><img alt=\"Turn on auto-fix\" src=\"https://www.cubic.dev/buttons/turn-on-auto-fix-light.svg\"></picture></a>\n\n<!-- End of auto-generated description by cubic. -->\n\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cibakhun/sc-patch-archive/issues/333/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cibakhun/sc-patch-archive/issues/333/timeline","performed_via_github_app":{"id":1236702,"client_id":"Iv23liqTIFEtdIu6Vn1r","slug":"claude","node_id":"A_kwHOBIuudM4AEt7e","owner":{"login":"anthropics","id":76263028,"node_id":"MDEyOk9yZ2FuaXphdGlvbjc2MjYzMDI4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/76263028?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/anthropics","html_url":"https://github.com/anthropics","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/anthropics/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/anthropics/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/anthropics/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/anthropics/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/anthropics/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/anthropics/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/anthropics/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/anthropics/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/anthropics/received_events","type":"Organization","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"name":"Claude","description":"Run Claude Code from your GitHub Pull Requests and Issues to respond to reviewer feedback, fix CI errors, or modify code, turning it into a virtual teammate that works alongside your development pipelines.\r\n\r\nThis is built on the publicly available Claude Code SDK.","external_url":"https://anthropic.com/claude-code","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/claude","created_at":"2025-04-30T17:54:24Z","updated_at":"2026-11-06T04:29:41Z","permissions":{"actions":"write","administration":"read","checks":"write","contents":"write","discussions":"write","issues":"write","members":"read","merge_queues":"read","metadata":"read","pull_requests":"write","repository_creation":"write","repository_hooks":"write","statuses":"read","workflows":"write"},"events":["branch_protection_rule","check_run","check_suite","commit_comment","discussion","discussion_comment","issues","issue_comment","merge_group","merge_queue_entry","pull_request","pull_request_review","pull_request_review_comment","pull_request_review_thread","push","release","repository_ruleset","status","sub_issues"]},"state_reason":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/stackrox/stackrox/issues/21529","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/stackrox/stackrox","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/stackrox/stackrox/issues/21529/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/stackrox/stackrox/issues/21529/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/stackrox/stackrox/issues/21529/events","html_url":"https://github.com/stackrox/stackrox/pull/21529","id":4789398094,"node_id":"PR_kwDOGd6UEM7s3udy","number":21529,"title":"feat(scanner): add --certs-secret flag to scannerctl","user":{"login":"jvdm","id":291493,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI5MTQ5Mw==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/291493?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jvdm","html_url":"https://github.com/jvdm","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jvdm/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jvdm/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jvdm/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jvdm/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jvdm/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jvdm/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jvdm/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jvdm/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jvdm/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":4083678216,"node_id":"LA_kwDOGd6UEM7zZ_wI","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/stackrox/stackrox/labels/area/scanner","name":"area/scanner","color":"82B098","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":6800753323,"node_id":"LA_kwDOGd6UEM8AAAABlVtCqw","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/stackrox/stackrox/labels/auto-retest","name":"auto-retest","color":"025AAF","default":false,"description":"PRs with this label will be automatically retested if prow checks fails","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":12961519213,"node_id":"LA_kwDOGd6UEM8AAAADBJEWbQ","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/stackrox/stackrox/labels/auto-rebase","name":"auto-rebase","color":"D0BF47","default":false,"description":"PRs with this label will be automatically rebased","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":3,"created_at":"2026-07-01T21:40:45Z","updated_at":"2026-11-10T18:19:25Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"draft":false,"pull_request":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/stackrox/stackrox/pulls/21529","html_url":"https://github.com/stackrox/stackrox/pull/21529","diff_url":"https://github.com/stackrox/stackrox/pull/21529.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/stackrox/stackrox/pull/21529.patch","merged_at":null},"body":"## Description\n\nAdds a `--certs-secret` flag to `scannerctl` that loads TLS certificates directly from a Kubernetes secret via the current kubeconfig, removing the need to manually extract certs to disk before scanning.\n\nThe flag accepts `[namespace/]name` notation — namespace defaults to `stackrox`. When neither `--certs` nor `--certs-secret` is provided, `stackrox/central-tls` is tried automatically (failure is a warning, not an error).\n\nIn-memory cert injection was considered but is not feasible without modifying the shared `pkg/mtls` package, which reads files via `os.ReadFile` and `tls.LoadX509KeyPair`. Instead, certs are written to a temp directory that is cleaned up on process exit via `defer` in `run()`.\n\n## User-facing documentation\n\n- [x] CHANGELOG.md is updated OR update is not needed\n- [ ] documentation PR is created and is linked above OR is not needed\n\n## Testing and quality\n\n- [x] the change is production ready: the change is [GA](https://github.com/stackrox/stackrox/blob/master/PR_GA.md), or otherwise the functionality is gated by a [feature flag](https://github.com/stackrox/stackrox/blob/master/pkg/features/README.md)\n- [ ] CI results are [inspected](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1d5ga073jkv4CO1kAJqp8MPGpC6E1bwyrCGZ7S5wKg3w/edit?tab=t.0#heading=h.w4ercgtcg0xp)\n\n### Automated testing\n\n- [x] added unit tests\n- [ ] added e2e tests\n- [ ] added regression tests\n- [ ] added compatibility tests\n- [ ] modified existing tests\n\n### How I validated my change\n\nBuilt scannerctl from the branch and scanned OCP images against a staging ACS cluster using all three flag forms: default (no flag), name-only (`--certs-secret central-tls`), and explicit namespace (`--certs-secret stackrox/central-tls`). Verified successful mTLS authentication, scan completion, and temp directory cleanup after exit.","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/stackrox/stackrox/issues/21529/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/stackrox/stackrox/issues/21529/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rustfs/operator/issues/297","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rustfs/operator","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rustfs/operator/issues/297/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rustfs/operator/issues/297/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rustfs/operator/issues/297/events","html_url":"https://github.com/rustfs/operator/pull/297","id":6252522754,"node_id":"PR_kwDOPKLvpc8AAAABNbgUPw","number":297,"title":"feat(tls): support mTLS with cert-manager","user":{"login":"boylee1111","id":2533897,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI1MzM4OTc=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/2533897?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/boylee1111","html_url":"https://github.com/boylee1111","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/boylee1111/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/boylee1111/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/boylee1111/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/boylee1111/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/boylee1111/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/boylee1111/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/boylee1111/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/boylee1111/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/boylee1111/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":2,"created_at":"2026-11-06T23:30:26Z","updated_at":"2026-11-10T18:18:52Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"draft":true,"pull_request":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rustfs/operator/pulls/297","html_url":"https://github.com/rustfs/operator/pull/297","diff_url":"https://github.com/rustfs/operator/pull/297.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/rustfs/operator/pull/297.patch","merged_at":null},"body":"## Type of Change\n- [x] New Feature\n- [ ] Bug Fix\n- [x] Documentation\n- [ ] Performance Improvement\n- [x] Test/CI\n- [ ] Refactor\n- [ ] Other:\n\n## Related Issues\n- Follow-up for distroless `/health/ready` probing under mTLS: https://github.com/rustfs/rustfs/issues/9256\n\n## Summary of Changes\nComplete end-to-end mutual TLS (mTLS) support with `cert-manager` when `caTrust.clientCaSecretRef` (or legacy `certManager.caSecretRef`) is configured on a `Tenant`, covering both client-to-Pod S3/Admin API connections and Pod-to-Pod internode RPC connections on `:9000` without introducing redundant CRD fields:\n- **TLS Plan (`src/types/v1alpha1/tls.rs`, `src/reconcile/pool_lifecycle.rs`)**:\n  - Add `TlsConfig::is_mtls_enabled()` driven by `caTrust.clientCaSecretRef` (and legacy `certManager.caSecretRef`).\n  - Force `internode_scheme = \"https\"` whenever mTLS is enabled so distributed `RUSTFS_VOLUMES` peer URLs always use `https://`.\n  - When mTLS is enabled, set `RUSTFS_SERVER_MTLS_ENABLE=true`, project `client_ca.crt` from `clientCaSecretRef`, and project the default certificate's `tls.crt` and `tls.key` to `client_cert.pem` and `client_key.pem` (alongside `rustfs_cert.pem` and `rustfs_key.pem`) for outbound Pod-to-Pod connections.\n- **cert-manager Reconciliation & Validation (`src/reconcile/tls.rs`)**:\n  - Block `tls.mode: disabled` when `clientCaSecretRef` is configured (`Reason::CertificateInvalid`).\n  - Default `spec.usages` on managed default `Certificate` resources to `[\"server auth\", \"client auth\"]` when mTLS is enabled and `certManager.usages` is omitted; reject explicit `certManager.usages` on the default certificate that omit `\"client auth\"` when mTLS is enabled.\n  - Validate the issued default certificate against the configured `clientCaSecretRef` trust anchors for `webpki::KeyUsage::client_auth()` before reporting TLS `Ready`.\n  - Include `mtls_enabled` in `tls_hash` to trigger StatefulSet rollout on changes.\n- **Workload Probes (`src/types/v1alpha1/tenant/workloads.rs`, `src/types/v1alpha1/tenant/helper.rs`)**:\n  - Switch `livenessProbe` and `startupProbe` to `tcpSocket` on port `9000` and `readinessProbe` to an `exec` probe (`curl -skf --cert <mountPath>/client_cert.pem --key <mountPath>/client_key.pem https://127.0.0.1:9000/health/ready`) by default when mTLS is enabled.\n  - Support `TENANT_MTLS_READINESS_PROBE=tcp` (via `tenant.spec.env` or operator environment) to fall back to `tcpSocket` on port `9000` for distroless container images without `curl` (tracked in https://github.com/rustfs/rustfs/issues/9256).\n  - Normalize Kubernetes API server probe defaults (`timeoutSeconds`, `periodSeconds`, `successThreshold`, `failureThreshold`, `initialDelaySeconds`, `httpGet.scheme`) when comparing container probes in `statefulset_needs_update_with_tls_plan_and_cluster_domain`.\n- **Operator Admin / Provisioning / STS Client (`crates/rustfs-admin/src/lib.rs`, `src/sts/rustfs_client.rs`)**:\n  - Add `RustfsAdminClient::new_with_base_url_tls_material` and `load_tenant_tls_client_identity` so the operator presents the Tenant's default certificate (`status.certificates.tls.serverSecretRef`) as its client identity when reconciling buckets/users/policies, pool lifecycle, monitoring, and STS against mTLS-enabled Tenants.\n- **Docs, Examples & Tests**:\n  - Update `docs/operator-user-guide.md`, `docs/operator-user-guide.zh-CN.md`, `deploy/rustfs-operator/README.md`, and `CHANGELOG.md`.\n  - Add `examples/cert-manager-mtls-tenant.yaml` and update `examples/cert-manager-ca-trust-tenant.yaml`.\n  - Add unit and e2e tests covering volume projections, `Certificate` usages and `client_auth` chain verification, mTLS probes (both default `exec` and `TENANT_MTLS_READINESS_PROBE=tcp`), probe default normalization, and operator client identity loading.\n\n## Checklist\n- [x] I have read and followed the [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md) guidelines\n- [x] Passed `make pre-commit` (fmt-check + clippy + test + console-lint + console-fmt-check)\n- [x] Added/updated necessary tests\n- [x] Documentation updated (if needed)\n- [x] CHANGELOG.md updated under `[Unreleased]` (if user-visible change)\n- [x] CI/CD passed (if applicable)\n\n## Impact\n- [ ] Breaking change (CRD/API compatibility)\n- [x] Requires doc/config/deployment update\n- [ ] Other impact: No CRD schema changes required; `caTrust.clientCaSecretRef` is used as the single source of truth for enabling mTLS.\n\n## Verification\n```bash\nmake release-metadata-check fmt-check clippy test e2e-check\n```\nAlso verified live on a 4-node Kind cluster (`kind-rustfs-e2e`) with `cert-manager` (`v1.16.2`) and `rustfs/rustfs:1.0.4`, as well as a 3-node distroless appliance cluster (`TENANT_MTLS_READINESS_PROBE=tcp`):\n- `Certificate` issued with `usages: [\"server auth\", \"client auth\"]`\n- All pods reached `1/1 Running` (`Ready`) with `readQuorum: true` and `writeQuorum: true` over HTTP/2 + mTLS\n- Peer HTTPS requests without a client certificate rejected with `tlsv13 alert certificate required`; requests with `client_cert.pem`/`client_key.pem` succeeded with `HTTP/2 200`\n- Operator bucket provisioning (`spec.buckets`) succeeded over mTLS (`ProvisioningReady=True`)","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rustfs/operator/issues/297/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rustfs/operator/issues/297/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/llvm/llvm-project/issues/239010","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/llvm/llvm-project","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/llvm/llvm-project/issues/239010/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/llvm/llvm-project/issues/239010/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/llvm/llvm-project/issues/239010/events","html_url":"https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/pull/239010","id":6213113306,"node_id":"PR_kwDOBITxeM8AAAABM75APA","number":239010,"title":"[Clang][SYCL] Compile device code once per IGCA target","user":{"login":"srividya-sundaram","id":8020274,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgwMjAyNzQ=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/8020274?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/srividya-sundaram","html_url":"https://github.com/srividya-sundaram","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/srividya-sundaram/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/srividya-sundaram/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/srividya-sundaram/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/srividya-sundaram/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/srividya-sundaram/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/srividya-sundaram/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/srividya-sundaram/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/srividya-sundaram/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/srividya-sundaram/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":1,"created_at":"2026-11-04T23:06:44Z","updated_at":"2026-11-10T18:18:19Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"draft":false,"pull_request":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/llvm/llvm-project/pulls/239010","html_url":"https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/pull/239010","diff_url":"https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/pull/239010.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/pull/239010.patch","merged_at":null},"body":"Compile SYCL device code once per IGCA target.\n\nSeveral Intel GPUs can share an IGCA target, e.g. `xe-mtl-u` and `xe-mtl-h` are both `igca_30r`. Until now, `-fsycl --offload-arch=xe-mtl-u,xe-mtl-h` compiled the device code once per GPU. Now it is compiled once for `igca_30r`, and then for both GPUs with a single `ocloc -device xe-mtl-h,xe-mtl-u` call.\n\nThe device image records the GPUs, as `arch=igca_30r,code=xe-mtl-h,xe-mtl-u`. `clang-linker-wrapper` links device code with the same `arch` and `code` together, passes the GPUs to `clang-sycl-linker` with a new hidden `--device=` option, and merges the results into one offload binary, as the SYCL runtime loads only one. An IGCA target named directly, e.g. `--offload-arch=igca_30r`, has no GPUs and is compiled just in time.\n\nDevice code compiled for different GPUs is linked separately. For example, with `a.o` compiled for `xe-mtl-u` and `b.o` for `xe-mtl-u,xe-mtl-h`, a function defined in `a.cpp` and called from device code in `b.cpp` will not be found. Until now, they were linked together for `xe-mtl-u`. Linking them together again is left to a follow-up.\n\n`-Xarch_` now names the IGCA target, e.g. `-Xarch_igca_20ca` for `--offload-arch=xe-pvc`.\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/llvm/llvm-project/issues/239010/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/llvm/llvm-project/issues/239010/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/oldsj/mainloop/issues/347","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/oldsj/mainloop","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/oldsj/mainloop/issues/347/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/oldsj/mainloop/issues/347/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/oldsj/mainloop/issues/347/events","html_url":"https://github.com/oldsj/mainloop/pull/347","id":6311679294,"node_id":"PR_kwDOQquIis8AAAABOKYXow","number":347,"title":"kagent v1 API: no suspend/resume, forced delete, ServiceAccount auth (draft)","user":{"login":"oldsj","id":12104969,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEyMTA0OTY5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/12104969?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/oldsj","html_url":"https://github.com/oldsj","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/oldsj/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/oldsj/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/oldsj/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/oldsj/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/oldsj/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/oldsj/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/oldsj/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/oldsj/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/oldsj/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":0,"created_at":"2026-11-10T16:59:04Z","updated_at":"2026-11-10T18:18:09Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"OWNER","active_lock_reason":null,"draft":true,"pull_request":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/oldsj/mainloop/pulls/347","html_url":"https://github.com/oldsj/mainloop/pull/347","diff_url":"https://github.com/oldsj/mainloop/pull/347.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/oldsj/mainloop/pull/347.patch","merged_at":null},"body":"## Summary\n\nMoves Mainloop to kagent's new control API (`kagent.api.v1`, the fork's `mainloop-substrate-1-0` branch on Substrate 1.0). Draft: tested with fakes, plus one bounded router check on Kind (below). It must not merge before the ported kagent is deployed.\n\n- **API package.** Vendored protos and generated clients are `kagent.api.v1`. They are upstream kagent-dev/kagent 61b153c1 with the fork additions the port keeps re-applied by hand, because the fork branch had no commits yet. `SOURCE` says so and is marked provisional.\n- **No suspend or resume.** kagent parks an idle Session's Actor itself and the Session stays READY. Mainloop reads asleep or awake from `runtime_association.current_active`. The next send, a workspace preparation, or a connection through the Substrate router wakes it.\n  - The workspace `suspend` and `resume` operations, `POST /workspaces/{id}/suspend` and `/resume`, and the idle sweep are removed. A queued old operation is closed as cancelled.\n  - `Wake` only refuses a workspace that is being deleted, and records the use.\n  - `suspended` now means asleep; `suspending` is no longer reported.\n  - Sends no longer resume anything. The main-thread provider switch leaves the old Session as it is.\n- **Forced delete.** Session restart and the task force-stop use `DeleteSession(force=true)`. Other deletes are not forced. Task stop presses nothing at the first grace, and a sleeping runtime counts as stopped.\n- **Live relay and preview.** The actor comes from the runtime association when kagent reports one, else kagent's stable name `session-<id>` in `KAGENT_ACTOR_ATESPACE`. Neither asks for a wake; the router connection is the wake.\n- **Router mTLS.** The Substrate 1.0 router has no plain-HTTP listener. The preview proxy and the live relay (one dial function) now CONNECT to its TLS listener on `:8444`. They verify it as `atenet-router.ate-system.svc` against the `servicedns` trust bundle and present a `podidentity` pod certificate, re-reading both files on every connect. The chart's `substrateRouterAuth.enabled` (on in production and live-dev) mounts both projected volumes into the API container only. An `http` router address is refused at startup. Needs `spiffe://cluster.local/ns/<ns>/sa/mainloop-backend` in the router's `ingressAllowedSPIFFEIDs`; the infra cutover branch lists `mainloop` and `mainloop-dev`.\n- **Auth.** The chart mounts a projected ServiceAccount token (audience `kagent-invocation`) instead of the `kagent-control-token` Secret.\n- **Web.** The workspace page has no Suspend or Resume button, and says a sleeping workspace wakes on use.\n- **Docs.** Specs (workspaces, sessions, chat, orchestration-health, tasks), architecture and README are updated. Live measurements from the previous kagent API are kept and labelled as such.\n\n## Not in this PR\n\n- Re-vendoring the protos from the real fork branch and pinning `SOURCE` to the deployed revision.\n\n## Assumptions to confirm with kagent-port\n\nEach is isolated in `server/internal/kagent` or `server/internal/liveactor`.\n\n1. The hand-applied fork additions match what the port lands (Session fields 16-22, CreateSessionRequest fields 6-8, `PrepareSessionWorkspace`, `ForkSessionRequest.fresh_conversation = 3`).\n2. `Session.agent` is `AgentProvenance{ref, uid}`; Mainloop compares only `ref`.\n3. The Actor name is `session-<lower-case id>` for the Session's life.\n4. A router connection to a sleeping Actor wakes it, and kagent then tracks it as active.\n5. A sleeping Session still reports its association (`current_active=false`), so `PrepareSessionWorkspace` has a generation id and actor uid to send. Mainloop never interprets `generation_id`; it only echoes it.\n6. A stale generation on `PrepareSessionWorkspace` is FAILED_PRECONDITION or ABORTED; Mainloop reads again and retries.\n7. `current_active` is reliable on every `GetSession`: task stop treats \"not active\" as stopped.\n8. `DeleteSession` without force is refused while a turn runs; with force it terminates the turn.\n9. A brief `SUSPENDED` state or a suspend, resume or revert operation means \"busy, read again\".\n10. The workspace setup digests are unchanged in the port. They were re-read at oldsj/kagent b785c99d (the newest fork commit with preparation code; the same as a1164013), and `setupDigestsSource` follows the provisional pin.\n\n## Measured on Kind (Substrate 1.0.1, `kind-substrate-1-0`)\n\nThis used a throwaway test binary that calls the branch's real `connectActor` and `routerTLS`; it is not committed. It ran in a pod with the chart's two projected volumes. The Mainloop backend itself was not deployed there.\n\n- As `ate-system/atenet-client`, which is allow-listed: CONNECT to a suspended `substrate-demo` actor answered `200`, and `GET /hostname` through the tunnel returned the actor's reply. The router logged `src.identity=spiffe://cluster.local/ns/ate-system/sa/atenet-client` and `ate.router.resume=triggered`, so the connection woke the actor.\n- As `default/default`, which is not allow-listed: the router answered the CONNECT with `200`, then closed the tunnel and logged \"network authorization denied\". The fake router now models this. A preview request from a client off the allow-list is a `502` and is not replayed.\n\n## Known gaps\n\n- Push enrollment no longer wakes an idle Actor (there is no model-free wake). It waits for the preparation or first message.\n- `dev.idle_timeout_minutes` is stored and returned but not enforced.\n- A preview of a just-woken workspace fails until the agent restarts its dev server, because processes do not survive sleep.\n- `RevertSession` is not called. `ForceSuspendAfter` remains as a setting that does nothing.\n\n## Checks run locally\n\n- **Server:** `go test -count=1 ./...` against a disposable PostgreSQL 16 (Linux container, no `-race`). Under full parallel load, a few timing tests in app (SSE heartbeat), devloop (sync waits), hitl and sessions missed their waits; each package passes when run alone, and the two sessions tests pass 3 of 3 alone. CI's `-race` run is the real check. golangci-lint v2.14.0, 0 issues; `go generate` and `go mod tidy`.\n- **API:** `redocly lint` valid (7 warnings, as on main).\n- **Web:** `tsc -b`, `eslint .`, `vite build`, and vitest on workspaces, health and projects (149 tests).\n- **Repository:** `make lint`.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/oldsj/mainloop/issues/347/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/oldsj/mainloop/issues/347/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/adamtrott78/workspace-control/issues/595","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/adamtrott78/workspace-control","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/adamtrott78/workspace-control/issues/595/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/adamtrott78/workspace-control/issues/595/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/adamtrott78/workspace-control/issues/595/events","html_url":"https://github.com/adamtrott78/workspace-control/pull/595","id":6312041543,"node_id":"PR_kwDOUffjcc8AAAABOKq9gw","number":595,"title":"Workspace Shell: add PADataset evidence + bounded research target","user":{"login":"adamtrott78","id":61768467,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjYxNzY4NDY3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/61768467?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/adamtrott78","html_url":"https://github.com/adamtrott78","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adamtrott78/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adamtrott78/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adamtrott78/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adamtrott78/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adamtrott78/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adamtrott78/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adamtrott78/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adamtrott78/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adamtrott78/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":10,"created_at":"2026-11-10T17:19:46Z","updated_at":"2026-11-10T18:18:05Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"OWNER","active_lock_reason":null,"draft":true,"pull_request":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/adamtrott78/workspace-control/pulls/595","html_url":"https://github.com/adamtrott78/workspace-control/pull/595","diff_url":"https://github.com/adamtrott78/workspace-control/pull/595.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/adamtrott78/workspace-control/pull/595.patch","merged_at":null},"body":"Extends the accepted Workspace Shell lineage with task-bound PADataset access: hardened Git provenance, bounded file list/read/hash profiles, sandboxed network-denied research Python execution, fail-closed target/profile policy, regression tests, and a rollback-capable Lambda upgrade operator. The MTH602 authority mirror is included only to bind Workspace Shell; PADataset TASK.md remains scientific authority. Live deployment is explicitly gated on exact-head validation and PADataset SHA 17e1147614f27702e43565089d618182ff71c5af.","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/adamtrott78/workspace-control/issues/595/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/adamtrott78/workspace-control/issues/595/timeline","performed_via_github_app":{"id":1144995,"client_id":"Iv23liVemv8A9if9v0F2","slug":"chatgpt-codex-connector","node_id":"A_kwHOAOQ6Gs4AEXij","owner":{"login":"openai","id":14957082,"node_id":"MDEyOk9yZ2FuaXphdGlvbjE0OTU3MDgy","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/14957082?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai","html_url":"https://github.com/openai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/received_events","type":"Organization","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"name":"ChatGPT Codex Connector","description":"Bring ChatGPT and Codex to your GitHub repositories.","external_url":"https://www.chatgpt.com","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/chatgpt-codex-connector","created_at":"2025-02-14T01:37:05Z","updated_at":"2026-09-23T18:52:59Z","permissions":{"actions":"write","checks":"read","contents":"write","emails":"read","issues":"write","metadata":"read","pull_requests":"write","statuses":"read","workflows":"write"},"events":["check_run","check_suite","commit_comment","issues","issue_comment","pull_request","pull_request_review","pull_request_review_comment","pull_request_review_thread","repository","status","sub_issues"]},"state_reason":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/intercolton/CASTP-Project/issues/8","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/intercolton/CASTP-Project","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/intercolton/CASTP-Project/issues/8/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/intercolton/CASTP-Project/issues/8/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/intercolton/CASTP-Project/issues/8/events","html_url":"https://github.com/intercolton/CASTP-Project/pull/8","id":6312065207,"node_id":"PR_kwDOVog0c88AAAABOKsL1Q","number":8,"title":"TLS 1.3 и контракт Authenticator (задача 3)","user":{"login":"intercolton","id":142524954,"node_id":"U_kgDOCH7CGg","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/142524954?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/intercolton","html_url":"https://github.com/intercolton","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intercolton/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intercolton/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intercolton/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intercolton/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intercolton/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intercolton/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intercolton/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intercolton/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intercolton/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[],"state":"closed","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":1,"created_at":"2026-11-10T17:21:11Z","updated_at":"2026-11-10T18:17:46Z","closed_at":"2026-11-10T18:17:46Z","assignee":null,"author_association":"OWNER","active_lock_reason":null,"draft":false,"pull_request":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/intercolton/CASTP-Project/pulls/8","html_url":"https://github.com/intercolton/CASTP-Project/pull/8","diff_url":"https://github.com/intercolton/CASTP-Project/pull/8.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/intercolton/CASTP-Project/pull/8.patch","merged_at":"2026-11-10T18:17:46Z"},"body":"<!-- CURSOR_AGENT_PR_BODY_BEGIN -->\n## Зачем\n\nЗадача 3 плана ASTP-M: конфиг TLS 1.3 и контракт `Authenticator` в крейте `astp-crypto`. База — текущий `main` `6ed7d8a` (MSRV 1.89, CI, `FrameReader`, герметичный DNS-тест), не `6d25c45` из брифа. Предварительные PR уже влиты. `astp-frame` не менялся.\n\nКрейт не открывает сокеты, не говорит на HTTP и не задаёт боевой формат учётных данных. Своей криптографии и форка rustls нет: rustls 0.23 с провайдером ring.\n\n## Что сделано\n\n- `server_config` / `client_config` → `Arc<rustls::ServerConfig>` / `Arc<rustls::ClientConfig>`.\n- Только TLS 1.3, версии задаются в конфиге. Feature `tls12` у rustls во workspace не выключается.\n- Сервер: `max_early_data_size = 0`, `send_half_rtt_data = false`, ALPN `h3`, затем `h2` из `astp-types`. `astp-m/1` не предлагается.\n- mTLS только через явный `ClientAuth::Required(roots)` и `WebPkiClientVerifier`. В дефолтном профиле клиентский сертификат не запрашивается.\n- Сертификат и ключ: PEM-пути или DER (цепочка с листа).\n- Свои ключи (ключ сертификата сервера и ключ mTLS-клиента) — только Ed25519 или ECDSA P-256. RSA, P-384 и прочие свои ключи отвергаются при сборке конфига (`CryptoError::UnsupportedOwnKey`). Проверка чужих цепочек не сужается: алгоритмы верификации ring по умолчанию, включая RSA и P-384, остаются, чтобы публичный CA (в том числе промежуточные Let's Encrypt) продолжал проходить.\n- Клиент: `enable_early_data = false`. Корни — поле `TrustAnchors::Webpki` или `TrustAnchors::Explicit`, не feature. Имя проверяется. Литерал IP без `tls_server_name` — ошибка конфигурации. ALPN: `h3`,`h2` для QUIC и `h2` для TCP.\n- Канал: `CHANNEL_BINDING_LABEL = \"EXPORTER-ASTP-M auth v1\"`, `CHANNEL_BINDING_LEN = 32`, контекст `None`. `channel_binding` берёт его из `export_keying_material`.\n- `Authenticator`: `verify(binding, credential) -> Result<ClientId, AuthError>`. Варианты `Rejected` / `Malformed` / `Internal` — только для лога; для удалённой стороны это одна и та же неудача.\n- `StaticTokenAuthenticator`: HMAC-SHA256 (ring) от binding, сравнение `subtle::ConstantTimeEq`. Feature `test-util`, не в default. Это не формат учётных данных для продакшена.\n- README крейта с инвариантами, включая правило про свои ключи. Ошибки через `thiserror`. В библиотечном коде нет `unsafe`, `unwrap`, `expect`, `panic!`.\n\n## Тесты и пункты брифа\n\nФайл `crates/astp-crypto/tests/suite/tls.rs` (модуль unit-тестов, `cfg(test)`).\n\n1. `tls13_handshake_negotiates_h2` — handshake TLS 1.3 на localhost через tokio-rustls, CA от rcgen, PEM, согласован ALPN `h2`, версия TLS 1.3. Сервер предлагает `h3`,`h2` и не предлагает `astp-m/1`. Клиент QUIC предлагает `h3`,`h2`, TCP — `h2`.\n2. `tls12_only_client_is_rejected` — клиент только с TLS 1.2 (собран в тесте, не через публичный API) получает отказ.\n3. `rejects_untrusted_ca_wrong_name_and_ip_without_server_name` — чужой CA → отказ; неверное имя → отказ; `127.0.0.1`, `::1`, `[::1]`, `[127.0.0.1]` без `tls_server_name` → ошибка конфигурации. Дополнительно: пустой набор корней отвергается, `TrustAnchors::Webpki` не принимает тестовый CA.\n4. `early_data_is_not_offered_on_resumption` — у нашего сервера `max_early_data_size == 0` и `send_half_rtt_data == false`, у клиента `enable_early_data == false`. При возобновлении ClientHello содержит PSK (тип 41) и не содержит `early_data` (тип 42), `handshake_kind == Resumed`, `is_early_data_accepted == false`. Контроль: тот же кэш сессий, но с `enable_early_data = true`, против тестового сервера с `max_early_data_size = 4096` — расширение `early_data` есть. Проверка не пустая.\n5. `mtls_is_opt_in_and_default_profile_has_no_client_auth` — дефолтный профиль принимает клиента без сертификата и не видит peer cert; mTLS без сертификата — отказ; с сертификатом — успех; после этого дефолтный профиль по-прежнему без client auth.\n6. `channel_binding_matches_within_connection_and_differs_across` — обе стороны одного соединения дают один `ChannelBinding`, два соединения — разные. Трейт реализован для `rustls::Connection`, `ClientConnection` и `ServerConnection`.\n7. `authenticator_rejects_credential_from_another_connection` — верный credential проходит (в том числе на binding второй стороны того же соединения); credential с другого соединения отвергается; та же длина с изменённым байтом — `Rejected`; неверная длина и пустой секрет — `Malformed`.\n8. `ed25519_and_p256_certificates_are_accepted` — полная цепочка Ed25519 и полная цепочка P-256: и сертификат сервера, и клиентский сертификат mTLS.\n\nДополнительно `rsa_and_p384_own_keys_are_rejected`: свой ключ RSA и свой ключ P-384 дают `CryptoError::UnsupportedOwnKey` и для сервера, и для ключа mTLS-клиента. Тот же тест проверяет, что набор алгоритмов верификации клиента по-прежнему содержит `ECDSA_NISTP384_SHA384` и `RSA_PKCS1_SHA256`.\n\n## Cargo.lock\n\nНовых крейтов и версий нет. Изменилась только секция пакета `astp-crypto`: она теперь ссылается на уже лежащие в lockfile крейты:\n\n- `astp-types` 0.1.0 (path)\n- `rcgen` 0.14.10 (dev)\n- `ring` 0.17.14\n- `rustls` 0.23.46\n- `rustls-pki-types` 1.15.1\n- `subtle` 2.6.1\n- `tempfile` 3.27.0 (dev)\n- `thiserror` 2.0.21\n- `tokio` 1.54.1\n- `tokio-rustls` 0.26.6\n- `webpki-roots` 1.0.11\n\n`THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES` не менялся: кода anet нет, новых сторонних текстов нет. `ring` и `subtle` уже были в дереве через rustls.\n\n## Принятые отклонения от брифа\n\n1. Конфиг собирается через `builder_with_provider(...).with_protocol_versions(&[&TLS13])`, а не через `ServerConfig::builder_with_protocol_versions`. Метод из брифа берёт process-default `CryptoProvider` и паникует, если его нет. Библиотека не должна паниковать и не должна ставить провайдер на весь процесс (в том же процессе живёт пробник). Версия по-прежнему ограничена только в этом конфиге. Feature `tls12` у rustls во workspace не выключается: она нужна пробнику.\n\n2. Сравнение секрета в `StaticTokenAuthenticator` делает `subtle::ConstantTimeEq`, а не `ring::constant_time::verify_slices_are_equal`. В ring 0.17.14 эта функция deprecated и даёт warning, который ломает `-D warnings`. В тексте deprecation сказано, что это внутренняя функция без обещаний по побочным каналам. HMAC по-прежнему считает ring. Бриф прямо разрешает `subtle` или `ring::constant_time`.\n\n4. `channel_binding` — одна функция над sealed-трейтом `TlsSession`. Трейт реализован для `rustls::Connection`, `ClientConnection` и `ServerConnection`, потому что tokio-rustls после handshake отдаёт конкретное соединение, а не enum. QUIC-путь по-прежнему задача 4.\n\n5. `StaticTokenAuthenticator` спрятан за feature `test-util` (не в default features) и дополнительно собирается при `cfg(test)`. Так тесты этого крейта выполняются в CI командой `cargo test --workspace --locked` без `--all-features`. Для других крейтов тип доступен только с feature `test-util`. Это не формат учётных данных для продакшена. Сами тесты лежат в `tests/suite/tls.rs` и компилируются как unit-тесты крейта: отдельный integration-тест не видит `cfg(test)`.\n\nТест отказа от 0-RTT при возобновлении в rustls 0.23 получился полностью, включая контроль, что клиент с включённым early data расширение шлёт. Ничего из восьми обязательных тестов не выкинуто.\n\n## Не входит\n\nQUIC/TCP-листенеры, HTTP/2 и HTTP/3, заглушка, ACME, ротация сертификатов, формат учётных данных для продакшена, маскировка ClientHello. `astp-frame`, CI, Dockerfile и пробник не менялись.\n\n<!-- CURSOR_AGENT_PR_BODY_END -->\n\n<div><a href=\"https://cursor.com/agents/bc-3d2659be-a804-5652-8649-4ed96d6397a0?cursor_ref=pr_footer&cursor_cta=open_in_web\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://cursor.com/assets/images/open-in-web-dark.png\"><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://cursor.com/assets/images/open-in-web-light.png\"><img alt=\"Open in Web\" width=\"114\" height=\"28\" src=\"https://cursor.com/assets/images/open-in-web-dark.png\"></picture></a>&nbsp;<a href=\"https://cursor.com/background-agent?bcId=bc-3d2659be-a804-5652-8649-4ed96d6397a0&cursor_ref=pr_footer&cursor_cta=open_in_cursor\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://cursor.com/assets/images/open-in-cursor-dark.png\"><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://cursor.com/assets/images/open-in-cursor-light.png\"><img alt=\"Open in Cursor\" width=\"131\" height=\"28\" src=\"https://cursor.com/assets/images/open-in-cursor-dark.png\"></picture></a>&nbsp;</div>\n\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/intercolton/CASTP-Project/issues/8/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":1,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/intercolton/CASTP-Project/issues/8/timeline","performed_via_github_app":{"id":1210556,"client_id":"Iv23liGzp2hacMWjAyXc","slug":"cursor","node_id":"A_kwHOB44z8s4AEni8","owner":{"login":"cursor","id":126759922,"node_id":"O_kgDOB44z8g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/126759922?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cursor","html_url":"https://github.com/cursor","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cursor/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cursor/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cursor/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cursor/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cursor/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cursor/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cursor/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cursor/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cursor/received_events","type":"Organization","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"name":"Cursor","description":"Connect Cursor to GitHub to allow agents to do work for you in the background.","external_url":"https://cursor.com","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/cursor","created_at":"2025-04-09T22:42:17Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T22:22:58Z","permissions":{"actions":"write","administration":"read","checks":"write","contents":"write","deployments":"read","discussions":"write","emails":"read","issues":"write","members":"read","merge_queues":"write","metadata":"read","organization_custom_org_roles":"read","organization_custom_properties":"read","organization_custom_roles":"read","packages":"read","pages":"read","pull_requests":"write","statuses":"read","workflows":"write"},"events":["branch_protection_configuration","branch_protection_rule","check_run","check_suite","commit_comment","deployment","deployment_status","issues","issue_comment","label","member","membership","merge_queue_entry","organization","pull_request","pull_request_review","pull_request_review_comment","pull_request_review_thread","push","repository","repository_ruleset","status","team","team_add","workflow_job","workflow_run"]},"state_reason":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nace-ai/kapitsa/issues/1160","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nace-ai/kapitsa","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nace-ai/kapitsa/issues/1160/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nace-ai/kapitsa/issues/1160/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nace-ai/kapitsa/issues/1160/events","html_url":"https://github.com/nace-ai/kapitsa/pull/1160","id":6312784514,"node_id":"PR_kwDOVLn4Ys8AAAABOLRUjQ","number":1160,"title":"Flash-Next: batch index-cache growth copies","user":{"login":"chainhelen","id":7046329,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjcwNDYzMjk=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7046329?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/chainhelen","html_url":"https://github.com/chainhelen","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/chainhelen/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/chainhelen/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/chainhelen/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/chainhelen/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/chainhelen/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/chainhelen/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/chainhelen/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/chainhelen/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/chainhelen/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":1,"created_at":"2026-11-10T18:02:58Z","updated_at":"2026-11-10T18:17:35Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"draft":true,"pull_request":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nace-ai/kapitsa/pulls/1160","html_url":"https://github.com/nace-ai/kapitsa/pull/1160","diff_url":"https://github.com/nace-ai/kapitsa/pull/1160.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/nace-ai/kapitsa/pull/1160.patch","merged_at":null},"body":"## What changed and why\n\nFlash-Next index-cache growth retains every old index buffer until all replacements have been allocated and copied. Attention-cache growth already releases old buffers in batches, while the index-cache path does not. Long-context index caches can therefore retain substantially more old storage than the reservation calculation allows for copying.\n\nThis change applies the existing attention-cache batching policy to the index caches: allocate and copy a batch, wait for that command buffer, then release that batch's old buffers. Batches target 256 MiB of destination buffers and always include at least one buffer; a single larger buffer can exceed that target. Index layout and copied contents are unchanged by the implementation.\n\nIncludes `index_growth_check`, which loads the actual model class under the GPU lock, fills each index buffer with distinct deterministic bytes, grows the cache, and compares every retained byte. Its allocation sampling is diagnostic and may miss a transient maximum.\n\n## Default for\n\nAll Flash-Next index-cache growth, including the MTP index caches. No opt-in switch or hardware-specific gate. This is a Draft while the remaining validation below is completed.\n\n## Measured\n\nSanitized derived summary, not raw execution logs.\n\nRuns: Apple M5 Pro, macOS 26.6.2, Apple clang 17; synthetic 12-layer Flash-Next fixture generated with `tools/fn_synth.py --layers 12`. Reference source: `903cdfcebafb3d138d50c9c83e7787912ee26b81`; candidate production source: `cbbf2dcf614761d834e1e4129e6a621ace3a3d6e`. The preliminary probes used the same production change before commit; the checker subsequently gained strict capacity parsing and scoped sampler-thread cleanup.\n\n- Small GPU check, capacity 64 to 128, with `MTL_SHADER_VALIDATION=1 MTL_DEBUG_LAYER=1`: 8 index buffers, all 100,352 retained bytes equal to the input patterns.\n- Long-cache check, capacity 262,144 to 327,680: 8 index buffers, all 402,655,232 retained bytes equal in every A/B/A/B arm.\n- Preliminary growth-only A/B/A/B timings: reference 117.440 ms, candidate 108.121 ms, reference 109.697 ms, candidate 104.720 ms. These measure synthetic cache growth, not inference throughput. The probe also samples allocations; raw allocation records remain private.\n\n## Checked\n\n- Release build of the CLI, logit dump, and growth checker.\n- Actual GPU cache copies and full retained-byte comparisons as described above.\n- All 23 `vk cpu-tests` commands passed, including the CLI integration checks.\n- `prek run --all-files` passed. The checker rejects malformed, overflowing, and decreasing capacities before touching the GPU.\n\n## Not verified\n\n- Full-model output gate and end-to-end performance on the candidate are not yet complete. Synthetic buffer preservation is not a logits or inference-output result.\n- A second-device run is not yet complete.\n- The full-model long-context failure mode has not been reproduced as an allocation failure; the current evidence establishes the retention behavior and successful synthetic copies.\n\n## Remaining work before Ready\n\n- M3 reference/candidate builds are complete. Restore the missing trained n-gram table and validate the complete Flash-Next pack.\n- Run paired cache-growth checks and old/new logit dumps on that pack, compare the complete outputs with `tools/logit_compare.py`, and preserve all failures and limitations.\n- Complete applicable interleaved model performance coverage and second-setup validation; review any regression before marking Ready.\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nace-ai/kapitsa/issues/1160/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nace-ai/kapitsa/issues/1160/timeline","performed_via_github_app":{"id":1144995,"client_id":"Iv23liVemv8A9if9v0F2","slug":"chatgpt-codex-connector","node_id":"A_kwHOAOQ6Gs4AEXij","owner":{"login":"openai","id":14957082,"node_id":"MDEyOk9yZ2FuaXphdGlvbjE0OTU3MDgy","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/14957082?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai","html_url":"https://github.com/openai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/received_events","type":"Organization","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"name":"ChatGPT Codex Connector","description":"Bring ChatGPT and Codex to your GitHub repositories.","external_url":"https://www.chatgpt.com","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/chatgpt-codex-connector","created_at":"2025-02-14T01:37:05Z","updated_at":"2026-09-23T18:52:59Z","permissions":{"actions":"write","checks":"read","contents":"write","emails":"read","issues":"write","metadata":"read","pull_requests":"write","statuses":"read","workflows":"write"},"events":["check_run","check_suite","commit_comment","issues","issue_comment","pull_request","pull_request_review","pull_request_review_comment","pull_request_review_thread","repository","status","sub_issues"]},"state_reason":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/joshclements518/gh-cc-test/issues/2380","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/joshclements518/gh-cc-test","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/joshclements518/gh-cc-test/issues/2380/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/joshclements518/gh-cc-test/issues/2380/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/joshclements518/gh-cc-test/issues/2380/events","html_url":"https://github.com/joshclements518/gh-cc-test/issues/2380","id":6175701899,"node_id":"I_kwDOUn8XWc8AAAABcBm7iw","number":2380,"title":"[Epic 2380] character counter scheduling feature in the mobile app","user":{"login":"joshclements518","id":73498019,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjczNDk4MDE5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/73498019?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/joshclements518","html_url":"https://github.com/joshclements518","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/joshclements518/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/joshclements518/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/joshclements518/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/joshclements518/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/joshclements518/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/joshclements518/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/joshclements518/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/joshclements518/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/joshclements518/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":12337220995,"node_id":"LA_kwDOUn8XWc8AAAAC31sNgw","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/joshclements518/gh-cc-test/labels/human:needed","name":"human:needed","color":"d93f0b","default":false,"description":null,"archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":12696700237,"node_id":"LA_kwDOUn8XWc8AAAAC9MhFTQ","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/joshclements518/gh-cc-test/labels/stage:plan:done","name":"stage:plan:done","color":"ededed","default":false,"description":null,"archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"closed","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":827,"created_at":"2026-11-03T03:28:20Z","updated_at":"2026-11-10T18:17:27Z","closed_at":"2026-11-03T04:43:58Z","assignee":null,"author_association":"OWNER","active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":14,"completed":14,"percent_completed":100},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"<!-- stage-approvals -->\n| Stage | At | Approver | Session | Phrase |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| intent | 2026-11-03T03:28:30.543Z | harness-seed-plan | seed-plan-epic | seeded intent accept |\n| requirements | 2026-11-03T03:28:30.543Z | harness-seed-plan | seed-plan-epic | seeded requirements accept |\n| design | 2026-11-03T03:28:30.543Z | harness-seed-plan | seed-plan-epic | seeded design accept |\n| plan | 2026-11-03T04:42:30.000Z | Josh Clements · b626a055… | [join](https://aicp-cp-prod.blackriver-a051e806.westus2.azurecontainerapps.io/sessions/sdk-fe7dd4d6-cab?k=aeba1319aa79473c8121b1a12676e60e) | policyVersion:1.0.0 |\n<!-- /stage-approvals -->\n\n<!-- aicp-feature-request\n{\n  \"idempotencyKey\": \"seed-plan-epic-2380-2026-11-03T03:28:30.543Z\",\n  \"text\": \"character counter scheduling feature in the mobile app\",\n  \"senderDisplayName\": \"Josh Clements\",\n  \"receivedAt\": \"2026-11-03T02:04:46.8367621Z\",\n  \"channelId\": \"19:NFdNe0N-Z6Hqqxra7ub1l6a2l6jwYl3URF9X5hOu9xM1@thread.tacv2\",\n  \"source\": \"teams\",\n  \"uiRepo\": \"joshclements518/gh-cc-test-ui\",\n  \"apiRepo\": \"joshclements518/gh-cc-test-api\"\n}\n-->\n\n## Feature request\n\ncharacter counter scheduling feature in the mobile app\n\nUI repo: joshclements518/gh-cc-test-ui\nAPI repo: joshclements518/gh-cc-test-api\n\n_Landed via `teams` · key `seed-plan-epic-2380-2026-11-03T03:28:30.543Z` · 2026-11-03T02:04:46.8367621Z_\n\n---\n\n---\n\n---\n\n<!-- PRD:START -->\n# PRD — Character Encounter Scheduling\n\nEnable cruisers to browse and book character encounter meet-and-greets via the cruise ship mobile app.\n\n## Problem\n\n- Cruisers want to meet entertainment characters (Disney, Marvel, etc.) during their cruise but have no easy way to discover available encounters or reserve a spot\n- Character meet-and-greets fill up quickly; families miss out without advance booking\n- No visibility into which characters are available when and where on the ship\n\n## In Scope / Out Scope\n\n| In scope | Out of scope |\n|----------|--------------|\n| Browse available character encounters with sorting options | Push notifications or reminders for bookings |\n| View character details (name, photo, time, location, duration, spots available) | Check-in functionality at encounter time |\n| Book one encounter per cabin (whole cabin attends) | Waitlist or queue management |\n| View current booking in dedicated \"My Booking\" section | Multiple bookings per cabin |\n| Cancel booking anytime before encounter starts | Integration with photo packages or merchandise |\n| Rebook different encounter after cancellation | Individual per-person bookings within a cabin |\n| Handle ship-side cancellations with in-app notification | |\n| Sort encounters by time, character, or availability | |\n\n## Who / Systems\n\n**Personas:**\n- Cruiser (any guest aboard the ship; books on behalf of their cabin/family group)\n\n**Systems:**\n- Mobile app UI (`joshclements518/gh-cc-test-ui`)\n- Mobile app API (`joshclements518/gh-cc-test-api`)\n- Existing ship operations system (data source for character encounter schedules)\n\n## Flow\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[Cruiser opens character encounters] --> B[Browse and sort encounter list]\n    B --> C{Cabin has existing booking?}\n    C -->|Yes| D[View in 'My Booking' section]\n    C -->|No| E[Select encounter from list]\n    D --> F{Want to cancel?}\n    F -->|Yes| G[Cancel booking]\n    F -->|No| H[Keep booking]\n    G --> E\n    E --> I{Spots available?}\n    I -->|Yes| J{Cabin already has booking?}\n    I -->|No| B\n    J -->|Yes| K[Show error message]\n    J -->|No| L[Book encounter]\n    K --> B\n    L --> M[Confirmation screen with details]\n    M --> N[Booking stored in 'My Booking']\n    \n    O[Ship cancels encounter] --> P[Clear booking]\n    P --> Q[Show in-app notification]\n```\n\n## User Stories\n\n1. As a cruiser, I want to browse available character encounters sorted by time, character, or availability, so that I can find the best option for my family.\n\n2. As a cruiser, I want to view detailed information about each encounter (character name, photo, time, location, duration, spots available), so that I can make an informed booking decision.\n\n3. As a cruiser, I want to book one character encounter for my entire cabin, so that my whole family can attend together.\n\n4. As a cruiser, I want to see a confirmation screen immediately after booking with all the details, so that I know my booking was successful.\n\n5. As a cruiser, I want to view my current booking in a dedicated \"My Booking\" section, so that I can easily find my encounter details later.\n\n6. As a cruiser, I want to cancel my booking anytime before the encounter starts, so that I have flexibility if plans change.\n\n7. As a cruiser, I want to book a different encounter after cancelling, so that I can still participate in a character meet-and-greet.\n\n8. As a cruiser, I want to see a clear error message if I try to book when my cabin already has a booking, so that I understand the one-booking limit.\n\n9. As a cruiser, I want to be notified in the app if the ship cancels my booked encounter, so that I know I need to make a new booking.\n\n## Decisions Captured\n\n- **Data source**: Character encounter schedules come from the existing ship operations system (integration required)\n- **Booking unit**: One booking per cabin/family group (not per individual person)\n- **Attendees**: When a cabin books, everyone in that cabin can attend the encounter together\n- **Capacity measurement**: Encounter capacity is measured by number of cabins, regardless of cabin size\n- **Booking window**: Cruisers can book anytime during the cruise, not restricted to same-day or advance windows\n- **Cancellation policy**: Cabins can cancel anytime before the encounter starts, no time restrictions\n- **Post-booking UX**: Show confirmation screen with booking details immediately after successful booking\n- **Booking access**: Dedicated \"My Booking\" section in the app to view current booking\n- **Duplicate booking handling**: Show error message if cabin attempts to book when they already have a booking\n- **List organization**: Encounter list supports multiple sort options: by time, by character, by availability\n- **Ship cancellation handling**: If ship cancels an encounter, clear the booking and show in-app notification to the cruiser\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- [ ] Cruisers can browse a list of available character encounters in the mobile app\n- [ ] Each encounter displays character name, photo, time, location on ship, duration, and available spots (cabin count)\n- [ ] Encounter list supports sorting by time, character, and availability\n- [ ] Cruisers can book one encounter per cabin when spots are available\n- [ ] Booking allows everyone in the cabin to attend together\n- [ ] System enforces one-booking-per-cabin limit\n- [ ] Attempting to book when cabin has existing booking shows clear error message\n- [ ] After successful booking, cruisers see confirmation screen with all encounter details\n- [ ] Cruisers can view their current booking in dedicated \"My Booking\" section\n- [ ] Cruisers can cancel their booking anytime before the encounter starts\n- [ ] After cancellation, cabin can book a different encounter\n- [ ] If ship cancels an encounter, booking is cleared and cruiser receives in-app notification\n- [ ] UI works on mobile app (iOS and Android)\n- [ ] Booking and cancellation updates are reflected in real-time availability counts\n- [ ] Integration with existing ship operations system retrieves encounter schedule data\n\n## Open Ambiguities\n\n> **Note:** Integration details with existing ship operations system\n> \n> While the data source is confirmed as the existing ship operations system, the specific integration mechanism (API endpoints, data refresh frequency, authentication) will be determined during the architecture stage.\n\n---\n\n```f07-requirements\n{\n  \"id\": \"REQ-2380\",\n  \"version\": 1,\n  \"intentId\": \"INT-2380\",\n  \"intentVersion\": 1,\n  \"scope\": \"Enable cruisers to browse character encounter schedules, book one encounter per cabin (whole cabin attends), view booking details, cancel and rebook, with sorting options and ship cancellation handling via mobile app\",\n  \"rules\": [\n    \"One booking per cabin/family group maximum\",\n    \"Entire cabin can attend the booked encounter together\",\n    \"Capacity measured by number of cabins, not individual attendees\",\n    \"Cancellation allowed anytime before encounter starts\",\n    \"Booking allowed anytime during cruise\",\n    \"Duplicate booking attempts show error message\",\n    \"Ship cancellations clear booking and trigger in-app notification\"\n  ],\n  \"journeys\": [\n    \"Browse character encounters sorted by time, character, or availability\",\n    \"Select encounter and view details (character, time, location, duration, spots available)\",\n    \"Book encounter for cabin (all cabin members attend)\",\n    \"View confirmation screen with booking details\",\n    \"Access 'My Booking' section to view current booking\",\n    \"Cancel booking and rebook different encounter\",\n    \"Receive in-app notification if ship cancels encounter\"\n  ],\n  \"criteria\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"AC-1\",\n      \"text\": \"Encounter list displays all available encounters with character name, photo, time, location, duration, and cabin spots available\",\n      \"measurable\": true\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"AC-2\",\n      \"text\": \"List supports sorting by time, character, and availability\",\n      \"measurable\": true\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"AC-3\",\n      \"text\": \"Booking succeeds when cabin has no existing booking and spots are available\",\n      \"measurable\": true\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"AC-4\",\n      \"text\": \"Booking attempt when cabin has existing booking shows error message\",\n      \"measurable\": true\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"AC-5\",\n      \"text\": \"Confirmation screen displays after successful booking\",\n      \"measurable\": true\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"AC-6\",\n      \"text\": \"My Booking section shows current encounter details\",\n      \"measurable\": true\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"AC-7\",\n      \"text\": \"Cancellation succeeds anytime before encounter start time\",\n      \"measurable\": true\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"AC-8\",\n      \"text\": \"After cancellation, cabin can book a different encounter\",\n      \"measurable\": true\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"AC-9\",\n      \"text\": \"Ship-side cancellation clears booking and shows in-app notification\",\n      \"measurable\": true\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"AC-10\",\n      \"text\": \"Availability updates in real-time after bookings and cancellations\",\n      \"measurable\": true\n    }\n  ],\n  \"testReferences\": [],\n  \"assumptions\": [],\n  \"status\": \"accepted\",\n  \"acceptedVersion\": 1,\n  \"acceptedBy\": \"Josh Clements\"\n}\n```\n<!-- PRD:END -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ARCHITECTURE-DECISIONS:START -->\n# ADR-1: Event-Driven Architecture for Character Encounters\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThe character encounter scheduling feature requires:\n\n1. **Real-time availability updates** across all mobile clients when bookings/cancellations occur\n2. **Ship-side cancellation notifications** with < 1 second latency to affected cabins\n3. **High booking contention** during peak hours (families rushing to book popular characters)\n4. **Integration with ship operations system** that controls encounter schedules\n5. **Audit trail** for compliance and troubleshooting booking disputes\n6. **Scalability** to support 1000+ concurrent users per ship across multiple ships in the fleet\n\nThree architectural patterns were evaluated:\n- **REST + Polling**: Simple but 30-60s availability lag, high mobile battery drain\n- **REST + WebSocket**: Good real-time updates, moderate complexity\n- **Event-Driven**: Best real-time, scalability, and auditability, but highest complexity\n\n## Decision\n\nWe will implement an **event-driven architecture** using an event bus (Kafka, AWS SNS/SQS, or Azure Event Grid) as the central integration point.\n\n### Core Components\n\n1. **Event Bus**: Central message broker for all domain events\n2. **Mobile API**: REST endpoints + WebSocket service for real-time push\n3. **Sync Service**: Event consumer that maintains write model (PostgreSQL) and read model (Redis)\n4. **Push Service**: Event consumer that pushes updates to WebSocket clients\n5. **Notification Service**: Event consumer that sends push notifications for ship cancellations\n6. **Event Store**: Append-only log of all events for audit and replay\n\n### Event Types\n\n**Ship Operations:**\n- `encounter.created`: New character encounter scheduled\n- `encounter.updated`: Time, location, or capacity changed\n- `encounter.cancelled`: Ship-side cancellation\n\n**Bookings:**\n- `booking.created`: Cabin booked an encounter\n- `booking.cancelled`: Cabin cancelled their booking\n- `booking.cancelled.ship`: Ship cancelled encounter, booking cleared\n\n### Data Flow\n\n1. Ship operations system publishes events → Event bus\n2. Mobile API publishes booking events → Event bus\n3. Sync service consumes events → Updates DB and Redis read model\n4. Push service consumes events → Sends WebSocket messages to subscribed clients\n5. Notification service consumes events → Sends push notifications for cancellations\n\n## Consequences\n\n### Positive\n\n- **True real-time updates** (< 1s latency) for availability and cancellations\n- **Horizontal scalability**: Event consumers can scale independently\n- **Decoupled services**: Mobile API, sync, push, and notification services deploy independently\n- **Audit trail**: Event store provides complete history for compliance and replay\n- **Resilience**: Event bus provides durability; failed consumers can catch up via replay\n- **Future extensibility**: Analytics, reporting, and ML services can subscribe to events without API changes\n\n### Negative\n\n- **Complexity**: Requires event bus infrastructure, multiple services, distributed tracing\n- **Eventual consistency**: Read model (Redis) may lag write model (PostgreSQL) by milliseconds\n- **Longer implementation**: 5-6 weeks vs 2-3 weeks for REST + polling\n- **Operational overhead**: Must monitor event bus, consumer lag, and event replay\n- **Debugging difficulty**: Distributed transactions require correlation IDs and centralized logging\n\n### Mitigations\n\n- **Complexity**: Use managed event bus (AWS EventBridge, Azure Event Grid) to reduce operational burden\n- **Eventual consistency**: Acceptable for this domain (sub-second lag is not user-perceptible)\n- **Implementation time**: Phased rollout (MVP with REST + polling, migrate to events in v1.1)\n- **Debugging**: Implement OpenTelemetry for distributed tracing from day one\n\n## Alternatives Considered\n\n### REST API + Client Polling\n\n**Rejected** because:\n- 30-60s availability lag creates race conditions (users book sold-out encounters)\n- High mobile battery drain from constant polling\n- High API load (all clients poll continuously)\n- Poor user experience for ship cancellations (5-15 minute notification delay)\n\n### REST API + WebSocket Push\n\n**Rejected** because:\n- Does not provide audit trail for compliance\n- Harder to scale horizontally (WebSocket server state management)\n- Tighter coupling between mobile API and ship operations system\n- Future analytics/reporting require querying production DB (no event log)\n\nHowever, **this was a close second choice**. If team lacks event-driven experience or timeline is constrained, this is the recommended fallback.\n\n## Supersedes\n\nNone (initial architecture decision)\n\n## Related ADRs\n\n- [ADR-2: CQRS Pattern for Availability Read Model](./ADR-2-cqrs-read-model.md)\n- [ADR-3: WebSocket Push for Real-Time Updates](./ADR-3-websocket-push.md)\n- [ADR-5: Ship Operations Integration via Event Bus](./ADR-5-ship-ops-integration.md)\n\n---\n\n```f07-design\n{\n  \"id\": \"DES-REQ-2380\",\n  \"version\": 1,\n  \"requirementsId\": \"REQ-2380\",\n  \"requirementsAcceptedVersion\": 1,\n  \"covers\": {\n    \"interfaces\": \"{\\n  \\\"mobile_to_api\\\": {\\n    \\\"protocol\\\": \\\"HTTPS REST + WebSocket Secure (WSS)\\\",\\n    \\\"authentication\\\": \\\"JWT Bearer token\\\",\\n    \\\"endpoints\\\": [\\n      \\\"GET /encounters?sort=time|character|availability&date=YYYY-MM-DD\\\",\\n      \\\"POST /bookings {cabinId, encounterId}\\\",\\n      \\\"GET /bookings/cabin/:cabinId\\\",\\n      \\\"DELETE /bookings/:bookingId\\\",\\n      \\\"WebSocket wss://api.example.com/ws with subscribe/unsubscribe actions\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"data_format\\\": \\\"JSON\\\",\\n    \\\"rate_limits\\\": \\\"100 req/min per cabin, WebSocket: 1 connection per device\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"api_to_event_bus\\\": {\\n    \\\"protocol\\\": \\\"Event bus native protocol (Kafka, AMQP, or managed service SDK)\\\",\\n    \\\"events_published\\\": [\\n      \\\"booking.created\\\",\\n      \\\"booking.cancelled\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"payload_format\\\": \\\"JSON with schema version\\\",\\n    \\\"ordering\\\": \\\"Events for same encounterId maintain order (partition key = encounterId)\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"event_bus_to_consumers\\\": {\\n    \\\"consumers\\\": [\\n      \\\"Sync Service (writes DB/Redis)\\\",\\n      \\\"Push Service (WebSocket fanout)\\\",\\n      \\\"Notification Service (push notifications)\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"consumption_pattern\\\": \\\"At-least-once delivery with idempotency keys\\\",\\n    \\\"consumer_groups\\\": \\\"Each service has dedicated consumer group for parallel processing\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"ship_ops_to_event_bus\\\": {\\n    \\\"protocol\\\": \\\"Event bus publish or REST webhook adapter\\\",\\n    \\\"events_published\\\": [\\n      \\\"encounter.created\\\",\\n      \\\"encounter.updated\\\",\\n      \\\"encounter.cancelled\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"fallback\\\": \\\"If ship ops cannot publish events, build polling adapter service\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"api_to_data\\\": {\\n    \\\"write_db\\\": \\\"PostgreSQL (source of truth for bookings and encounters)\\\",\\n    \\\"read_cache\\\": \\\"Redis (CQRS read model for availability counts)\\\",\\n    \\\"event_log\\\": \\\"Event bus native persistence or dedicated event store (e.g., EventStore DB)\\\"\\n  }\\n}\",\n    \"dependencyImpacts\": \"{\\n  \\\"new_infrastructure\\\": [\\n    \\\"Event bus (Kafka cluster, AWS SNS/SQS, or Azure Event Grid)\\\",\\n    \\\"Redis cluster for read model (high availability, persistence enabled)\\\",\\n    \\\"PostgreSQL database for write model\\\",\\n    \\\"WebSocket service infrastructure (load balancer with sticky sessions or session affinity)\\\",\\n    \\\"Push notification gateway integration (Firebase Cloud Messaging, Apple Push Notification Service)\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"ship_operations_system\\\": {\\n    \\\"capability_required\\\": \\\"Publish events to event bus OR expose REST API for polling adapter\\\",\\n    \\\"data_required\\\": \\\"Character encounter schedules (character, time, location, capacity, status)\\\",\\n    \\\"authentication\\\": \\\"Mutual TLS or API key for event publishing\\\",\\n    \\\"change_impact\\\": \\\"If ship ops must modify their system to publish events, requires coordination with ship ops team\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"mobile_apps\\\": {\\n    \\\"sdk_additions\\\": [\\n      \\\"WebSocket client library (e.g., Socket.IO, native WebSocket)\\\",\\n      \\\"JWT token refresh logic\\\",\\n      \\\"Push notification registration and handling\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"state_management\\\": \\\"Local cache of encounter list and current booking for offline resilience\\\"\\n  }\\n}\",\n    \"testability\": \"{\\n  \\\"unit_tests\\\": {\\n    \\\"api\\\": \\\"Mock event bus publish calls, test booking validation logic\\\",\\n    \\\"sync_service\\\": \\\"Mock event consumption, verify DB/Redis updates\\\",\\n    \\\"push_service\\\": \\\"Mock WebSocket connections, verify message fanout\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"integration_tests\\\": {\\n    \\\"api_to_db\\\": \\\"Test booking creation flow with real PostgreSQL (testcontainers)\\\",\\n    \\\"event_flow\\\": \\\"Publish event to test event bus, verify consumers process correctly\\\",\\n    \\\"websocket\\\": \\\"Connect test client, subscribe to channel, verify push messages received\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"contract_tests\\\": {\\n    \\\"event_schemas\\\": \\\"Pact or JSON Schema validation for all event types\\\",\\n    \\\"rest_api\\\": \\\"OpenAPI spec with contract tests (Pact, Dredd)\\\",\\n    \\\"ship_ops_events\\\": \\\"Validate ship ops event payloads match schema\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"e2e_tests\\\": {\\n    \\\"booking_flow\\\": \\\"Mobile app books encounter \\\\u2192 verify DB updated, other clients receive push\\\",\\n    \\\"ship_cancellation\\\": \\\"Simulate ship ops cancellation event \\\\u2192 verify affected cabins notified\\\",\\n    \\\"race_condition\\\": \\\"Two clients book last spot simultaneously \\\\u2192 verify one succeeds, one fails\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"chaos_tests\\\": {\\n    \\\"event_bus_downtime\\\": \\\"Event bus unavailable \\\\u2192 API should queue events and retry\\\",\\n    \\\"consumer_lag\\\": \\\"Sync service behind by 10s \\\\u2192 verify read model eventually consistent\\\",\\n    \\\"websocket_disconnect\\\": \\\"Client disconnects \\\\u2192 verify reconnect and state reconciliation\\\"\\n  }\\n}\",\n    \"security\": \"{\\n  \\\"authentication\\\": {\\n    \\\"mobile_to_api\\\": \\\"JWT tokens issued by ship's identity provider, validated on every request\\\",\\n    \\\"token_expiry\\\": \\\"15-minute access tokens, 7-day refresh tokens\\\",\\n    \\\"cabin_authorization\\\": \\\"Token claims include cabinId, API verifies user can only manage their cabin's booking\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"data_protection\\\": {\\n    \\\"in_transit\\\": \\\"TLS 1.3 for all HTTPS and WSS connections, mTLS for ship ops integration\\\",\\n    \\\"at_rest\\\": \\\"Encrypted PostgreSQL volumes, Redis persistence encryption enabled\\\",\\n    \\\"pii_handling\\\": \\\"Cabin IDs and guest IDs are pseudonymous; no PII in event payloads\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"input_validation\\\": {\\n    \\\"api\\\": \\\"Validate all inputs against schema (cabinId format, encounterId exists, timestamp in future)\\\",\\n    \\\"event_consumers\\\": \\\"Validate event payloads against JSON schema before processing\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"rate_limiting\\\": {\\n    \\\"api\\\": \\\"100 requests/minute per cabin to prevent booking abuse\\\",\\n    \\\"websocket\\\": \\\"1 connection per device token, max 5 subscriptions per connection\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"audit\\\": {\\n    \\\"event_log\\\": \\\"Immutable append-only log of all bookings and cancellations\\\",\\n    \\\"retention\\\": \\\"7 years per maritime compliance requirements\\\",\\n    \\\"access_control\\\": \\\"Event log read-only except for append, admin access requires MFA\\\"\\n  }\\n}\",\n    \"operation\": \"{\\n  \\\"deployment\\\": {\\n    \\\"strategy\\\": \\\"Blue-green deployment for API and consumers (zero downtime)\\\",\\n    \\\"rollback\\\": \\\"Event consumers can replay events from offset in case of bad deployment\\\",\\n    \\\"database_migrations\\\": \\\"Use Flyway/Liquibase for versioned schema migrations\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"monitoring\\\": {\\n    \\\"metrics\\\": [\\n      \\\"Event bus consumer lag (alert if > 10 seconds)\\\",\\n      \\\"Booking API latency (p99 < 500ms)\\\",\\n      \\\"WebSocket connection count and message delivery rate\\\",\\n      \\\"Redis cache hit rate (target > 99%)\\\",\\n      \\\"PostgreSQL query performance\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"alerting\\\": [\\n      \\\"Consumer lag > 30 seconds \\\\u2192 page on-call\\\",\\n      \\\"Redis cache miss rate > 5% \\\\u2192 investigate\\\",\\n      \\\"API error rate > 1% \\\\u2192 alert\\\",\\n      \\\"Event bus unavailable \\\\u2192 immediate page\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"logging\\\": \\\"Structured JSON logs with correlation IDs (trace entire request flow across services)\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"disaster_recovery\\\": {\\n    \\\"event_log_backup\\\": \\\"Daily snapshots to object storage (S3, Azure Blob)\\\",\\n    \\\"database_backup\\\": \\\"PostgreSQL continuous archiving (PITR) with 30-day retention\\\",\\n    \\\"redis_backup\\\": \\\"Daily RDB snapshots, AOF persistence enabled\\\",\\n    \\\"recovery_time\\\": \\\"RTO: 1 hour, RPO: 15 minutes (event replay from last checkpoint)\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"scaling\\\": {\\n    \\\"api\\\": \\\"Horizontal auto-scaling based on CPU (target 70%) and request rate\\\",\\n    \\\"event_consumers\\\": \\\"Add consumer instances to consumer group (Kafka partitions enable parallelism)\\\",\\n    \\\"redis\\\": \\\"Redis Cluster mode with sharding by encounterId\\\",\\n    \\\"websocket\\\": \\\"Sticky sessions via load balancer (session affinity by device token hash)\\\"\\n  }\\n}\",\n    \"ui\": \"{\\n  \\\"screens\\\": [\\n    \\\"Encounter List: Browse encounters with sort/filter, shows availability in real-time\\\",\\n    \\\"Encounter Detail: Character photo, time, location, duration, book button (disabled if cabin has booking)\\\",\\n    \\\"Booking Confirmation: Shows booked encounter details immediately after booking\\\",\\n    \\\"My Booking: Dedicated section showing current booking with cancel button\\\",\\n    \\\"Cancellation Alert: In-app alert when ship cancels encounter (triggered by WebSocket push)\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"real_time_updates\\\": {\\n    \\\"availability_changes\\\": \\\"Encounter list updates spot counts as other cabins book/cancel (via WebSocket)\\\",\\n    \\\"ship_cancellation\\\": \\\"My Booking section clears and shows alert when ship cancels\\\",\\n    \\\"offline_mode\\\": \\\"Show last-known state, queue booking requests for retry on reconnect\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"error_handling\\\": {\\n    \\\"duplicate_booking\\\": \\\"Show error: 'Your cabin already has a booking. Cancel it first to book a different encounter.'\\\",\\n    \\\"sold_out\\\": \\\"Show 'Sold out' badge, disable book button, offer 'Notify me if available' (future feature)\\\",\\n    \\\"network_error\\\": \\\"Show retry button, cache failed request for background retry\\\"\\n  }\\n}\",\n    \"api\": \"{\\n  \\\"rest_endpoints\\\": {\\n    \\\"list_encounters\\\": {\\n      \\\"method\\\": \\\"GET\\\",\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"/encounters\\\",\\n      \\\"query_params\\\": [\\n        \\\"sort (time|character|availability)\\\",\\n        \\\"date (YYYY-MM-DD)\\\",\\n        \\\"limit\\\",\\n        \\\"offset\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"response\\\": \\\"Array of encounter objects with availableSpots from Redis cache\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"create_booking\\\": {\\n      \\\"method\\\": \\\"POST\\\",\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"/bookings\\\",\\n      \\\"body\\\": {\\n        \\\"cabinId\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n        \\\"encounterId\\\": \\\"string\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"response\\\": \\\"201 Created with booking object, 409 Conflict if duplicate or sold out\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"get_cabin_booking\\\": {\\n      \\\"method\\\": \\\"GET\\\",\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"/bookings/cabin/:cabinId\\\",\\n      \\\"response\\\": \\\"200 OK with booking object, 404 Not Found if no active booking\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"cancel_booking\\\": {\\n      \\\"method\\\": \\\"DELETE\\\",\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"/bookings/:bookingId\\\",\\n      \\\"response\\\": \\\"204 No Content on success, 403 Forbidden if encounter already started\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"websocket_protocol\\\": {\\n    \\\"connection\\\": \\\"wss://api.example.com/ws?token=<jwt>\\\",\\n    \\\"subscribe\\\": {\\n      \\\"action\\\": \\\"subscribe\\\",\\n      \\\"channel\\\": \\\"encounter.availability.<encounterId>\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"unsubscribe\\\": {\\n      \\\"action\\\": \\\"unsubscribe\\\",\\n      \\\"channel\\\": \\\"encounter.availability.<encounterId>\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"pushed_messages\\\": [\\n      {\\n        \\\"type\\\": \\\"encounter.availability\\\",\\n        \\\"encounterId\\\": \\\"...\\\",\\n        \\\"availableSpots\\\": 5\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \\\"type\\\": \\\"cabin.booking.cancelled\\\",\\n        \\\"bookingId\\\": \\\"...\\\",\\n        \\\"reason\\\": \\\"ship_cancelled\\\"\\n      }\\n    ]\\n  }\\n}\"\n  },\n  \"alternatives\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"event_bus_technology\",\n      \"summary\": \"Selected based on existing ship infrastructure: Kafka if available, otherwise AWS EventBridge or Azure Event Grid\",\n      \"accepted\": true\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ship_ops_integration\",\n      \"summary\": \"If ship ops cannot publish events directly, build adapter service that polls ship ops REST API and publishes events to event bus\",\n      \"accepted\": true\n    }\n  ],\n  \"exceptions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"eventual_consistency\",\n      \"text\": \"Redis read model may lag PostgreSQL write model by < 1 second. Acceptable for this domain (booking conflicts handled via optimistic locking).\",\n      \"version\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"websocket_fallback\",\n      \"text\": \"If WebSocket connection fails, mobile app falls back to polling GET /encounters every 30 seconds until reconnected.\",\n      \"version\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"status\": \"accepted\",\n  \"acceptedVersion\": 1,\n  \"brownfield\": \"workspace\",\n  \"brownfieldSummary\": \"Workspace brownfield — UI/API sample apps + ship-ops integration described in ADRs; F06 Neo4j graph not required for this design package.\",\n  \"rejectionReason\": null\n}\n```\n\n# ADR-2: CQRS Pattern for Availability Read Model\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThe encounter list query (`GET /encounters`) must:\n\n1. Return availability counts for all encounters (50-100 encounters per day)\n2. Respond in < 200ms to avoid slow mobile app loading\n3. Handle high read traffic during peak hours (1000+ concurrent users)\n4. Stay consistent with booking writes (no stale availability data)\n\nTwo approaches were considered:\n\n**Direct database query:**\n- `SELECT encounter_id, capacity_cabins - COUNT(active_bookings) AS available FROM encounters JOIN bookings`\n- Simple, but slow (JOIN + COUNT on every request)\n- High PostgreSQL load during peak hours\n\n**CQRS with Redis cache (read model):**\n- Maintain `encounter:{id}:available` counters in Redis\n- Update counters atomically on booking/cancellation events\n- Read queries hit Redis (sub-millisecond latency)\n- Eventual consistency with PostgreSQL write model\n\n## Decision\n\nWe will implement a **CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation)** pattern with Redis as the read model for encounter availability.\n\n### Write Model (Command Side)\n\n**PostgreSQL** is the source of truth:\n- `encounters` table: Character, time, location, capacity\n- `bookings` table: Cabin bookings\n\nAll writes (bookings, cancellations, encounter updates) go through PostgreSQL.\n\n### Read Model (Query Side)\n\n**Redis** cache stores availability counts:\n- Key: `encounter:{encounterId}:available`\n- Value: Integer (remaining cabin spots)\n- TTL: None (persists until encounter starts or is cancelled)\n\n**Sync service** maintains read model:\n- Consumes `booking.created` → `DECR encounter:{id}:available`\n- Consumes `booking.cancelled` → `INCR encounter:{id}:available`\n- Consumes `encounter.created` → `SET encounter:{id}:available {capacity_cabins}`\n- Consumes `encounter.cancelled` → `DEL encounter:{id}:available`\n\n### Consistency Guarantees\n\n**Atomic decrement during booking:**\n```\nBEGIN TRANSACTION\n  available = DECR encounter:{id}:available\n  IF available < 0:\n    INCR encounter:{id}:available  // rollback\n    ABORT TRANSACTION\n  ELSE:\n    INSERT INTO bookings ...\n    COMMIT\nEND TRANSACTION\n```\n\n**Nightly reconciliation:**\n- Cron job at 3 AM compares Redis counters with PostgreSQL counts\n- Fixes any drift (e.g., Redis eviction, consumer lag)\n\n## Consequences\n\n### Positive\n\n- **Fast queries**: Redis reads are < 1ms (vs 50-100ms for PostgreSQL JOIN)\n- **Scalable**: Redis handles millions of reads/sec\n- **Reduced DB load**: PostgreSQL only handles writes (10-100x fewer than reads)\n- **Atomic operations**: `DECR`/`INCR` are atomic, prevent race conditions\n\n### Negative\n\n- **Eventual consistency**: Redis may lag PostgreSQL by milliseconds (event processing time)\n- **Cache invalidation complexity**: Must update Redis on every booking/cancellation event\n- **Persistence required**: Redis must persist to disk (RDB + AOF) to survive restarts\n- **Reconciliation needed**: Periodic job to fix Redis drift\n\n### Mitigations\n\n- **Eventual consistency**: Acceptable for this domain (sub-second lag is not user-perceptible)\n- **Cache invalidation**: Sync service handles all updates (single responsibility)\n- **Persistence**: Configure Redis with `appendonly yes` and `save 900 1`\n- **Reconciliation**: Automated nightly job with alerting on drift > 5%\n\n## Alternatives Considered\n\n### Direct PostgreSQL Queries\n\n**Rejected** because:\n- Slow query performance (50-100ms with JOIN + COUNT)\n- High database load during peak hours\n- Difficult to scale reads horizontally (read replicas have replication lag)\n\n### Materialized View in PostgreSQL\n\n**Rejected** because:\n- Materialized view refresh is slow (locks table)\n- Still requires PostgreSQL for reads (doesn't reduce DB load)\n- Refresh frequency vs consistency tradeoff\n\n### Write-Through Cache (Redis + PostgreSQL)\n\n**Rejected** because:\n- Booking writes must update both Redis and PostgreSQL in transaction\n- Distributed transaction complexity (2PC or Saga pattern)\n- Harder to roll back on failure\n\n## Supersedes\n\nNone (initial data access pattern decision)\n\n## Related ADRs\n\n- [ADR-1: Event-Driven Architecture](./ADR-1-event-driven-architecture.md) — Events trigger read model updates\n- [ADR-4: One Booking Per Cabin Constraint](./ADR-4-one-booking-per-cabin.md) — Uses Redis for duplicate check\n\n# ADR-3: WebSocket Push for Real-Time Updates\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThe mobile app must show real-time availability updates when other cabins book or cancel encounters. Three delivery mechanisms were considered:\n\n1. **Client polling**: App polls `GET /encounters` every 30-60 seconds\n   - Simple, but 30-60s lag and high battery drain\n\n2. **WebSocket push**: Persistent bidirectional connection for instant updates\n   - Near-instant delivery (< 1s), moderate complexity\n\n3. **Server-Sent Events (SSE)**: Unidirectional server-to-client stream\n   - Good for read-only updates, but limited browser/library support on mobile\n\n## Decision\n\nWe will use **WebSocket (WSS)** for real-time push notifications to mobile clients.\n\n### Architecture\n\n**Push Service:**\n- Subscribes to event bus for `booking.created`, `booking.cancelled`, `encounter.cancelled` events\n- Maintains registry of active WebSocket connections (cabin ID → connection)\n- Publishes messages to subscribed clients via WebSocket\n\n**Mobile App:**\n- Establishes WebSocket connection on app launch: `wss://api.example.com/ws?token=<jwt>`\n- Subscribes to channels:\n  - `encounter.availability.{encounterId}` — Availability changes for viewed encounters\n  - `cabin.booking.{cabinId}` — Booking/cancellation for user's cabin\n- Receives JSON messages and updates UI in real-time\n\n**Connection Management:**\n- **Load balancer**: Sticky sessions (route by device token hash)\n- **Authentication**: JWT token in connection query params, validated on connect\n- **Heartbeat**: Client sends `ping` every 30s, server responds with `pong`\n- **Reconnect**: Exponential backoff (1s, 2s, 4s, 8s, max 30s)\n- **Fallback**: If WebSocket fails, fall back to polling `GET /encounters` every 30s\n\n### Message Types\n\n**From client (subscribe):**\n```json\n{\n  \"action\": \"subscribe\",\n  \"channel\": \"encounter.availability.enc_789\"\n}\n```\n\n**From server (availability update):**\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"encounter.availability\",\n  \"encounterId\": \"enc_789\",\n  \"availableSpots\": 4,\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-03T14:35:22Z\"\n}\n```\n\n**From server (ship cancellation):**\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"cabin.booking.cancelled\",\n  \"bookingId\": \"bk_123\",\n  \"encounterId\": \"enc_789\",\n  \"reason\": \"ship_cancelled\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-03T14:30:00Z\"\n}\n```\n\n## Consequences\n\n### Positive\n\n- **Near-instant updates** (< 1s latency from event to UI)\n- **Lower mobile battery usage** vs polling (persistent connection, no repeated HTTP handshakes)\n- **Bidirectional**: Can add client-to-server events in future (e.g., typing indicators)\n- **Wide library support**: WebSocket libraries available for iOS (Starscream, SocketRocket) and Android (OkHttp)\n\n### Negative\n\n- **Connection management complexity**: Must handle disconnects, reconnects, network switches\n- **Load balancer configuration**: Requires sticky sessions or Redis-backed session store\n- **Infrastructure cost**: Persistent connections consume server memory (1-10KB per connection)\n- **Firewall issues**: Some corporate/ship networks block WebSocket (must fall back to polling)\n\n### Mitigations\n\n- **Connection management**: Use battle-tested WebSocket library (Socket.IO, Starscream)\n- **Load balancer**: Configure sticky sessions via AWS ALB, Azure App Gateway, or NGINX\n- **Infrastructure cost**: Acceptable for expected scale (5000 connections × 5KB = 25MB per server)\n- **Firewall issues**: Detect WebSocket failure, auto-fallback to polling\n\n## Alternatives Considered\n\n### Client Polling\n\n**Rejected** because:\n- 30-60s lag is poor UX (users may book sold-out encounters)\n- High battery drain from repeated HTTP requests\n- High API load (all clients poll continuously)\n\n### Server-Sent Events (SSE)\n\n**Rejected** because:\n- Unidirectional (server → client only, no subscription management)\n- Poor mobile library support (requires EventSource polyfill)\n- Not widely used in mobile apps (less battle-tested than WebSocket)\n\nHowever, **SSE is a valid fallback** if WebSocket proves problematic in production. Can switch without changing event architecture.\n\n### Push Notifications Only\n\n**Rejected** because:\n- Push notifications are for background alerts, not real-time UI updates\n- Delivery is best-effort (may be delayed or dropped)\n- Cannot update UI while user is actively viewing encounter list\n\n**Push notifications are used in addition to WebSocket** for ship cancellations when app is backgrounded.\n\n## Supersedes\n\nNone (initial real-time delivery mechanism)\n\n## Related ADRs\n\n- [ADR-1: Event-Driven Architecture](./ADR-1-event-driven-architecture.md) — Push service consumes events\n- [ADR-5: Ship Operations Integration](./ADR-5-ship-ops-integration.md) — Ship cancellations pushed via WebSocket\n\n# ADR-4: One Booking Per Cabin Constraint\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nPer PRD requirements:\n- **Booking unit**: One booking per cabin/family group (not per individual person)\n- **Attendees**: When a cabin books, everyone in that cabin can attend together\n- **Constraint**: Cabin can have at most one active booking at any time\n- **Rebooking**: After cancellation, cabin can book a different encounter\n\nThis constraint must be enforced reliably, even under high concurrency (two users from same cabin attempting to book simultaneously).\n\n## Decision\n\nWe will enforce the one-booking-per-cabin constraint using a **unique database constraint** plus **check-before-write** in the API.\n\n### Database Schema\n\n**Unique partial index in PostgreSQL:**\n```sql\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_one_booking_per_cabin\nON bookings (cabin_id)\nWHERE status = 'active';\n```\n\nThis ensures:\n- Only one `active` booking per `cabin_id` (database-level guarantee)\n- Cancelled bookings (`status = 'cancelled_by_cabin'` or `'cancelled_by_ship'`) do not violate constraint\n- Race conditions are prevented at the database level (transaction serialization)\n\n### API Enforcement\n\n**Before creating booking:**\n```sql\nSELECT booking_id FROM bookings\nWHERE cabin_id = :cabinId AND status = 'active';\n```\n\nIf result is non-empty:\n- Return `409 Conflict` with error: `\"Cabin {cabinId} already has an active booking. Cancel it before booking a different encounter.\"`\n\nIf result is empty:\n- Proceed to insert booking (database will reject if race condition occurred)\n\n### Race Condition Handling\n\n**Scenario**: Two users from cabin 1234 attempt to book simultaneously.\n\n1. User A calls `POST /bookings` → API checks DB → No active booking\n2. User B calls `POST /bookings` → API checks DB → No active booking (A's booking not committed yet)\n3. User A's transaction inserts booking, commits → Success\n4. User B's transaction attempts insert → **Database constraint violation** → Transaction aborted\n5. API catches `UniqueViolationError`, returns `409 Conflict` to User B\n\n### Cancellation Flow\n\n**When cabin cancels their booking:**\n```sql\nUPDATE bookings\nSET status = 'cancelled_by_cabin', cancelled_at = NOW()\nWHERE booking_id = :bookingId AND cabin_id = :cabinId;\n```\n\nUnique constraint no longer applies (status != 'active'), cabin can book a different encounter.\n\n## Consequences\n\n### Positive\n\n- **Database-enforced consistency**: Constraint cannot be violated even if API logic fails\n- **Race condition safety**: Database handles concurrency via transaction isolation\n- **Clear error messages**: API can detect duplicate booking and return helpful error\n- **Rebooking support**: Cancelled bookings do not violate constraint\n\n### Negative\n\n- **Database dependency**: Constraint logic is in database schema (harder to test in isolation)\n- **Error handling**: Must catch database constraint violation and translate to API error\n- **Migration risk**: Adding unique index to existing table with duplicate data will fail (requires cleanup first)\n\n### Mitigations\n\n- **Database dependency**: Acceptable tradeoff (database is best place for data integrity)\n- **Error handling**: Standardize constraint violation → `409 Conflict` mapping in API error handler\n- **Migration risk**: N/A for new feature (no existing data), for future: cleanup script before index creation\n\n## Alternatives Considered\n\n### Application-Level Lock (e.g., Redis Distributed Lock)\n\n**Rejected** because:\n- Requires Redis lock acquisition before booking (adds latency and complexity)\n- Lock must be held across database transaction (long-lived lock reduces concurrency)\n- Lock expiration and deadlock handling complexity\n- Database constraint is simpler and more reliable\n\n### Optimistic Locking with Version Number\n\n**Rejected** because:\n- Requires version column on `cabin` or `bookings` table\n- Client must retry on conflict (poor UX, multiple round-trips)\n- Does not prevent duplicate bookings, only detects them\n\n### No Constraint, Allow Duplicate Bookings\n\n**Rejected** because:\n- Violates PRD requirement (\"one booking per cabin\")\n- Would require periodic cleanup job to detect and fix duplicates\n- Unclear user experience if cabin has multiple bookings\n\n## Supersedes\n\nNone (initial constraint enforcement decision)\n\n## Related ADRs\n\n- [ADR-1: Event-Driven Architecture](./ADR-1-event-driven-architecture.md) — Bookings are written to DB and published as events\n- [ADR-2: CQRS Read Model](./ADR-2-cqrs-read-model.md) — Read model does not enforce constraint (write model does)\n\n# ADR-5: Ship Operations Integration via Event Bus\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nCharacter encounter schedules are managed by an external **ship operations system**. The mobile API must:\n\n1. Receive encounter schedules (character, time, location, capacity)\n2. Detect new encounters, schedule updates, and ship-side cancellations\n3. Update mobile app availability in near real-time (< 1 minute for schedule changes)\n\n**Integration constraint**: Per PRD, \"specific integration mechanism (API endpoints, data refresh frequency, authentication) will be determined during architecture stage.\"\n\n**Unknown**: Does ship operations system have a REST API? Can it push events? Or must we poll/batch sync?\n\nThree integration patterns were considered:\n\n1. **Event-driven**: Ship ops publishes events to event bus (ideal)\n2. **Webhook**: Ship ops sends HTTP POST to mobile API on changes (good)\n3. **Polling adapter**: Mobile API polls ship ops REST API every 5-15 minutes (fallback)\n\n## Decision\n\nWe will integrate via the **event bus**, with an **adapter service** as a fallback if the ship operations system cannot publish events directly.\n\n### Primary Pattern: Ship Ops Publishes Events\n\n**If ship operations system supports event publishing:**\n\nShip ops publishes to event bus:\n- `encounter.created` — New character encounter scheduled\n- `encounter.updated` — Time, location, or capacity changed\n- `encounter.cancelled` — Encounter cancelled by ship operations\n\n**Event payload example:**\n```json\n{\n  \"eventType\": \"encounter.created\",\n  \"encounterId\": \"enc_789\",\n  \"shipOpsId\": \"SHIP-MG-20261103-001\",\n  \"cruiseId\": \"cruise_123\",\n  \"character\": {\n    \"name\": \"Mickey Mouse\",\n    \"photoUrl\": \"https://cdn.example.com/characters/mickey.jpg\"\n  },\n  \"schedule\": {\n    \"startTime\": \"2026-11-03T15:00:00Z\",\n    \"durationMinutes\": 30,\n    \"locationCode\": \"DECK5-ATRIUM\",\n    \"locationName\": \"Deck 5 Atrium\"\n  },\n  \"capacity\": {\n    \"cabins\": 20\n  },\n  \"publishedAt\": \"2026-11-03T10:00:00Z\"\n}\n```\n\n**Sync service** consumes events:\n1. Validate event schema (JSON Schema validation)\n2. Write to `encounters` table (insert or update)\n3. Initialize Redis cache: `SET encounter:{id}:available {capacity_cabins}`\n4. For `encounter.cancelled`, clear bookings and notify affected cabins\n\n### Fallback Pattern: Polling Adapter\n\n**If ship operations system cannot publish events:**\n\nBuild **adapter service** that:\n1. Polls ship ops REST API every 5-15 minutes for encounter schedules\n2. Compares with local DB state to detect changes (diff algorithm)\n3. Publishes events to event bus on behalf of ship ops (`encounter.created`, `encounter.updated`, `encounter.cancelled`)\n4. Rest of system unchanged (sync service consumes events as usual)\n\n**Adapter service responsibilities:**\n- Authentication with ship ops API (API key, OAuth, or mutual TLS)\n- Retry logic for failed API calls\n- Change detection (compare fetched encounters with DB state)\n- Event publishing (identical payload format as primary pattern)\n\n### Authentication\n\n**Ship ops → Event bus (primary pattern):**\n- Mutual TLS or API key for event bus authentication\n- Event signature (HMAC) to verify authenticity\n\n**Adapter → Ship ops API (fallback pattern):**\n- API key, OAuth 2.0, or mutual TLS per ship ops system requirements\n- Store credentials in secret manager (AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault)\n\n## Consequences\n\n### Positive\n\n- **Flexible integration**: Works whether ship ops can push events or not\n- **Consistent architecture**: Adapter service hides ship ops integration complexity\n- **Future-proof**: When ship ops adds event support, remove adapter without changing rest of system\n- **Event-driven benefits**: Real-time updates, audit trail, decoupling (even with polling adapter)\n\n### Negative\n\n- **Adapter service complexity**: Requires change detection, retry logic, state tracking\n- **Polling lag**: If using adapter, 5-15 minute delay for ship cancellations (acceptable per PRD)\n- **Coordination required**: Must work with ship ops team to define integration contract\n\n### Mitigations\n\n- **Adapter complexity**: Use battle-tested change detection library (e.g., diff-json)\n- **Polling lag**: For urgent cancellations, ship ops can call webhook endpoint directly (bypass adapter)\n- **Coordination**: Define event schema first, ship ops implements when ready (adapter buys time)\n\n## Alternatives Considered\n\n### Direct API Calls from Mobile API\n\n**Rejected** because:\n- Tight coupling between mobile API and ship ops system\n- No audit trail or event log for schedule changes\n- Harder to scale (every mobile API instance calls ship ops)\n- No buffer for ship ops downtime (mobile API fails if ship ops is down)\n\n### Database Replication from Ship Ops DB\n\n**Rejected** because:\n- Requires direct database access (security risk)\n- Schema coupling (ship ops DB schema changes break mobile API)\n- No change notifications (must poll or use triggers)\n\n### Webhook Receiver in Mobile API\n\n**Rejected as primary pattern** because:\n- Requires ship ops to implement webhook sending logic\n- Mobile API becomes tightly coupled to ship ops (must be online to receive)\n- No retry mechanism if mobile API is down during webhook delivery\n\nHowever, **webhook is a valid alternative to polling adapter**. If ship ops prefers webhooks over event bus, implement webhook receiver that publishes events to event bus.\n\n## Supersedes\n\nNone (initial ship ops integration decision)\n\n## Related ADRs\n\n- [ADR-1: Event-Driven Architecture](./ADR-1-event-driven-architecture.md) — Event bus is integration point\n- [ADR-2: CQRS Read Model](./ADR-2-cqrs-read-model.md) — Sync service updates read model from ship ops events\n\n## Open Questions\n\nTo be resolved during implementation:\n\n1. **Ship ops system capabilities**: Does it have a REST API? Can it publish events to event bus? Does it support webhooks?\n2. **Authentication mechanism**: API key, OAuth, mutual TLS, or other?\n3. **Data refresh frequency**: Real-time, hourly, daily?\n4. **Encounter schedule format**: JSON, XML, CSV, or other?\n5. **Historical data**: Must we backfill encounters from past cruises, or only future?\n\n**Recommendation**: Start with polling adapter (fallback pattern) to unblock mobile API development. Migrate to event-driven pattern when ship ops integration details are finalized.\n<!-- ARCHITECTURE-DECISIONS:END -->\n\n<!-- SYSTEM-OVERVIEW-ADDITIONS:START -->\n# System Context — Character Encounter Scheduling\n\nThis diagram shows the system landscape for the character encounter scheduling feature, including the new components and integrations.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    Cruiser[\"Cruiser<br/>Mobile App User\"]\n    \n    subgraph Mobile[\"Mobile Applications (NEW)\"]\n        iOS[\"iOS App<br/>gh-cc-test-ui\"]\n        Android[\"Android App<br/>gh-cc-test-ui\"]\n    end\n    \n    subgraph API[\"Mobile API Layer (NEW)\"]\n        REST[\"REST API<br/>gh-cc-test-api\"]\n        WS[WebSocket Service]\n        Auth[Auth Service]\n    end\n    \n    subgraph Events[\"Event Infrastructure (NEW)\"]\n        EB[\"Event Bus<br/>Kafka/SNS/EventGrid\"]\n        Sync[\"Sync Service<br/>Event Consumer\"]\n        Push[\"Push Service<br/>Event Consumer\"]\n        Notify[\"Notification Service<br/>Event Consumer\"]\n    end\n    \n    subgraph Data[\"Data Layer (NEW)\"]\n        DB[\"(Booking DB<br/>PostgreSQL)\"]\n        ReadModel[\"(Read Model<br/>Redis)\"]\n        EventLog[\"(Event Store)\"]\n    end\n    \n    subgraph External[\"External Systems\"]\n        ShipOps[\"Ship Operations<br/>System\"]\n        PushGateway[\"Push Notification<br/>Gateway\"]\n    end\n    \n    Cruiser -->|Browse/Book| iOS\n    Cruiser -->|Browse/Book| Android\n    \n    iOS -->|HTTPS REST| REST\n    Android -->|HTTPS REST| REST\n    iOS -->|WSS| WS\n    Android -->|WSS| WS\n    \n    REST -->|Validate token| Auth\n    WS -->|Validate token| Auth\n    \n    REST -->|Read| ReadModel\n    REST -->|Write| DB\n    REST -->|Publish event| EB\n    \n    WS -->|Subscribe| EB\n    \n    ShipOps -->|Publish events| EB\n    \n    EB -->|Subscribe| Sync\n    EB -->|Subscribe| Push\n    EB -->|Subscribe| Notify\n    \n    Sync -->|Write| DB\n    Sync -->|Update| ReadModel\n    Sync -->|Append| EventLog\n    \n    Push -->|Send update| WS\n    Notify -->|Send| PushGateway\n    PushGateway -->|APNS/FCM| iOS\n    PushGateway -->|APNS/FCM| Android\n    \n    classDef added fill:#e1f5e1,stroke:#4caf50,stroke-width:2px\n    classDef external fill:#f5f5f5,stroke:#999,stroke-width:1px\n    \n    class Mobile,API,Events,Data added\n    class ShipOps,PushGateway external\n```\n\n## Components\n\n### New Components (This Epic)\n\n**Mobile Applications:**\n- iOS/Android apps with character encounter browsing and booking UI\n- WebSocket client for real-time availability updates\n- Local state management for offline resilience\n\n**Mobile API Layer:**\n- REST API: CRUD operations for bookings, encounter queries\n- WebSocket Service: Real-time push for availability and cancellations\n- Auth Service: Token validation (JWT)\n\n**Event Infrastructure:**\n- Event Bus: Central message broker (Kafka, AWS SNS/SQS, or Azure Event Grid)\n- Sync Service: Consumes events, updates DB and read model\n- Push Service: Consumes booking/cancellation events, pushes to WebSocket clients\n- Notification Service: Consumes events, sends push notifications for ship cancellations\n\n**Data Layer:**\n- Booking DB (PostgreSQL): Source of truth for bookings and encounters\n- Read Model (Redis): Materialized view of encounter availability (CQRS)\n- Event Store: Append-only log of all events for audit and replay\n\n### External Systems\n\n**Ship Operations System:**\n- Publishes encounter events (created, updated, cancelled)\n- Integration via event bus or adapter service\n\n**Push Notification Gateway:**\n- Existing infrastructure (Firebase Cloud Messaging, Apple Push Notification Service)\n\n## Key Interactions\n\n1. **Browse Encounters:** Mobile app → REST API → Read Model (Redis)\n2. **Book Encounter:** Mobile app → REST API → Booking DB + Publish event\n3. **Real-time Updates:** Event Bus → Push Service → WebSocket → Mobile app\n4. **Ship Cancellation:** Ship Ops → Event Bus → Sync + Notify → Mobile app (WebSocket + Push notification)\n<!-- SYSTEM-OVERVIEW-ADDITIONS:END -->\n\n<!-- DATA-MODEL-ADDITIONS:START -->\n# Data Model — Character Encounter Scheduling\n\nThis entity-relationship diagram shows the core domain model for character encounter bookings.\n\n```mermaid\nerDiagram\n    CRUISE {\n        string cruise_id PK\n        string ship_name\n        date departure_date\n        date return_date\n        string status\n    }\n    \n    CABIN {\n        string cabin_id PK\n        string cabin_number\n        string cruise_id FK\n        int occupancy_count\n        string primary_guest_id\n    }\n    \n    ENCOUNTER {\n        string encounter_id PK\n        string ship_ops_id \"External system ID\"\n        string cruise_id FK\n        string character_name\n        string character_photo_url\n        timestamp start_time\n        int duration_minutes\n        string location_code\n        string location_name\n        int capacity_cabins \"Max cabin count\"\n        string status \"scheduled, cancelled, completed\"\n        timestamp created_at_added\n        timestamp updated_at\n    }\n    \n    BOOKING {\n        string booking_id PK\n        string cabin_id FK\n        string encounter_id FK\n        string status \"active, cancelled_by_cabin, cancelled_by_ship\"\n        timestamp booked_at_added\n        timestamp cancelled_at\n        string cancellation_reason\n    }\n    \n    EVENT_LOG {\n        string event_id_added PK\n        string event_type_added \"encounter.created, booking.created, etc\"\n        string aggregate_id_added \"encounter_id or booking_id\"\n        jsonb payload_added\n        timestamp occurred_at_added\n        string source_service_added\n    }\n    \n    AVAILABILITY_CACHE {\n        string cache_key_added PK \"encounter:{encounter_id}:available\"\n        int available_cabins_added\n        timestamp last_updated_added\n    }\n    \n    CRUISE ||--o{ CABIN : \"has\"\n    CRUISE ||--o{ ENCOUNTER : \"schedules\"\n    CABIN ||--o| BOOKING : \"books one\"\n    ENCOUNTER ||--o{ BOOKING : \"has\"\n    ENCOUNTER ||--|| AVAILABILITY_CACHE : \"cached in\"\n    BOOKING ||--o{ EVENT_LOG : \"generates\"\n    ENCOUNTER ||--o{ EVENT_LOG : \"generates\"\n\n%% New entities and fields for this epic marked with _added suffix or comment\n```\n\n## Entity Descriptions\n\n### CRUISE\nRepresents a cruise voyage. Pre-existing entity (assumed to exist in ship systems).\n\n### CABIN\nRepresents a cabin/stateroom on the ship. Links to cruise. Pre-existing entity.\n\n- `occupancy_count`: Number of guests in the cabin (not used for capacity calculation per PRD)\n- `primary_guest_id`: Guest who can manage bookings via app\n\n### ENCOUNTER (NEW)\nRepresents a scheduled character meet-and-greet.\n\n- `ship_ops_id`: Foreign key to ship operations system (for sync/correlation)\n- `character_name`: Display name (e.g., \"Mickey Mouse\", \"Spider-Man\")\n- `character_photo_url`: URL to character image asset\n- `location_code`: Ship deck/room code (e.g., \"DECK5-ATRIUM\")\n- `capacity_cabins`: **Capacity measured by cabin count, not individual attendees** (per PRD)\n- `status`: Lifecycle state (scheduled → cancelled/completed)\n\n### BOOKING (NEW)\nRepresents a cabin's reservation for an encounter. **One booking per cabin maximum** (unique constraint on `cabin_id` where `status = 'active'`).\n\n- `status`:\n  - `active`: Current valid booking\n  - `cancelled_by_cabin`: Cruiser cancelled\n  - `cancelled_by_ship`: Ship operations cancelled the encounter\n- `cancellation_reason`: Free text or code (for ship cancellations)\n\n### EVENT_LOG (NEW)\nAppend-only event store for audit trail and event replay (event sourcing pattern).\n\n- `event_type`: Domain event name (e.g., `encounter.created`, `booking.created`, `booking.cancelled.ship`)\n- `aggregate_id`: ID of the affected entity (encounter or booking)\n- `payload`: Full event data as JSON\n- `source_service`: Which service published the event (for debugging)\n\n### AVAILABILITY_CACHE (NEW)\nRedis cache for fast availability lookups (CQRS read model).\n\n- `cache_key`: Format `encounter:{encounter_id}:available`\n- `available_cabins`: Real-time count of remaining spots\n- Atomically decremented on booking, incremented on cancellation\n- Rebuilt from DB on cache miss or nightly reconciliation\n\n## Key Constraints\n\n1. **One booking per cabin**: Unique index on `(cabin_id)` in `BOOKING` table where `status = 'active'`\n2. **Capacity enforcement**: `available_cabins` in cache never goes negative (atomic operations)\n3. **Booking window**: No time-based restrictions (per PRD, book anytime during cruise)\n4. **Cancellation allowed**: Until encounter `start_time` is reached\n\n## Data Flow\n\n1. **Encounter creation**: Ship ops publishes event → Sync service writes to `ENCOUNTER` table + Redis cache\n2. **Booking creation**: API writes to `BOOKING` table, decrements Redis cache, publishes event\n3. **Availability query**: API reads from `AVAILABILITY_CACHE` (sub-millisecond latency)\n4. **Ship cancellation**: Sync service updates `ENCOUNTER.status`, marks related `BOOKING` records as `cancelled_by_ship`\n<!-- DATA-MODEL-ADDITIONS:END -->\n\n<!-- SEQUENCE-ADDITIONS:START -->\n### docs/architecture/sequences/booking-flow.md\n\n# Sequence: Booking an Encounter\n\nThis sequence diagram shows the interaction flow when a cruiser books a character encounter, including real-time availability updates pushed to other users.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    actor User_A as User A<br/>(Cabin 1234)\n    actor User_B as User B<br/>(Cabin 5678)\n    participant Mobile_A as Mobile App A\n    participant Mobile_B as Mobile App B\n    participant REST as REST API\n    participant Auth as Auth Service\n    participant DB as Booking DB\n    participant Cache as Redis Cache\n    participant EventBus as Event Bus\n    participant Push as Push Service\n    participant WS as WebSocket Service\n    \n    Note over Mobile_A,Mobile_B: Both users viewing same encounter with 5 spots available\n    \n    User_A->>Mobile_A: Tap \"Book\" on encounter\n    Mobile_A->>REST: POST /bookings<br/>{cabinId: 1234, encounterId: enc_789}\n    REST->>Auth: Validate JWT token\n    Auth-->>REST: Token valid, cabin 1234\n    \n    rect rgb(240, 248, 255)\n        Note over REST,Cache: Booking transaction\n        REST->>DB: BEGIN TRANSACTION\n        REST->>DB: SELECT * FROM bookings<br/>WHERE cabin_id='1234' AND status='active'\n        DB-->>REST: No existing booking\n        REST->>Cache: DECR encounter:enc_789:available\n        Cache-->>REST: 4 (decremented from 5)\n        \n        alt Cache returned >= 0 (spot available)\n            REST->>DB: INSERT INTO bookings<br/>(booking_id, cabin_id, encounter_id, status, booked_at)\n            REST->>DB: COMMIT\n            DB-->>REST: Booking created: bk_abc123\n            \n            REST->>EventBus: Publish event:<br/>{\"type\": \"booking.created\", \"bookingId\": \"bk_abc123\",<br/>\"cabinId\": \"1234\", \"encounterId\": \"enc_789\"}\n            REST-->>Mobile_A: 201 Created<br/>{bookingId: \"bk_abc123\", confirmation: {...}}\n            Mobile_A->>User_A: Show confirmation screen\n            \n        else Cache returned < 0 (no spots)\n            REST->>Cache: INCR encounter:enc_789:available\n            REST->>DB: ROLLBACK\n            REST-->>Mobile_A: 409 Conflict<br/>{\"error\": \"No spots available\"}\n            Mobile_A->>User_A: Show \"Sold out\" message\n        end\n    end\n    \n    rect rgb(240, 255, 240)\n        Note over EventBus,Mobile_B: Real-time push to other users\n        EventBus->>Push: Event: booking.created<br/>encounterId: enc_789\n        Push->>WS: Broadcast to subscribers:<br/>channel \"encounter.availability.enc_789\"\n        WS->>Mobile_B: WebSocket message:<br/>{\"type\": \"encounter.availability\",<br/>\"encounterId\": \"enc_789\", \"availableSpots\": 4}\n        Mobile_B->>User_B: Update encounter list:<br/>5 spots → 4 spots (instant)\n    end\n    \n    Note over Mobile_A,Mobile_B: User A sees confirmation, User B sees updated availability in < 1 second\n```\n\n## Key Steps\n\n1. **Authorization**: API validates JWT token to confirm user has access to cabin 1234\n2. **Duplicate booking check**: Query for existing active booking by cabin\n3. **Optimistic locking**: Atomic Redis decrement reserves the spot\n4. **Database transaction**: Write booking record only if cache decrement succeeded\n5. **Event publishing**: Notify other services and clients of the booking\n6. **Real-time push**: WebSocket service broadcasts availability change to subscribed clients\n\n## Error Scenarios\n\n### User already has a booking\n- API returns `409 Conflict` with message: \"Cabin 1234 already has an active booking\"\n- User must cancel existing booking before rebooking\n\n### Race condition (two users book last spot simultaneously)\n- Both clients call API at same time\n- First to acquire Redis lock succeeds; second gets `availableSpots: -1` → transaction rolled back\n- Second user receives `409 Conflict: No spots available`\n\n### Ship operations cancels encounter during booking\n- API checks `encounter.status` before writing booking\n- If status is `cancelled`, return `410 Gone: Encounter cancelled by ship`\n\n### docs/architecture/sequences/ship-cancellation-flow.md\n\n# Sequence: Ship Cancels Encounter\n\nThis sequence diagram shows what happens when the ship operations system cancels a character encounter, including how affected cabins are notified.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    actor ShipOps as Ship Operations<br/>Staff\n    participant ShipSystem as Ship Ops<br/>System\n    participant EventBus as Event Bus\n    participant Sync as Sync Service\n    participant DB as Booking DB\n    participant Cache as Redis Cache\n    participant Notify as Notification<br/>Service\n    participant Push as Push Service\n    participant PushGateway as Push Notification<br/>Gateway\n    participant WS as WebSocket<br/>Service\n    participant Mobile as Mobile App<br/>(Affected cabins)\n    actor User as Cruiser\n    \n    ShipOps->>ShipSystem: Cancel encounter enc_789<br/>(Character unavailable)\n    \n    rect rgb(255, 245, 245)\n        Note over ShipSystem,EventBus: Ship operations publishes event\n        ShipSystem->>EventBus: Publish event:<br/>{\"type\": \"encounter.cancelled\",<br/>\"encounterId\": \"enc_789\",<br/>\"reason\": \"Character unavailable\",<br/>\"cancelledAt\": \"2026-11-03T14:30:00Z\"}\n    end\n    \n    rect rgb(240, 248, 255)\n        Note over EventBus,Cache: Sync service processes cancellation\n        EventBus->>Sync: Event: encounter.cancelled\n        Sync->>DB: BEGIN TRANSACTION\n        Sync->>DB: UPDATE encounters<br/>SET status='cancelled'<br/>WHERE encounter_id='enc_789'\n        Sync->>DB: SELECT booking_id, cabin_id<br/>FROM bookings<br/>WHERE encounter_id='enc_789' AND status='active'\n        DB-->>Sync: 12 affected bookings<br/>[{bk_1, cabin_101}, {bk_2, cabin_205}, ...]\n        \n        Sync->>DB: UPDATE bookings<br/>SET status='cancelled_by_ship',<br/>cancellation_reason='Character unavailable'<br/>WHERE encounter_id='enc_789' AND status='active'\n        Sync->>Cache: DEL encounter:enc_789:available\n        Sync->>DB: COMMIT\n        \n        Sync->>EventBus: Publish events (12x):<br/>{\"type\": \"booking.cancelled.ship\",<br/>\"bookingId\": \"bk_1\", \"cabinId\": \"cabin_101\",<br/>\"encounterId\": \"enc_789\", \"reason\": \"Character unavailable\"}\n    end\n    \n    par Notify affected cabins via push notification\n        rect rgb(255, 250, 240)\n            EventBus->>Notify: Event: booking.cancelled.ship (x12)\n            Notify->>DB: SELECT push_tokens<br/>FROM cabin_devices<br/>WHERE cabin_id IN (...)\n            DB-->>Notify: 18 device tokens<br/>(some cabins have multiple devices)\n            \n            loop For each device token\n                Notify->>PushGateway: Send push notification:<br/>\"Your Mickey Mouse meet-and-greet<br/>at 3pm has been cancelled\"\n                PushGateway->>Mobile: APNS/FCM push\n            end\n            Mobile->>User: Banner notification\n        end\n    and Push to connected WebSocket clients\n        rect rgb(240, 255, 240)\n            EventBus->>Push: Event: booking.cancelled.ship (x12)\n            Push->>WS: Publish to cabin channels:<br/>\"cabin.booking.cabin_101\", etc.\n            \n            alt Cabin is connected via WebSocket\n                WS->>Mobile: WebSocket message:<br/>{\"type\": \"booking.cancelled.ship\",<br/>\"encounterId\": \"enc_789\",<br/>\"reason\": \"Character unavailable\"}\n                Mobile->>User: In-app alert:<br/>\"Encounter cancelled by ship\"\n                Mobile->>Mobile: Clear \"My Booking\" section\n            else Cabin is offline\n                Note over WS,Mobile: Will see empty booking<br/>on next app open\n            end\n        end\n    end\n    \n    User->>Mobile: Open app to rebook\n    Mobile->>Mobile: \"My Booking\" shows empty\n    Note over Mobile,User: User can now browse and book a different encounter\n```\n\n## Key Steps\n\n1. **Event origin**: Ship operations staff marks encounter as cancelled in their system\n2. **Event publishing**: Ship ops system publishes `encounter.cancelled` event to event bus\n3. **Synchronous processing**: Sync service consumes event, updates DB in transaction\n4. **Cascade cancellation**: All active bookings for the encounter are marked `cancelled_by_ship`\n5. **Parallel notification**: Both push notifications and WebSocket messages sent to affected cabins\n6. **Graceful offline handling**: Offline users see empty \"My Booking\" when they reconnect\n\n## Notification Channels\n\n### Push Notification (via APNS/FCM)\n- **Pros**: Works when app is backgrounded or closed\n- **Delivery**: Best-effort (may be delayed or dropped)\n- **Content**: Short message with encounter details\n\n### WebSocket Message\n- **Pros**: Instant delivery for connected clients\n- **Delivery**: Guaranteed if client is connected\n- **Content**: Full event payload, app can update UI immediately\n\n### State reconciliation on reconnect\n- App fetches current booking state via `GET /bookings/cabin/:cabinId` on launch\n- If `My Booking` is empty but was previously set, show in-app alert\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n### Cabin has no registered devices\n- Push notification is skipped (no tokens)\n- User will see empty \"My Booking\" on next app open\n\n### Multiple users from same cabin are online\n- Each device receives push notification\n- Each WebSocket connection receives message\n- All see the same alert (idempotent)\n\n### User books different encounter immediately after cancellation\n- Cancellation clears the constraint\n- API allows new booking (one-per-cabin rule no longer violated)\n<!-- SEQUENCE-ADDITIONS:END -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- STORIES:START -->\nCreated 14 stories from docs/stories/epic-2380-stories.md:\n\n1. #2381 — [Epic 2380/Story 2381] Database Schema and Encounter Data Model (`stage:code:waiting` → `stage:code:ready`)\n2. #2382 — [Epic 2380/Story 2382] Redis Cache Setup for Availability Read Model (`stage:code:waiting`)\n3. #2383 — [Epic 2380/Story 2383] Encounter List API Endpoint (`stage:code:waiting`)\n4. #2384 — [Epic 2380/Story 2384] Encounter List Mobile UI (`stage:code:waiting`)\n5. #2385 — [Epic 2380/Story 2385] Book Encounter API with Constraint Enforcement (`stage:code:waiting`)\n6. #2386 — [Epic 2380/Story 2386] Booking UI and Confirmation Screen (`stage:code:waiting`)\n7. #2387 — [Epic 2380/Story 2387] My Booking Section UI (`stage:code:waiting`)\n8. #2388 — [Epic 2380/Story 2388] Cancel Booking API (`stage:code:waiting`)\n9. #2389 — [Epic 2380/Story 2389] Cancel Booking UI (`stage:code:waiting`)\n10. #2390 — [Epic 2380/Story 2390] Event Bus Infrastructure (`stage:code:waiting`)\n11. #2391 — [Epic 2380/Story 2391] WebSocket Push Infrastructure (`stage:code:waiting`)\n12. #2392 — [Epic 2380/Story 2392] Real-Time Availability Updates in UI (`stage:code:waiting`)\n13. #2393 — [Epic 2380/Story 2393] Ship Operations Event Integration (`stage:code:waiting`)\n14. #2394 — [Epic 2380/Story 2394] Ship Cancellation Handling and Notification (`stage:code:waiting`)\n<!-- STORIES:END -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- PLAN:START -->\n{\n  \"id\": \"PLAN-epic-2380\",\n  \"version\": 1,\n  \"intentId\": \"INT-epic-2380\",\n  \"tasks\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Database Schema and Encounter Data Model\",\n      \"acceptedInputVersions\": {\n        \"intent\": 1,\n        \"requirements\": 1,\n        \"design\": 1\n      },\n      \"branchScope\": \"epic/2380/t-1\",\n      \"role\": \"coder\",\n      \"budget\": \"45m\",\n      \"stopConditions\": [\n        \"tests green\",\n        \"budget elapsed\",\n        \"acceptance criteria met\"\n      ],\n      \"independentVerification\": \"QE / explore verifies story acceptance criteria\",\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"api change\"\n      ],\n      \"acceptanceEvidence\": [\n        \"story acceptance criteria verified\"\n      ],\n      \"tools\": [\n        \"git\"\n      ],\n      \"repairLimit\": \"2\",\n      \"escalationOwner\": \"delivery-lead\",\n      \"dependsOn\": [],\n      \"contractDependency\": {\n        \"id\": \"ctx-api-contract\",\n        \"version\": \"v1.0.0\"\n      },\n      \"id\": \"T-1\",\n      \"repo\": \"api\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Redis Cache Setup for Availability Read Model\",\n      \"acceptedInputVersions\": {\n        \"intent\": 1,\n        \"requirements\": 1,\n        \"design\": 1\