894981?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hiraditya","html_url":"https://github.com/hiraditya","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hiraditya/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hiraditya/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hiraditya/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hiraditya/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hiraditya/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hiraditya/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hiraditya/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hiraditya/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hiraditya/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[],"state":"closed","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":0,"created_at":"2026-11-10T08:53:20Z","updated_at":"2026-11-10T16:54:42Z","closed_at":"2026-11-10T16:54:39Z","assignee":null,"author_association":"COLLABORATOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"draft":false,"pull_request":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vx-lang/Vx/pulls/3152","html_url":"https://github.com/vx-lang/Vx/pull/3152","diff_url":"https://github.com/vx-lang/Vx/pull/3152.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/vx-lang/Vx/pull/3152.patch","merged_at":"2026-11-10T16:54:39Z"},"body":"On a Mac, a launch of an Apple GPU kernel cost about 64 us of CPU time in the runtime. The MoE model of #3094 makes about 600 launches for each token, so the CPU would set its speed. This PR cuts the cost of a launch to about 10 us.\n\n## Where the time went\n\nI added timers around each step of `vx_metal_dispatch` and ran the Qwen2.5-Coder-0.5B app for 32 tokens. This is the average of 7047 launches:\n\n| step | us per launch |\n|---|---:|\n| search the image for the `// vx.assert` line of the kernel | 35.5 |\n| search the image for the `// vx.kernel` line (`parametersOf`) | 21.2 |\n| find the `image=` field of the payload | 3.3 |\n| everything else | 3.8 |\n| total | 63.8 |\n\nThe image holds the Metal source of every kernel of the program. Most kernels have no assert, so the first search read the whole image at each launch.\n\n## The change\n\n- The runtime searches the image for a kernel once, and keeps its parameters and whether it has asserts.\n- A kernel with asserts got a new buffer of 4 bytes at each launch. Now a batch gives each such launch one word of a buffer of 1024 words, and it makes a new buffer when one is full.\n\n## Results\n\nOn the 0.5B model, a launch now takes 10.1 us in the runtime, with the launches at load time included. The tokens and logits are the same. Time per token, in runs that took turns:\n\n| | before | after |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| round 1 | 15.1 ms | 13.5 ms |\n| round 2 | 15.0 ms | 13.7 ms |\n| round 3 | 15.3 ms | 11.9 ms |\n\nThe token did not get 11 ms faster, because the GPU now sets its speed: it took 13 to 18 ms of GPU time for each token in a run with `VX_DISPATCH_VERBOSE=1`. The 0.5B model reads about 1 GB of weights for each token, which takes at least 8 ms at 120 GB/s.\n\nThe `image=` field still costs 3.3 us. The payload reader walks to the end of an entry before it compares the key, so that it never reads past the payload. That is a change for another PR.\n\n## Tests\n\n`a_failed_assert_after_many_apple_gpu_launches_names_its_assert.vx` runs 1211 launches of two kernels with asserts before one wait, and launch 1201 fails. So the failed launch has a word in the second buffer. The fixture checks that the runtime reports the assert of that launch. It runs with `MTL_DEBUG_LAYER=1`, the checks of the Metal API.\n\nI broke the change in three ways, one at a time, and the fixture failed each time:\n\n| break | what the fixture saw |\n|---|---|\n| every launch gets word 0 of its buffer | the assert of `add_below`, not `scale_below` |\n| a batch never makes a second buffer | Metal: `offset(4096) must be < [buffer length](4096)` |\n| no kernel has asserts | no failed assert, and the program printed `not printed` |\n\nWithout `MTL_DEBUG_LAYER=1`, the second break passed: the GPU wrote past the end of the buffer with no fault. The old runtime also passes the fixture.\n\nAll 21 fixtures that use `fleet/m4-uma.vx` passed with the new runtime, and the pre-commit hook passed.\n\nFixes #3132\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vx-lang/Vx/issues/3152/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vx-lang/Vx/issues/3152/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/goosepod/naviamp/issues/454","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/goosepod/naviamp","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/goosepod/naviamp/issues/454/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/goosepod/naviamp/issues/454/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/goosepod/naviamp/issues/454/events","html_url":"https://github.com/goosepod/naviamp/issues/454","id":6142353623,"node_id":"I_kwDOTQ8-QM8AAAABbhzg1w","number":454,"title":"Investigate intermittent iOS mTLS missing-identity rejection timeout","user":{"login":"ursasmar","id":540564,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU0MDU2NA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/540564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/ursasmar","html_url":"https://github.com/ursasmar","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ursasmar/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ursasmar/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ursasmar/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ursasmar/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ursasmar/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ursasmar/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ursasmar/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ursasmar/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ursasmar/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":11450503686,"node_id":"LA_kwDOTQ8-QM8AAAACqoDSBg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/goosepod/naviamp/labels/bug","name":"bug","color":"d73a4a","default":true,"description":"Something isn't working","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":12706826610,"node_id":"LA_kwDOTQ8-QM8AAAAC9WLJcg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/goosepod/naviamp/labels/priority:%20high","name":"priority: high","color":"D93F0B","default":false,"description":"Prioritize ahead of optional feature work","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/goosepod/naviamp/milestones/10","html_url":"https://github.com/goosepod/naviamp/milestone/10","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/goosepod/naviamp/milestones/10/labels","id":19150680,"node_id":"MI_kwDOTQ8-QM4BJDdY","number":10,"title":"v2.11.0","description":"v2.11.0 includes the accepted Aurral expansion and compatibility upgrade, contributor playback/TV fixes, Android startup compatibility, artwork settings, repeat/tooltip polish, translation updates, iOS test reliability and CI caching, and official signed Linux Flatpak distribution.\n\nRemaining release work: accept exact-head iOS fixture reliability verification (#454/#564), integrate platform evidence (#563/#565), and finish versioning, shared About changelog, release notes, complete verification, packaging and announcements (#566). The measured cache acceptance (#512) and tooltip/translation integration are complete.\n\nOwner decision 2026-11-10: unresolved Android raster visibility (#330), broader macOS acceptance (#342), Windows startup presentation (#493), and additional Linux desktop/recovery acceptance (#552) remain open, nonblocking follow-ups outside this milestone. Passing controls do not establish root causes or missing physical acceptance. Required CI assertions and the complete exact-source release matrix remain enforced.\n\nThe existing signed Flatpak contains v2.10.0. Publishing its v2.11.0 update requires the currently unavailable existing signing environment and is tracked separately in #569; release notes must state this availability difference.\n\nFurther visualizer/animation performance work, original unreproduced album Play-button Gapless #520, voice PRs #173/#174 and native acquisition cancel/retry remain outside scope. No release date is selected.\n","creator":{"login":"ursasmar","id":540564,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU0MDU2NA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/540564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/ursasmar","html_url":"https://github.com/ursasmar","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ursasmar/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ursasmar/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ursasmar/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ursasmar/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ursasmar/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ursasmar/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ursasmar/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ursasmar/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ursasmar/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"open_issues":5,"closed_issues":89,"state":"open","created_at":"2026-11-02T19:02:19Z","updated_at":"2026-11-10T18:05:08Z","due_on":null,"closed_at":null},"comments":5,"created_at":"2026-11-01T02:26:14Z","updated_at":"2026-11-10T16:46:45Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"Two runs on the Android-only Fragment change timed out in the unchanged iOS mTLS integration test during missing client identity rejection, while the paired release PR passed the same test and complete matrix on the same source tree c29d359eb23a5fb9bfa00e3274eb0d2da7ad80c1.\n\nEvidence: main Verify 41262370342 attempt 1, iOS job 124718103241; distribution fallback 41262524348 attempt 1, iOS job 124718591964. Both HTML reports say: mTLS timed out during: missing client identity rejection; state=Idle; progress position/duration null. These are negative-authentication timeouts, not an assertion that an invalid identity authenticated. Paired release Verify 41262502777 passed all nine checks on the same tree. Main iOS has a targeted retry in progress. Failed logs/reports are preserved locally under build/fragment-450-release/ios-main-failure and ios-packaging-failure.\n\nOnly Android dependency metadata changed in #451/#452, so no Apple production input was intentionally changed. Investigate simulator TLS-service readiness, request cancellation/timeouts and fixture behavior before attributing this to production code or changing test timing. Cold simulator startup is a hypothesis, not a confirmed cause.\n\nAcceptance: reproduce or characterize on fresh simulator boots; preserve all missing/untrusted/wrong-password/hostname negative checks plus positive and native playback/seek controls; make the test reliable without silently increasing thresholds or skipping authentication coverage. Keep portable test orchestration in common testkit, with native effects limited to actual Apple APIs. Track any demonstrated product failure separately. Do not count failed attempts as passing verification.\n\nRelated release controls #440 and Android dependency fix #450. No source fix is included in the Fragment PR.\n\n## PR #467 recurrence — November 3, 2026\n\nVerify [41682705435](https://github.com/goosepod/naviamp/actions/runs/41682705435), attempt 1, on PR #467 head `6765c9b6ac239742d761065fc317a992b01033cc` (accepted main merged into the contributor branch) failed the same negative-authentication phase in `IosMutualTlsPlaybackIntegrationTest.realMutualTlsAuthenticatesAndSeeksThroughNormalPlayback`.\n\nThe HTML report says: `mTLS timed out during: missing client identity rejection; state=Idle; progress=PlaybackProgress(positionSeconds=null, durationSeconds=null, decodedPositionSeconds=null, seekGeneration=0)`. Native iOS suite: 15 tests, one failure. The common iOS compilation/test stage and all other platform jobs passed. This matches the observed assertion tracked here; the cause and any relationship to #467 are not established. It does not assert that a missing identity authenticated.\n\nPR #467 remains unmerged in v2.11.0 because the full workflow is red. No retry is counted as a fix, no negative check was skipped, and no timeout was increased. Failed logs and the downloaded `ios-test-reports` artifact are preserved privately under `/private/tmp/review-467-current-ci-failed.log` and `/private/tmp/review-467-ios-failure`. The hosted run/artifact is the durable evidence.\n\n## Demonstrated main-loop harness correction — November 3, 2026\n\nPR #509 at `725c650820e9dbe14398aca7fd22f5a946206466` corrects a demonstrated test-harness defect: `runBlocking` parks the native main thread while a Default → Main callback waits. A deterministic native callback probe deadlocked under that pattern; a preserved process sample records `BlockingCoroutine.joinBlocking` / `Worker.park` on the main thread. This establishes callback starvation in the harness without claiming a particular private CFNetwork routine caused every historical timeout.\n\nCommon testkit now owns asynchronous test completion, failure propagation and cancellation; a thin iOS test adapter services Foundation's main run loop. Both test hosts require actual main-thread callback delivery before authentication. All five negative TLS cases and the existing 25-second rejection deadlines, positive controls and native playback/seek/range/identity audits remain intact. The final diff changes no production TLS or playback code.\n\nFive shared lifecycle/failure tests pass; Android/JVM/both iOS common compilation, Android test-host compilation and architecture checks pass. The two direct native run-loop/failure regressions pass in 0.096 seconds. Three independently generated fixture/device suites pass all 17 native methods each: 51 executions, zero failures/errors/skips. The exact Desktop-compatible revision also passed another fresh 17-method suite (zero failures/errors/skips), plus the complete Desktop shared mTLS integration in 16.85 seconds. The initial Linux compile failure exposed the missed Desktop host; restoring the portable no-op callback default corrected it. Every created fixture process and device was cleaned up. Evidence is recorded in `docs/verification/454-ios-mtls-rejection.md` and privately under `/private/tmp/naviamp-454-fixed-9f1aojit`.\n\nExact-source Verify [41702529392](https://github.com/goosepod/naviamp/actions/runs/41702529392) gates merge. Earlier diagnostic passes/retries are not counted as fixes; the original failures remain preserved. After this correction is accepted into main, refresh #467 from accepted main and rerun its required matrix before merging it.\n\n\n\n## November 4 recurrence: isolated before TLS\n\nReopened after PR #496's refreshed head `2adee0de1505824953b0ffd6877f137738b05996` failed in [Verify 41850406958](https://github.com/goosepod/naviamp/actions/runs/41850406958), iOS job 126644972076. This head contains accepted #509, #510 and #513. Its only spacing diff is shared Now Playing layout, its rendered test and verification documentation.\n\nThe added positive native request now isolates the failure at `fixture bootstrap`, before certificate import or authentication: `tlsStage=not started; nativeContext=; challenges=[]`. CFNetwork records a successful loopback TCP connection, a sent HTTP request and zero response bytes, followed by cancellation at the existing 10-second deadline. The fixture JVM still owns all four listening ports; its later failure-only thread dump has HTTP dispatchers waiting in KQueue and no active request handler. This establishes failure to receive the live control response, not a production TLS credential acceptance defect or proof of the underlying OS/JVM cause.\n\nThe disposable fixture started before roughly thirteen minutes of common compilation and remained alive across simulator boot. Moving its lifetime closer to native execution is a possible follow-up, not yet a demonstrated fix. Raw iOS reports and diagnostics are preserved in the hosted `ios-test-reports` artifact, and privately under `/private/tmp/496-ios-failure` and `/private/tmp/496-ios-failure.log`.\n\nAll prior passing evidence remains valid for those runs, including three hosted suites (51 executions) in 41740582425 and the clean #467 run 41746836760. It does not establish that this intermittent condition is resolved. No assertion or deadline has been weakened. One unchanged, isolated iOS retry may establish the independent spacing PR's checks; it is not counted as a fix here, and this issue remains open regardless of that retry's result.\n## November 6: owner-prioritized hosted diagnosis\n\nThe current #529 failures (Verify 42321711902 and 42339520367) and #530 failure (42341439802) are at fixture bootstrap, not negative authentication. All 17 native cases execute; only the mTLS case fails. CFNetwork connects to the fixture and sends a 175-byte plain HTTP GET, but records zero response bytes before the existing ten-second deadline. The initial main callback completes; no TLS challenge has started. All failed reports, service logs and JVM thread/socket snapshots are retained.\n\nSix hosted controls include passes after 12.3, 18.9 and 23.3 minutes of pre-native fixture idle, and failures after 16.2, 18.8 and 21.6 minutes. Age alone is not an established cause. The inspected passing/failing #529 jobs use the same macos-26-arm64 image 20261102.0473.1 and Temurin 21.0.13+9.\n\nA matched aged/fresh experiment is running at https://github.com/goosepod/naviamp/actions/runs/42343047551, exact source 39e8ff146. Two aged and two fresh fixtures each get a separate cold simulator and the same full native binary. It preserves failed cases, original deadlines and all authentication/native assertions, records loopback packet metadata and post-test host control health, and uses separately named diagnostic checks. The complete canonical Verify matrix is retained unchanged. Results are pending; no cause, fix, merge acceptance or resolution is claimed. Existing application production code is unchanged.\n## Corrected diagnostic experiment\n\nThe first paired job at 42343047551 compiled the native binary but executed zero native cases: its first CoreSimulator device-list RPC exceeded the cold-replay helper's 30-second setup limit. That is a diagnostic-harness failure, not an application result. The normal iOS job on the same source independently failed at bootstrap again. Raw setup and native-failure artifacts are retained.\n\nThe corrected experiment is https://github.com/goosepod/naviamp/actions/runs/42344966834, source 990794cbd. It provisions CoreSimulator explicitly before per-device work, uses the same complete iOS compilation/common-test graph as Verify, and compares persistent versus single-use Gradle daemons with host memory/swap/JVM snapshots. The existing iOS job's GRADLE_OPTS override omits the global org.gradle.daemon=false setting; this is a measured hypothesis, not a cause claim. Fixtures age naturally during normal compilation in this follow-up; no extra twenty-minute wait is added. Native bootstrap/authentication/playback deadlines and all assertions are unchanged, and the complete canonical matrix remains intact. Results are pending.\n\n## Comparison status, November 6 evening\n\nRun 42344966834 preserved 17/17 passing native cases in each first aged-fixture condition: 879 seconds old with single-use Gradle daemons, and 1152 seconds old with a persistent daemon. The ordinary iOS job also passed its native step. Both diagnostic jobs then failed while stopping their packet observer, before fresh-fixture comparisons. These are incomplete experiments, not evidence of resolution. The persistent Gradle JVM retained about 2313 MiB resident memory; that resource difference is real, but these results do not establish it as the bootstrap cause.\n\nThe corrected observer uses a verified isolated process group to reap both sudo monitor and tcpdump, captures native XML before observer teardown, and checks observer lifecycle before compilation without warming the fixture endpoint. All 21 selected Python iOS helper tests pass locally, including ownership refusal checks. The complete corrected comparison is running at https://github.com/goosepod/naviamp/actions/runs/42347030189, source 08dc265a7. Original native deadlines, negative/positive authentication assertions and required checks remain unchanged. The earlier reports and packet logs remain preserved; no application production code has changed.\n\n\n## Captured host-side response delay and corrective PR #531\n\nThe preserved single-use-daemon job in 42347030189 supplies three completed suites with the identical native binary: the aged fixture fails only bootstrap (16/17 pass), and two fresh fixtures pass all 17 cases each. The fourth aged suite was starting when the diagnostic run was cancelled and supplies no completed result; this remains a partial comparison.\n\nThe failed connection's packet timestamps establish the delay: GET sent at 22:51:40.076434 and acknowledged immediately by the host; client cancellation at 22:51:50.242033; first 79 server response bytes at 22:51:59.076583, 19.000149 seconds after the GET. No body arrives on that failed connection. The post-test host probe then passes in 0.401s. Fresh controls first respond in about 0.174s and 4.243s. Thus this reproduction is a late fixture/host response, before TLS, rather than evidence of a credential rejection or client discarding a timely response. A retained Gradle daemon is not required. The precise OS/JVM reason for the delay is still unproven.\n\nPR https://github.com/goosepod/naviamp/pull/531 now starts the ordinary fixture after common compilation and dedicated simulator boot; each cold replay also boots before creating its fixture. Native live-bootstrap verification, the ten-second deadline, negative/positive authentication and streaming/seek checks are unchanged. No application production code changes. Optional diagnostics are bounded and preserve failures. All 28 selected local helper tests pass. Corrective head 78c4cb7c0831f6932547948d34bfa7a592dc15fe is running the required matrix with verify:ios-native-cold: initial suite plus two independent cold fixture/device replays, 51 native executions. Keep this issue open until that exact-head acceptance passes. Earlier passing controls and incomplete experiments are not counted as the fix.\n\n## November 7: current corrective candidate\n\nHead `29d7b958435926a6370bd4ba41c9d22eed3df776` in [Verify 42355618596](https://github.com/goosepod/naviamp/actions/runs/42355618596) uses the final order: compile, prepare/start a fresh fixture, boot the simulator, execute native tests. Each independent cold replay follows the same order. The ordinary iOS job inherits the global single-use Gradle policy, releasing the measured approximately 2.3 GiB build daemon before simulator execution.\n\nThe preceding corrective run 42350790835 passed 34 native executions (standard suite and first replay), but its second replay reached the old 90-second fixture provisioning limit before native testing. Replay setup now has a separate bounded 180-second provisioning budget and preserves startup/probe timings. Run 42353532414 then exposed a keytool RSA generation child exceeding its unchanged 45-second limit after simulator boot, with zero native cases executed. Preparing certificate chains before boot avoids that demonstrated overlap and matches the successful fresh-fixture controls.\n\nAll 31 selected local helper tests pass. Native HTTP, authentication, streaming and seek deadlines/assertions remain unchanged. No application production code changes. The current run has completed fixture provisioning and is booting its dedicated simulator; final acceptance requires the full exact-head matrix and all 51 native executions, with no failures/errors/skips. The exact OS/JVM reason for the captured late response remains unproven. This issue remains open until that acceptance is complete.\n## November 7: fresh staging does not resolve the TLS stall\n\nVerify 42355618596 on 29d7b9584 fails one of 17 native cases. Bootstrap responds in approximately 62ms. The missing-client-identity exchange then receives no server response bytes until approximately 54.43 seconds after its first handshake packet, beyond the unchanged 25-second rejection deadline. The later JVM dump shows idle dispatchers/worker and misses the actual stall. No cold replays execute. This candidate is not accepted.\n\nPR #531 head cd45c2cb5 now records the disposable fixture JVM during execution using an opt-in bounded Java Flight Recording, with environment/system-property/process-command events disabled. A local standalone smoke test proves bootstrap/stop, readable recording, and absence of a private environment sentinel and sensitive event types. JVM fixture tests and 31 Python checks pass. The matched aged/fresh experiment with recording is running at https://github.com/goosepod/naviamp/actions/runs/42358080227. Native assertions and deadlines remain unchanged. The precise OS/JVM cause is still unproven; the issue remains open.\n\nCorrection to earlier checkpoint prose: source inspection confirms the existing keytool deadline is 30 seconds, not the previously stated 45 seconds. It has not been changed.\n## TLS readiness candidate, fe7ee3b70\n\nThe fixture declared readiness after binding sockets, while its host probe checked only HTTP. PR #531 now requires trusted, hostname-verified TLS handshakes on all three HTTPS listeners before publishing bootstrap. TLS-only preflight sends no HTTP requests and leaves native identity/path/range/audio audits untouched. The wrong-name listener is verified against its own certificate name; native tests still connect by IP and must reject the mismatch. Preparation happens before simulator boot, after compilation. Native test assertions and deadlines are unchanged.\n\nBoth local JVM fixture tests pass, including strict hostname rejection, untouched HTTP audits, concurrent fixture isolation and socket cleanup. A standalone recorded fixture passes the three handshakes in 561ms, 55ms and 92ms, serves live bootstrap and stops normally. Its readable recording excludes the private environment sentinel and all four sensitive event types. All 31 Python helper checks pass.\n\nExact-head corrective Verify: https://github.com/goosepod/naviamp/actions/runs/42359239143 (fe7ee3b70290e40008569a4ed45219ffad9c51d5). Full matrix and all 51 cold native executions remain required. The recorder-only comparison 42358080227 stays pinned to cd45c2cb5 and continues independently; it does not count as acceptance of the readiness candidate. The underlying OS/JVM delay remains under investigation, and this issue stays open.\n## Completed recorded comparisons, November 7\n\nRecorded run 42358080227 on cd45c2cb5 completed both two-pair comparisons. Each condition uses one unchanged native binary within its own job; hashes differ across independently built jobs, so cross-job binary identity is not claimed.\n\n- Persistent-daemon job: two aged fixtures fail only bootstrap; two fresh fixtures pass all 17 native methods. All four suites complete: 68 executions, two bootstrap failures.\n- Single-use-daemon job: both aged and both fresh fixtures pass all 17 methods. All four suites complete: 68 executions, zero failures/errors/skips.\n- Persistent-daemon second aged connection: GET at 01:37:27.423862, client cancellation at 01:37:37.746274, first server response bytes at 01:38:30.285394 (62.861532 seconds after GET). First aged connection has no response bytes before capture ends.\n- The failing JVM recordings show ordinary HTTP request parsing, class loading and Date/header formatting making gradual progress; they do not show a single long DNS wait explaining these bootstrap failures. The runner CPU is saturated throughout the second failed response window. Post-test swap use is approximately 1.8 and 2.1 GiB after the persistent-daemon failures, versus about 58 MiB after the first passing single-use control and 487 MiB after its last aged control. Failed fixtures are about 44–45 MiB resident after their tests. These post-test observations support a resource/residency mechanism; they do not isolate a specific OS paging or scheduling cause.\n- The ordinary recorded iOS job also passes all 17 native methods, production app build and Keychain test. Its first TLS response takes about 676ms. This is a passing control, not corrective-head acceptance. The overall manual workflow is correctly red because the two aged failures are retained.\n\nEarlier single-use-daemon bootstrap failure 42347030189 remains valid. Retaining a Gradle daemon is a contributing resource/lifetime condition in the new comparison, not an established sole cause of every historical failure.\n\nFinal corrective acceptance is https://github.com/goosepod/naviamp/actions/runs/42359731692 on cbe0eb00c13ae6746991eebf5d480660b0a3015f. The initial 17-case native suite has passed; two independent cold replays are running. Fixture flight recording is disabled for this PR run, including replays, so acceptance cannot depend on the profiler keeping the server active. Explicit manual diagnostics retain recording. All original native assertions/deadlines remain intact. Keep this issue open until the full exact-head matrix and all 51 executions pass.\n## Accepted correction\n\nPR #531 merged at 10e6f4e7e047988be14bd469cf31dff15f1d91ee. Verify 42359731692 passes all nine standard checks, including production iOS app build and Keychain. Artifact audit confirms three identical 17-case native suites: 51 executions, zero failures/errors/skips, both replay cleanup records and successful fixture startup traces. JVM flight recording is disabled for the entire acceptance run. The tested source is clean; accepted main tree 6bd2532e8f5202a3e9776c001bd8025feb1beed6 exactly matches the immutable verification receipt.\n\nThe correction prepares fresh fixture TLS before simulator boot, keeps it out of the long compile, and inherits single-use Gradle policy. Original native assertions and deadlines remain unchanged. Bootstrap first-response timings are 0.353s, 2.553s and 5.685s across the three suites. The exact underlying OS paging/scheduling mechanism remains unproven; the captured fixture-side delays and resource comparisons are retained above. No application production files changed.\n\n\n## November 9 bootstrap recurrence on the visualizer draft\n\n[Verify 42715678433](https://github.com/goosepod/naviamp/actions/runs/42715678433), head `56888d195505486f649eac7197ea6e399e4697f8`, iOS job `129496527729`, fails `realMutualTlsAuthenticatesAndSeeksThroughNormalPlayback` during **fixture bootstrap** at the unchanged 10,000ms deadline. State is Idle, progress unknown, TLS stage “not started”, and there are no TLS challenges. The log prints `mTLS runner bootstrap supplied`, so the preceding native main-queue callback probe completed. This is not evidence that an invalid identity authenticated or that playback assertions failed.\n\nThe expected inventory is complete: 17 started, 17 finished, 16 passing, one failing, zero ignored. Native binary SHA-256: `d025ece6ce77cc36b4d2eee4c609d2b9e3296b7132edb5b06c3bba19a69ecc0a`. The fixture's TLS readiness probes pass at 305ms, 29ms and 53ms. Its post-failure thread/socket dumps show the four HTTP listeners still present; that later snapshot does not establish what delayed the failed bootstrap request. No packet recording or JVM flight recording was enabled for this ordinary run.\n\nThe prior code head `146be1a627c5a60d8158dec0b2cbfd23b3c82a56` passed full [Verify 42708699393](https://github.com/goosepod/naviamp/actions/runs/42708699393), including native iOS. The intervening commit changes only the performance verification document. Treat the new failure as a reliability recurrence; its relationship to visualizer/native resource load is unproven. Separately, the corrected CI-cache head `2ee1820dfd5e4becb1fc4cabcc670bb94db4afbc` passes all nine checks in [Verify 42715502765](https://github.com/goosepod/naviamp/actions/runs/42715502765). Neither passing control erases this failed attempt.\n\nThe original failed log, complete inventory and diagnostics artifact are retained. PR #242 remains draft and its failed head has not been retried into acceptance. Preserve all existing bootstrap/authentication/playback assertions and deadlines; characterize native HTTP/fixture response timing and resource state before claiming a cause or fix.\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/goosepod/naviamp/issues/454/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/goosepod/naviamp/issues/454/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":"reopened","pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NeverWe1come/KMIPKit/issues/165","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NeverWe1come/KMIPKit","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NeverWe1come/KMIPKit/issues/165/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NeverWe1come/KMIPKit/issues/165/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NeverWe1come/KMIPKit/issues/165/events","html_url":"https://github.com/NeverWe1come/KMIPKit/pull/165","id":6304498200,"node_id":"PR_kwDOU6dXVs8AAAABOEmtuQ","number":165,"title":"KMIPKIT-0084 Rust live interoperability validator","user":{"login":"NeverWe1come","id":111150798,"node_id":"U_kgDOBqAGzg","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/111150798?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NeverWe1come","html_url":"https://github.com/NeverWe1come","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NeverWe1come/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NeverWe1come/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NeverWe1come/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NeverWe1come/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NeverWe1come/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NeverWe1come/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NeverWe1come/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NeverWe1come/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NeverWe1come/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[],"state":"closed","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":1,"created_at":"2026-11-10T09:57:17Z","updated_at":"2026-11-10T16:40:43Z","closed_at":"2026-11-10T16:40:43Z","assignee":null,"author_association":"OWNER","active_lock_reason":null,"draft":false,"pull_request":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NeverWe1come/KMIPKit/pulls/165","html_url":"https://github.com/NeverWe1come/KMIPKit/pull/165","diff_url":"https://github.com/NeverWe1come/KMIPKit/pull/165.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/NeverWe1come/KMIPKit/pull/165.patch","merged_at":"2026-11-10T16:40:43Z"},"body":"## Summary\n\nImplement the Rust validator as an independent consumer of installed KMIPKit `.crate` artifacts. It supports offline `plan` and `preflight`, real TLS/HTTPS requests, high-level and typed APIs, incoming listener dispatch, explicit mutation guards, redacted schema 2.0 JSON/Markdown reports, and artifact provenance.\n\nDispatch all 218 cases in the shared catalog using deterministic recipes, local enum/bitmask checks, OASIS-backed exclusions, and documented public API limitations. Run the controlled catalog through both APIs for KMIP 2.0 and 2.1. Compare per-case outcomes, canonical numeric Result Status/Reason, and normalized outgoing request/response trees, including setup and cleanup. Trees are ephemeral: timestamps are omitted, identifiers use stable aliases, and sensitive subtrees, text, bytes, and big integers are redacted. Secret and cryptographic output values are outside this structural comparison. Five incoming operations have separate tests in both protocol versions.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Built from the verified release package artifact; SHA-256: `8cb649af459ea7a37d8ecbe4da3cfe65686a324e8719f3facfaa446c2e31b123`.\n- Rust 1.94 installed-package consumer and controlled mTLS catalog, incoming, safety, and parity checks passed.\n- Validator handwritten-line coverage gate (at least 90%) passed.\n- Updating from `release/1.0.0` exposed a timing-sensitive transport test on macOS. Commit `1f06d2d1b336996fa88608c7de6f5b19dedb6400` changes it to Tokio paused time; all 108 `timeout_delivery` tests and the dependency-policy test passed locally.\n- Current head `1f06d2d1b336996fa88608c7de6f5b19dedb6400`: all 44 applicable GitHub checks passed; 3 policy/informational checks were skipped. [CI run](https://github.com/NeverWe1come/KMIPKit/actions/runs/42851497453).\n\nNo deployed vendor KMS was available; this PR makes no vendor compatibility, profile certification, or FIPS claim.\n\n## Scope and limits\n\n- The shared-contract correction in PR #167 is merged into `release/1.0.0`. This branch includes that release update and runs the 218-case catalog.\n- Ping, Process, Query Asynchronous Requests, and Set Defaults remain excluded from KMIP 2.0 based on the pinned OASIS 2.0 source.\n- KMIP 2.0 Login, Logout, and Delegated Login pass through the controlled peer using the session-tag correction in the release branch.\n- Generic outbound TTLV submission, raw-TLS text listener reception, and authentication injection into async follow-ups remain documented public API limits. Direct State mutation is a library preflight rejection, independent of validator mutation opt-in, and is not counted as server support.\n- Cross-language parity remains `not_compared` until comparable runs use the same server, version, configuration, catalog, and library.\n\nThe PR is ready for review. Approval and merge remain with the maintainer; neither is requested by this update.","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NeverWe1come/KMIPKit/issues/165/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NeverWe1come/KMIPKit/issues/165/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1610","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/anaregdesign/lantern","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1610/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1610/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1610/events","html_url":"https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1610","id":5684505136,"node_id":"I_kwDOJeHh7M8AAAABUtKqMA","number":1610,"title":"track(auth): coordinate remaining distribution and selected-environment acceptance","user":{"login":"piroyoung","id":6128022,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjYxMjgwMjI=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/6128022?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/piroyoung","html_url":"https://github.com/piroyoung","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/piroyoung/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/piroyoung/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/piroyoung/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/piroyoung/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/piroyoung/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/piroyoung/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/piroyoung/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/piroyoung/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/piroyoung/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":5458718403,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJeHh7M8AAAABRV1uww","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/anaregdesign/lantern/labels/enhancement","name":"enhancement","color":"a2eeef","default":true,"description":"New feature or request","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":11120411215,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJeHh7M8AAAACltQCTw","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/anaregdesign/lantern/labels/go","name":"go","color":"16e2e2","default":false,"description":"Pull requests that update go code","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":11128734818,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJeHh7M8AAAACl1MEYg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/anaregdesign/lantern/labels/ha","name":"ha","color":"5319e7","default":false,"description":"High availability / leaderless replication epic","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":11428602419,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJeHh7M8AAAACqTKiMw","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/anaregdesign/lantern/labels/admin","name":"admin","color":"ededed","default":false,"description":null,"archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":210,"created_at":"2026-10-02T23:06:08Z","updated_at":"2026-11-10T16:38:17Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"parent_issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1599","issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":4,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"## Current acceptance and backlog index (2026-11-10 JST)\n\n**OPEN: remaining work resumed, except actual Helm/Kubernetes verification.** #2076/#2083 source, non-Dart publication and actual three-node OFF/OIDC acceptance are complete at their recorded source. Later maintenance #2104 and #2105 is merged with final main CI passing. Historical evidence below keeps its original source and scope.\n\n### Remaining owners\n\n- **#1766: Dart installed sync and archives (P1).** Android/iPhone factory/sync/adoption 0/2; parent/Offline archive/publication 0/2. Current source candidates are parent 0.5.0 and Offline 0.6.0; live pub.dev latest versions are parent 0.4.1 and Offline 0.5.0. Fresh parent physical evidence and verified publication/archive equality precede the actual hosted-lock refresh and Offline's separate physical/archive/publication gates. SQLite remains unpublished.\n- **#1750: native Google/Apple login (P2).** Real Google Android/iOS and Apple iOS/Android 0/4; affected publication/archive 0/1. Browser, synthetic-provider and host tests do not complete native-provider/device acceptance.\n- **#1758: installed enrollment and personal area (P2).** Installed acceptance 0/1. Browser registration, UID/personal-area source and Allow-only typed-resource behavior are implemented; actual signed installed acceptance remains separate.\n- **#1636: Kubernetes (P2, held).** Actual cluster 0/1 UNVERIFIED. The owner explicitly deferred Helm verification again on 2026-11-09. It is not a prerequisite for the resumed mobile/Dart work.\n\n### Current prerequisites and boundaries\n\n**Completed source prerequisite:** #2116/#2117 closed through [PR #2118](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/pull/2118) at `bd75e7b0455ff57c14858f05426511bf2679fa5b`, tree `0f8a4c458cb8f8046f31e4454ecd0a4894292ea8`. Fresh local 79/79 (0 carried) and all four selected exact-main workflows passed ([Go](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/actions/runs/42661619786), [Admin](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/actions/runs/42661619636), [Dart SDK](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/actions/runs/42661620295), and [CodeQL](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/actions/runs/42661619687)); Node/Rust workflows were NOT_SELECTED and conditional skipped jobs remain SKIPPED. Native target host builds at this source are staged separately; installed/provider acceptance is still uncompleted.\n\nBoth signed normal native apps were installed from `7223e6176c488eca7c62c78afde3ce3a0cd5f786`. #2112 and #2113 are closed by normal PRs #2115/#2114, with combined exact-main CI PASS. No new installed acceptance has passed. The first Android Google attempt returned a sign-in-incomplete message; its exact cause is not established. iPhone install passed after reconnection, while later launch/control-tunnel failures remain separately recorded; a cached device listing is not live lock/connectivity proof. See [the retained attempt and cleanup checkpoint](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1750#issuecomment-6743876446).\n\nThe isolated normal-build Server passed one-time receipt-WAL initialization, guarded ordinary restart and later fully observed normal CLEAN shutdown. All task listeners/services are stopped, with JSON, identity material and native/WAL state retained for intact resume. Actual Admin inspection confirmed registration OFF, selected four Native profiles and scoped joining Role; #2116 blocked Apply and no activation occurred. The bounded source fix is now qualified; rebuilt Admin/runtime artifact use and actual approval/device/provider exits remain separate. Use normal WAL restart and publisher resume. Source, physical/provider, exact publication/archive and security-change approval boundaries remain separate.\n\nShare compatible #1750/#1758/#1766 evidence without double-counting. Record source, installed-device, actual-provider and archive/publication evidence independently.\n\n### Completed maintenance and retained limits\n\n- #2103, #2089 and #1902 closed through [PR #2104](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/pull/2104). Closure does not mean every optional/deferred proposal was implemented.\n- #1920 closed through [PR #2105](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/pull/2105). Two signer-erasure SA1019 warnings intentionally remain documented; this is not whole-Server lint PASS.\n- The historical [singleton /readyz observation](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1610#issuecomment-6714556950) is now repaired by #2112 / PR #2115 and qualified by its source/runtime evidence. That repair does not increment native-provider or physical-device exits.\n- Unselected production, legacy-cutover, quantitative and separate Mac-host SDK4 claims remain unqualified. Do not create additional blanket acceptance campaigns from dated records.\n\nThe retained checkpoints below are historical; their old open counts, holds and immediate order are superseded by this index.\n\n---\n<details>\n<summary>Retained dated checkpoints and historical requirements</summary>\n<!-- root0361-delivery-20261031 -->\n## Current non-Dart delivery checkpoint — 2026-10-31\n\n#1834 is complete for the declared non-Dart releases and actual public-artifact local OFF / synthetic OIDC-MCP profiles. Server/CLI [v0.36.1](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/releases/tag/v0.36.1) is published from exact main `da346671fa89a0ac7b47810f1a2c3d2c3ba1c5a7`; canonical [tag CI](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/actions/runs/41178394371) passed all eight jobs. Public archive/checksum and OCI source/signature/provenance correspondence, OFF backup/intact restart/fresh restore, and synthetic three-member OIDC/MCP same-state restart have passed. Bounded acceptance-harness repairs #1977/#1979 are closed; historical failures remain preserved. Disposable runtime resources were removed after evidence retention.\n\nPublished non-Dart families now include PB 0.15.1, Core 0.21.2, Go SDK 0.27.2, MCP 0.15.2, Admin 0.24.0, Rust SDK 0.4.0 and root Server/CLI 0.36.1. The dated version/publication counts below are historical.\n\nThis does not close this broader coordinator. Real-provider/browser, Dart publication, physical-device, installed-factory/registration and Kubernetes/deployment evidence retain their existing independent holds. Separate Mac-host SDK4 qualification is NOT_RUN; hosted Linux source proof is not another host's qualification. No held work was resumed or waived.\n\n---\n\n## Configuration target update — 2026-10-25\n\n#1871 is completed through [PR #1873](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/pull/1873): the environment-based OFF/OIDC implementation, explicit initialization action and fake-placeholder Compose samples are merged as `2f2996738d4779f3937238c807cfdd6fe40399fb`, tree `fbf02d7616181b2cfd086a70c9f86b8b9eb577d5`. The fresh combined local gate passed **77/77, carry 0**, required PR checks passed, and all six ordinary exact-main workflows succeeded ([Go](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/actions/runs/40163319195)). #1872's minimal Tokio 1.53.3 repair is also merged and closed. It replaces the operator-facing proposal of separate secret-binding/key environment inputs and a routine fresh/resume switch. The existing authority, native time, membership-renewal, durable custody and revocation contracts remain required; source work is not current publication or provider/device acceptance. #1834 continues to own independent published-runtime delivery. Existing held provider, Dart, device and Kubernetes work stays held.\n\n---\n\n## Current coordination index (2026-10-23)\n\n**OPEN.** This umbrella coordinates distribution and independently selected acceptance evidence. #1834 owns current non-Dart release execution and shipped local OFF/OIDC Compose qualification. Source integration, publication, final-image performance, published-runtime checks, real providers and physical devices remain separate claims.\n\n### Active owners and dependencies\n\n- **#1834: remaining publication and shipped-runtime acceptance.** Published component families include pb0.15.0, Core0.21.0, Go SDK0.27.0, Node0.14.0, Admin0.23.0 and MCP0.15.0. Server/CLI0.36.0, Rust SDK0.4.0 and the MCP0.15.1 rebuild remain unpublished; their exact remote tags are absent at this triage.\n- **#1843: not-started P2 maintenance.** Static-bearer scaffolding retirement is distinct from the completed MCP prose repair, Snapshot recovery and release execution. It is not a new blanket release prerequisite.\n- Completed bounded source/setup repairs: **#1838, #1840, #1866 and #1867**. Their closure does not close #1834 or qualify unpublished artifacts.\n\nAt the previous checkpoint, main was `ab43d9d3561f5b2ee4f6112f906447fb14b06759`; [PR #1870](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/pull/1870) records fresh local **77/77 PASS, carry 0**, with identical reviewed/main tree and all 13 main checks passing. Its load ledger records formal PASS for all 8+4 scenarios on the unchanged product `7001e264` image, cumulatively over six finite intervals and multiple diagnostic harnesses. It is not a fresh single-harness campaign. Use #1834 for exact current source/image/UTC provenance rather than carrying older-source results forward.\n\nEarlier failure/NOT_RUN records remain intact. Passing later cases does not prove every historical cause, leak absence or a production SLO. Further historical causal investigation is not added as a completion prerequisite for the scoped repairs.\n\n### Open acceptance exits\n\n- [ ] Complete the three remaining publications and their normal tag/Search/protected-environment/archive/image/signature gates in #1834.\n- [ ] Complete published-image OFF and OIDC Compose startup, authorized operations/refusals, normal stop and intact restart.\n- [ ] Complete published MCP runtime/tool-inventory verification.\n- [ ] Reconcile independent final artifact/evidence identities before closing the current non-Dart delivery owner.\n\n### Deferred independent owners\n\n- **#1766:** Dart parent/Offline publication/archive **0/2**, installed Android/iPhone factory/sync **0/2**.\n- **#1750:** real Google/Apple native-provider acceptance **0/4**, plus its affected publication exit.\n- **#1758:** installed registration/personal-area acceptance **0/1**; runtime registration remains disabled.\n- **#1636:** separately deferred Kubernetes acceptance **0/1 UNVERIFIED**, excluded from current delivery prerequisites.\n\nDart/provider/device/Kubernetes work remains **held, not complete**. The later Google reauthentication refusal in #1834 occurred before registration enablement was dispatched; earlier LAN/helper observations do not establish its cause or create an immediate network task. Keep resolved provider/profile selections and original requirements without resuming external operations.\n\nThe dated history below preserves prior source proofs, requirements and failed runs; its old issue/PR counts, publication counters, source SHAs and immediate phone/provider order are not the current work queue.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Retained dated requirements, preparation and historical evidence</summary>\n\n## Current delivery exits - 2026-10-18\n\nThis dated index supersedes the historical current-status wording below, not its valid evidence. Read-only remote metadata still shows main `95f3d626c64512c482e6e47076edf3426a7a8def`; PR #1816 is OPEN at `e233647248a557569b1451453d90c81c9bbfcdeb`, with iOS and aggregate Gate failures. This metadata read is not fresh worktree/host or final-source qualification.\n\n| Independent exit | Done/total | Current blocker |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Prepared source/CI integration groups | 0/3 | #1815/#1818 repair-observation, #1817 private examples, and held Node/Rust release metadata; actual published Go consumer pins are later separate work, not included in this denominator. |\n| Selected final-source acceptance | 0/1 | Unmerged `b44427de` canonical gate: 65 PASS / 1 FAIL / 11 NOT_RUN, 66 executed / 0 carried; F PASS / configured-source Q FAIL. #1819 remains an operational investigation, not a demonstrated product/Rust defect. |\n| Ten target publications and actual artifact/archive verification | 0/10 | No target release published by this work; retain pb/core before Go consumers, Node before Admin, and parent Dart archive before hosted Offline lock and release. |\n| Human/device and selected deployment evidence | Separate 0/2 mobile, 0/4 native-provider, 0/1 installed signup, 0/1 Helm | #1766, #1750, #1758 and #1636 respectively. Helm is now selected/prepared but unexecuted, not excluded from the resumed task. No image/device/platform proof is supplied by preparation. |\n\nThe single owned-PID observation stopped before GO, with empty sampler output: [retained result](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1819#issuecomment-6236257509). It is neither a new source PASS nor a completed source FAIL. Its authorization is consumed; @piroyoung owns existing-path-log/next-method selection only. No new probe, collector repair, privilege/source/profile/guard/time change, Q/full gate or CI rerun is inferred. #1788/#1791 are completed historical follow-ups, not new maintenance work.\n\nIndependent registry preparation confirmed the Rust expected-owner metadata match; current Rust Trusted Publisher binding remains unverified because browser reading was unavailable. This is not publication authorization or archive acceptance: [record](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1610#issuecomment-6236524039). Private provider/bootstrap decisions and final physical evidence remain separate.\n\n<!-- lantern-status-refresh-20261018 -->\n\n---\n\n## Historical records retained below\n\n## Current remaining-work index — 2026-10-16\n\nRead-only reconciliation against main [`b0e86df6`](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/commit/b0e86df62f0dabdfb51db82e489d01e6975ca8f7). This index supersedes older progress counts, prerequisites and unselected blanket campaigns retained below. It does not change production guards or repository qualification policy.\n\n| Work / claim | Current evidence and next owner |\n| --- | --- |\n| Normal-container auth/RBAC/Head/CDC/HA source functionality | Complete within the bounded source-functional map below; #1599, #1608, #1609, #1612 and #1613 remain closed. Do not repeat a historical whole matrix without a changed input or identified gap. |\n| Ordinary real Google browser data/permission check | [Human-reported 15/15 PASS](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1610#issuecomment-6119080662), scoped CRUD plus explicit-Deny/outside-prefix/mixed-read refusal. [Run ending and limitations](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1610#issuecomment-6120204082) retain the earlier BLOCKED outcome, unknown cause and readiness dips. This is human-verified browser evidence, not independent capture, native plugin evidence or uninterrupted availability. No repeat is required merely because the operation window ended early. |\n| Continuing membership lifecycle required in the next root release | #1742 source and exact-main qualification complete through #1745/#1749; [completion](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1610#issuecomment-6137474332). The earlier failed source remains FAIL. Include this delivered change in the next root release; version, publication and deployment remain separate. |\n| Native Google/Apple login and registration | #1750 owns four real-provider/platform exits; #1758 owns registration/personal-area acceptance and default-disabled activation. Their source implementations and CI are merged. Browser Google evidence does not qualify native credentials. |\n| Unified Dart automatic Sync | #1766 owns the new product's acceptance: [6/6 children, 25/25 source/host items, 1/1 exact-source exit](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1766#issuecomment-6215033437). Physical Android/iPhone 0/2 STOPPED and parent/Offline publication/archive equality 0/2 NOT STARTED. Prior `8a255c85` remains NOT_RUN. |\n| Remaining distribution | This Issue coordinates release ordering; #1750/#1758/#1766 link their affected feature evidence to the same actual artifacts. One publication or device record may satisfy multiple mapped claims; do not add the overlapping counters or repeat the same run. Preserve pb/Core before consumers, Go SDK before root consumers, Node before Admin, independent Rust gates, then Dart parent archive equality before hosted Offline lock/archive/publication. SQLite remains `publish_to: none`. The old 0/9 is a historical cohort inventory, not a refreshed registry audit or nine new mandatory runs. |\n| Selected production environment / quantitative claims | Conditional and unscheduled. Choose the actual deployment or numerical claim before budgeting only its relevant evidence. No undefined universal SLO, fixed three-repeat campaign, whole-history crash cross-product, both architectures, or physical-host campaign is added to daily source use. Existing mandatory F/Q, security, physical-release and archive gates remain in force. |\n| Reliability follow-ups / excluded work | #1788 is the remaining unimplemented classifier-cleanup investigation (P2). #1791's bounded diagnostic implementation is complete through #1789; its historical cause remains unknown and is not a current #1766 blocker. #1636 remains deferred Helm/Kubernetes acceptance, outside current delivery. |\n\n**Practical order:** maintain source usability and address #1788 as a bounded maintenance task; when the owner resumes external work, select one compatible candidate and jointly map #1750/#1758/#1766 provider/device cases, then execute the existing release order. Production topology and optional quantitative promises are selected separately. Physical/OS-sleep, release and deployment are not resumed by this audit. No new design decision is needed for this reconciliation; enabling registration for exact provider profiles remains an explicit owner decision.\n\n---\n\n## SDK external exits transferred from #1613\n\nTransfer recorded on 2026-10-10. This Issue is the explicit owner of the **unfinished** SDK distribution, affected installed-platform and conditional quantitative claims below. #1613's stream/receipt/cache source-function criteria have been mapped to existing contract, native and real-wire evidence; no additional functional implementation or test gap was identified in that bounded scope. This section supersedes the earlier pending #1613 mapping wording. #1612's source-function completion is recorded in the normal-container map below; its unselected quantitative claims remain explicitly unqualified.\n\n- [ ] **Distribution / hosted archives — not completed.** Separately authorize and execute the compatible parent release, verify its actual pub.dev archive against the exact candidate's file paths and bytes, generate and review a real hosted offline lock satisfying the declared parent floor/checksum, then qualify the independent isolated offline archive and verify its actual publication/archive equality. Preserve parent-before-offline and the existing overall release order. No path override, paired-source result or fabricated lock substitutes for hosted proof; SQLite remains `publish_to: none`. The dated 2026-10-10 17:56 UTC inventory in #1613 found candidate `lantern_client 0.5.0` / `lantern_client_offline 0.6.0` unpublished. This transfer does not refresh that inventory or authorize publication.\n- [ ] **Affected installed mobile/platform claims — not completed; postponed while the owner is away.** Keep actual Android/iPhone evidence for any retained installed-platform claim distinct from source completion. Scope it to the affected OS trust/local-network privacy, radio/vendor power/lifecycle behavior and executed signed binary, including any selected installed offline/receipt/Head/CDC behavior. Existing broad parent-tag and offline physical gates remain active until repository rules/code are changed normally; this record grants no bypass. Private publisher-binding confirmation and any needed device/runtime fixture actions remain separate owner-held operations, not newly provisioned or performed here.\n- [ ] **Conditional quantitative stream/cache/backpressure guarantees — unselected / not qualified.** Before claiming sustained fanout/consumer lag, producer/tail latency, memory/heap/allocation or quantitative non-regression, select the specific claim, workload/budget, relevant environment and evidence. Functional bounded-queue/drop, cancellation, resume and lock-release tests do not establish those measurements. No universal SLO, three-repeat rule, whole-matrix rerun or physical-host/clock qualification is added by this transfer; unselected/unmeasured guarantees stay unqualified without reopening completed source functionality.\n\nReusable source evidence: [#1725 bounded S4 completion ledger](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1725#execution-and-completion-ledger), [current-main native SDK4 producer](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/actions/runs/38078360332/job/114290016728), and [Dart paired-source / real-wire / SQLite CI](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/actions/runs/38078360093). The producer records head `058805a51e9bd566b898c22a1f8a8ba8fd84aee1`, tree `d56fef60ecaac22b47289bb809dadb7cf4af2532`, receipt SHA-256 `1c32f742543bd942a13a10f08eb4431128beff03788c5cf3312e679ffcaf7c8f`. It is configured native/source evidence, not physical-host certification. See #1613's source completion map for named cases and the separation between component, current-v2 native and real-wire proof.\n\nNo new product change, test, deployment, credential, tag or publication was performed for this record transfer. Earlier failures, source attribution, quantitative limitations and all original content below are retained; distribution, physical devices and performance are not marked passed.\n\n---\n\n## Selected-environment operations transferred from #1609\n\nTransfer recorded on 2026-10-10. #1610 owns the **unselected / unverified** operational conditions below after #1609's bounded source/runbook completion. They become applicable when an actual production environment or legacy cutover is selected and separately authorized; they are not universal development blockers or reasons to reopen completed source functionality. No selected environment is qualified by this transfer. The SDK external-exit ledger above and independent quantitative/provider/device claims retain their states; the completed source-function map below does not qualify this unselected environment.\n\nThe operator-facing configuration/startup contract for any later selected environment is the completed #1883 / PR #1885 design: supply and consume the same complete `LANTERN_SECURITY_CONFIG_JSON` on provisioning, explicit initialization and every start/restart. Durable material retains identity keys, fixed trust and seed identity; it is not a current-configuration fallback. Internal native lifecycle/fixture names and their original evidence remain unchanged; this clarification does not remove existing-state, CLEAN, time, quorum, renewal or custody checks.\n\n- [ ] **Public origin and routing — environment not selected / not verified.** For a selected production environment, record one trusted HTTPS public origin for Admin/browser/auth/RPC and each exact Issuer-specific callback. Select the operator-controlled profile-eligible control nodes and pin login start/callback only to the initiating attempt/process; preserve independent data/CDC cursor affinity contracts. Verify actual upstream hostname/TLS/CA and exact trusted-proxy Host/scheme/IP boundaries, with no browser-controlled upstream and no public private-workload listener. Retain a sanitized route/configuration map and relevant boundary evidence. The existing fixed-upstream Caddy implementation does not qualify production eligible-node topology or provide an automatic eligible-node router.\n- [ ] **Original inputs and least-privilege key/secret placement — environment not selected / not verified.** For that environment, confirm the original genesis/node/cohort/generation/member/incarnation and signed membership/capability inputs. Keep each origin's own key and only the IdP secret bindings required by its login capability; an origin-free member has no origin private key. Separate read-only original provisioning/keys, private durable M/P/B and private custody mounts, with fixed paths, private regular-file ownership/permissions and no aliases. Record sanitized role/mount/binding checks; private keys, client secrets, tokens and PKCE verifiers never enter shared sys state or public artifacts.\n- [ ] **Authority/data readiness and normal intact restart — environment not selected / not verified.** For the selected environment, verify fresh native time under the existing conditional source/path/rate premises, valid membership/TLS, current quorum/catch-up and independent data/WAL/replication/search readiness before protected serving. Size the service-manager stop budget for drain, worker/producer joins, all closes, final floors and CLEAN last. Check process success and matching CLEAN binding/cycle/exact floor hash; then perform ordinary startup/restart with the same complete `LANTERN_SECURITY_CONFIG_JSON` and intact original identity, durable material and persistence. Explicit `init-security` is a one-time action for new state, not a fresh/resume switch or a recovery fallback. Preserve refused state and logs: no deleting custody/leases, implicit fresh/reset, rebuilt floors or OFF fallback. Reuse existing normal restart/network-loss/recovery/refusal evidence when its relevant inputs remain valid; add environment evidence only for a real gap. Container evidence does not certify physical reboot/power-loss behavior, and separate custody storage is not an external monotonic witness against whole-bundle rollback.\n- [ ] **Legacy cutover — conditional; not selected / not performed.** Only if an actual legacy installation is selected for cutover, review and authorize its stopped/fenced transition: retain original status/data/receipt history and import provenance, choose independent keys/genesis and a new generation/cohort, invalidate old sessions/cursors and install compatible clients. Reject old peers/readers before transfer or floor advancement. Record the plan and sanitized execution/readiness evidence for that selected environment. No mixed-cohort rolling activation, silent reset, automatic/online converter or downgrade is implied; new cohort numbers alone do not establish current grants. A deployment with no legacy installation does not acquire a fictitious migration task.\n\nReusable public evidence: [#1726 current-v2 composition](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/pull/1726), [#1728 orderly custody and its bounded existing campaign](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/pull/1728), [#1734 operations alignment and existing qualification](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/pull/1734), the [CLEAN/resume contract](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/blob/440dc16431ce8d0598ec9af2200161ccf98113bd/docs/security-current-authority.md#orderly-floor-custody-and-process-restart) and [custody Compose runbook](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/blob/440dc16431ce8d0598ec9af2200161ccf98113bd/testbed/current-custody/README.md). Keep each result's original source/fixture attribution and all earlier failures. Real-provider, installed-device, distribution and quantitative claims retain their independent ledgers; physical sleep/live resume, backup/VM snapshot/clone return and Helm/Kubernetes remain deferred outside this source scope.\n\nThis explicit ledger completes the earlier generic transfer/mapping wording for #1609, not the unchecked operational conditions. It supersedes earlier references to keeping #1609 open for an unselected deployment. Active repository local/CI/release gates remain unchanged. No new test, product edit, deployment, credential, host configuration, device action or publication was performed or authorized by this record update.\n\n\n---\n\n## Normal-container source-functional acceptance complete — 2026-10-10\n\nThe selected normal-container source-functional criteria are now mapped to existing proof, including the final active-export case integrated at [462adebe](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/commit/462adebeff53d2747480253260a71b16bdad66b7). No remaining product implementation or functional-test gap was identified in this bounded scope. This Issue stays open as the explicit owner of the unfinished conditional environment, provider/device, quantitative and distribution claims above/below; their non-completion is not relabeled as a source defect.\n\n### Reusable completed evidence\n\n- [x] #1723/#1724 native authority and #1725/#1726 public current-v2 control/session/data/SDK/Admin/offline source.\n- [x] #1727/#1728 normal custody: 10 phases/9 CLEAN checkpoints/two intact restart cycles; #1729/#1730 post-main audit-fixture repair.\n- [x] #1670 rendered refusal/correction; #1732 TLS session-view/relogin recovery at 1280/390 across two qualifying runs with Apply exactly once; the additional 12:22 UTC suspend case passed old-session401/new-callback401/other-admin200.\n- [x] #1733 one-file observation repair and d39cea9 six successful post-main workflows; #1734 docs-only local76/fresh SDK4/scoped Rust and owning CI.\n- [x] #1674/#1706 WAL safety and #1667/#1677 staged lifecycle source. Historical unknown failure causes are retained, not automatically new acceptance blockers.\n- [x] #1710/#1731 query preparation and the [2026-10-10 exploratory record](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1612#issuecomment-6099834717): 24 cases/9m27s, 23 passing plus one strict unissued-slot failure. No final statistical claim is inferred.\n- [x] #1701/#1702 release ordering and relevant scoped paired-source/native/SQLite source gates.\n\n### Completed normal-container functional evidence map\n\n| Criterion | Existing public proof and completion boundary |\n| --- | --- |\n| OFF/capability/scope/cache/CSRF/credential and management Role/Deny/revoke/suspend/high-impact/machine boundaries | Closed #1606/#1607: PR #1732, named source unit/native/Playwright cases, normal-TLS cases and the separately passed suspension case. No new continuous whole-UI run was needed. |\n| Readiness, orderly intact restart, network loss/recovery and refused abnormal checkpoint | [#1728](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/pull/1728): 10 phases, 9 CLEAN checkpoints, two restart cycles, partition/reconnect, isolated restart and pre-CLEAN kill refusal. Keep its original native/source/storage assumptions; this is not physical power-loss certification. |\n| Uncertain-WAL fail-closed behavior and lost-ack original-result recovery | [#1674/#1706](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/pull/1706): local/relay adapters, real receipt-less Create, held-WAL observers, panic fault latching and actual FileWAL write-success/lost-ack replay. |\n| Publication order, held-WAL visibility and immutable chosen/original results | Closed [#1667](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1667) / [#1677](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/pull/1677): behavior-preserving staged lifecycle and reviewed lock/release/publication boundaries. No quantitative before/after claim is inferred. |\n| TTL/Delete/data-component restore, shared-corpus isolation, hidden-resource scope and cursor invalidation | #1612's existing Core/real-Connect functional record and [shared-ranking verification](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/blob/058805a51e9bd566b898c22a1f8a8ba8fd84aee1/testbed/bench/evidence/issue-1612/shared-ranking/README.md#verification-and-remaining-acceptance); current-v2 cut/old-cursor refusal is linked in closed #1613. Data/component restore is distinct from deferred security backup/clone return. |\n| Active current-v2 export after real authorization withdrawal | [#1738](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/pull/1738) / [exact-main completion](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1612#issuecomment-6102204705): already-authorized five-byte prefix may complete; the later body is refused after real Prepare/Apply revocation. Full76 fresh, PR4/main3 attempt1 passed; exact-main SDK4/export receipt executed with zero required skips. Historical writer-only negative-control FAIL remains pinned to 7d215eea and is not relabeled as a latest-main run. |\n| Scoped stream/receipt/cache/frame recovery and functional bounded backpressure | Closed [#1613's named evidence map](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1613): native current-v2 and real-wire/SQLite execution, opaque cursor/cache drift, original status-first safety and no unsafe resend. |\n| Source/runbook versus actual environment | Closed #1609: source/lifecycle/runbook complete. The selected-environment ledger above retains every unperformed routing/key-placement/readiness/cutover condition without treating an unselected deployment as a missing product implementation. |\n\n- [x] All selected source-functional criteria have public source/case/CI references; only a newly established gap or invalidated input warrants more execution.\n- [x] Conditional SDK and environment exits have explicit ownership here, while #1612's quantitative limitations and original failed sample are preserved below.\n- [x] No new test, product change, blanket matrix, performance rerun or requirement was introduced by this record reconciliation.\n\n### Conditional query measurements transferred from #1612\n\n#1610 owns these **unselected / not qualified** quantitative claims after #1612's source closure. Select an actual quantitative claim, workload/budget, repetitions and appropriate environment before undertaking further measurement; no universal SLO, fixed three-repeat rule or quiet-host prerequisite is added to ordinary source completion.\n\n- [ ] Any selected p99/long-run tail/miss-probability, allocation/internal heap/retained/exact-peak, internal writer-lock-wait or whole-family/phase comparative guarantee remains unqualified where unmeasured. Functional tests and endpoint latency/RSS cannot substitute for those specific measurements.\n- Preserve the [24-case / 566.7-second normal-use exploration](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1612#issuecomment-6099834717) exactly as observed: **23 passing cases and one strict offered-schedule failure**, 4,401 of 4,402 reader slots issued, all 2,000 writer slots passed, 11 preparation pairs passed and one rejected. The missed producer_saturated slot remains failed; it was not a DB rejection or proof of an internal lock defect.\n- Keep p50/p95 as successful-sample, per-repetition observations and RSS as externally sampled resident footprint. Do not promote these to offered-load success, pooled/tail percentiles, internal heap/lock measurements, isolated JWT cost or final performance/no-regression acceptance. No rerun is scheduled to erase the failure.\n\nThese unchecked quantitative claims are not source-function blockers and do not reopen #1612. #1667's refactor-before/after comparison remains a separate, unmeasured axis.\n\n### Conditional quantitative and external work\n\n- Quantitative performance/no-regression claims require their predeclared workload, relevant environment and evidence. A controlled quiet host is appropriate for those comparisons; it is not a universal normal-container functional gate. No undefined SLO or numerical protected-query budget is added.\n- p99/heap/retained/exact-peak/internal-lock-wait remain unmeasured where absent. They do not by themselves keep source functionality incomplete. Full-family/phase measurement and repetitions must serve a stated claim; there is no substantiated universal three-repeat rule.\n- #1667 matched before/after quantitative non-regression remains unmeasured here. #1612 OFF/ON measurements cannot substitute for that different comparison.\n- Real-provider/human, installed Android/iPhone, actual hosted archive equality and publication have independent states. They apply to the affected provider/mobile/release claim, not every source change.\n- #1613's dated registry check confirms candidate parent 0.5.0/offline 0.6.0 are unpublished. Source/Git/path development is available. For actual distribution, preserve parent-before-offline generated hosted-lock/archive/equality and existing release ordering; no publication is authorized here.\n\nPhysical sleep/live resume, rare backup/VM snapshot/clone same-cluster return and Helm/Kubernetes #1636 remain outside the current delivery. The #1668 mixed-transaction implementation is complete via [#1739](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/pull/1739), and the selected real three-node HA campaign plus its two defect repairs are complete via [#1740](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/pull/1740) ([merged-source and CI completion record](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1610#issuecomment-6115246876)). Existing normal restart/network recovery and fail-closed missing/mixed/uncertain/known-old-state behavior stay required; universal rollback detection is not claimed.\n\nPreserve the current native kernel/storage premises, policy and durability guarantees. No physical reboot/power-loss proof is inferred from containers. Keep failed evidence and original source attribution; do not require every reusable evidence item to share a later documentation-only SHA.\n\n### Adopted prose gate and remaining policy proposals\n\n#1736/#1737 is merged: the narrow explanatory-prose path applies only to its verified eligible inputs and executes fresh required checks; canonical/security/platform contracts and unknown inputs retain full qualification. The adoption itself passed the prior full76. Identical-SHA/whole-sequence reruns without relevant changes and both physical platforms on every pure-Dart parent tag remain scope/cost correction candidates. Until repository instructions and gate code are changed through their normal route, current mandatory local/CI/release checks remain required. A policy-changing PR uses the previously applicable gate. This Issue grants no skip or assertion weakening.\n\n### Evidence continuity\n\nThe detailed historical record below is preserved. The current scope/completion criteria and explicit transfer owners above supersede its older progress, broad acceptance scheduling and source-versus-release prerequisites. Historical checklists are not a second current task list. Security, durability, immutable-original and fail-closed guarantees remain in force; no unperformed quantitative, environmental or release claim is marked qualified. Reuse valid evidence and retain original failures and source attribution.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Retained detailed requirements and historical evidence</summary>\n\n## Approved ordinary-management policy update — 2026-10-05\n\n#1672 owns removal of the mandatory five-minute recent-auth gate from ordinary Server/Admin management. Missing/older signed auth_time is allowed with valid authentication and explicit authority. On 2026-10-05 at 03:30:13 UTC the owner approved per-operation reauthentication for high-impact trust changes/effective security.manage expansion, and prohibited machine management mutations while retaining explicitly authorized reference/status access. G1 in #1608 is selected as Profile B bounded freshness with fail-closed stale isolated serving; its algorithm, numerical timing bounds and migration remain unqualified. Human Bearer management remains eligible; trusted actor classification is an implementation/compatibility question, not a browser-only policy. Historical evidence and all completion counters are unchanged.\n\n## Reconciled remaining work and execution plan — 2026-10-05\n\n**Single final-qualification owner.** Historical baseline source/CI is complete; practical repairs remain **0/3** (#1669–#1671), new #1672 policy source/CI is **0/1**, and leaderless proof is not delivered. Preserve target/provider/device/publication counters and historical failures. The 29-case local-provider API exploration is diagnostic history, not combined UI/provider or new-policy qualification.\n\n- [ ] Early preparation in parallel: real Server/IdP browser fixture (#1606/#1607/#1609), a genuinely protected Role-bound TLS comparative path (#1612), scoped CDC/receipt cases and parent/offline workflow repair (#1613), and S0 profile/model review (#1608).\n- [ ] Immediate sequence #1669 -> #1672 -> #1670 final rejection/correction cases, with #1671 independent. Actual UI proves ordinary missing/older-auth_time success and preserved denial/recovery checks before broad target changes. Carry regressions forward; source proof and browser reproduction remain distinct.\n- [ ] After approved S1–S5 and all selected candidate changes, freeze exact Server source/image once, declare workload/floors/numeric budgets/run order/repetitions/fresh-state boundaries, preflight the qualified clock/quiet-host/native environment and run one linked HA/recovery/security/performance/combined UI matrix. Domain Issues supply scenarios; they do not require duplicate whole-host sweeps. Re-run only after relevant changes, invalid evidence or predeclared repetitions.\n- [ ] Distinguish #1667's matched before/after refactor comparison from the OFF/ON performance axis. If the refactor is included, land it before freeze; #1668 design is not a final-acceptance prerequisite.\n- [ ] Independently finish ordinary Google OIDC login, actual provider-neutral Server/Admin ordinary management (including missing/older auth_time success), exact Android/iPhone and archive/publication gates. Separately test selected high-impact/machine policy. Google Security bundle/extra-claim app verification and #1636 Helm are excluded from blockers.\n\n**Order correction:** Server behavior is implemented before adapting SDK/Admin consumers; the Server module has no SDK dependency. Publication later obeys archive dependency order (pb/Core → Go SDK before root CLI artifacts, Node before Admin, independent Rust; Dart last). Parent publication/equality precedes offline hosted-lock refresh/resolution and the offline candidate's exact-source/device/archive qualification, then offline publication. Do not demand an unpublished parent from a parent-tag Gate or infer hosted acceptance from a paired-source override.\n\nThis is an executable plan, not permission to select G1–G4, start costly environments/provider setup, deploy or publish. Current exact-source acceptance remains **0/1**, human/provider **0/2**, physical devices **0/2**, artifact publication **0/9**.\n\nBaseline source: [93d53789](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/blob/93d537892f3025d4a1666dcab36c5748d5cfb9f6). Active inventory and shared execution order: #1599; final matrix/evidence owner: #1610. This planning update preserves all historical evidence and done/total counts.\n\n## Approved baseline scope — Google Security bundle excluded, 2026-10-05\n\nThe owner explicitly chose **Google Security bundle is not a baseline requirement**. Google-specific additional `auth_time`/`amr` enablement, the Google app publication/verification required for those optional claims, branded clients and Google Account reauthentication mechanisms are not generic implementation, qualification, Issue-closure or release prerequisites.\n\nRequired authentication remains provider-neutral OIDC. #1672 qualifies ordinary management with missing/older signed authentication time as successful when the credential and explicit Role authority are valid. Requested ordinary Google OIDC login is retained separately from actual provider-neutral Server/Admin management evidence. Local deterministic fixtures do not complete real-provider/human acceptance. Google Security bundle and its extra-claim app publication/verification are outside baseline gates.\n\nThe owner approved removing the fixed five-minute requirement from ordinary management. Missing evidence remains unknown and freshness is never inferred from `iat`, callback time, consent or account selection. Invalid/future/contradictory signed evidence, expired or revoked credentials, missing permissions, CSRF and policy/admission failures remain rejected. #1672 owns the current Server/Admin implementation; #1608 G3b specifies separate high-impact trust/administrator-authority protection and machine eligibility without selecting a Google feature or exact step-up mechanism. The existing bootstrap-to-data-Role path no longer needs extra operator provisioning solely because `auth_time` is absent; optional out-of-band authority/recovery remains separately specified work.\n\nAll existing done/total counts and retained historical evidence stay unchanged. Ordinary Google login/client preparation, provider-neutral management evidence, physical-device evidence and artifact publication remain independently tracked. This scope edit authorizes no provider setup, Google app publication/verification, runtime deployment or package publication. Helm/Kubernetes remains excluded; Dart releases remain last.\n\n## Leaderless replacement qualification — new target, old evidence retained\n\n#1608 is the canonical specification and S0–S6 plan. This issue owns S6 after its protocol/session/migration gates. Baseline #1661 source/CI remains complete; leaderless source/CI and exact-target acceptance are not delivered. No prior fixed-writer test is relabeled as proof of the new architecture, and unrelated provider/device/archive/publication exits remain active.\n\n- [ ] Record the owner-selected E/B profile and completed G1–G4 design gates before freezing final acceptance. Profile E must demonstrate and disclose stale permission/session/grant behavior during partition; Profile B must prove its actual membership/quorum/certificate/control-admission algorithm and measured expiry/enforcement bound. Majority ACKs or eventual convergence alone qualify neither freshness nor global enforcement.\n- [ ] Model/property and real-wire tests: causal permutations, same/disjoint-object edits, Deny/revoke/regrant, delete/recreate, stale-admin cross-object grants and descendants, global admin invariant, atomic security bundles and explicit later conflict supersession.\n- [ ] Native multi-origin/three-node/failure-size-appropriate runs: any eligible origin/coordinator; concurrent changes; former-writer loss; partition/rejoin; incomplete/torn/corrupt/capacity/indeterminate journals; checkpoint+tail/GC/old origin/backup; membership/key rotation and mixed profile/mode/schema refusal before transfer.\n- [ ] Real Server/Admin: callback affinity/single use/loss, session failover/lag/logout/revoke/replacement, ordinary OIDC and #1672 ordinary-management success with missing/older auth_time, selected high-impact/machine policy and bootstrap explicit data Role creation/self-assignment. Optional step-up/out-of-band operator cases only if retained/selected. No credential/PII disclosure or automatic write replay.\n- [ ] Regenerated API/SDK/Admin effective-policy and change-status contracts; cache/cursor invalidation across incomparable cuts; status-first retries and retired evidence. Keep private security events out of public CDC/results and preserve original business receipt semantics. Once approved in #1608, qualify its Change ID ownership/deduplication contract: same-ID concurrent origins, response loss followed by failover, partition, conflicting intent and retired/unknown results; never execute an ambiguous or retired change afresh.\n- [ ] Paired genuine protected OFF/ON performance with matched corpus/TLS/topology/offered load, separate capacity, security/session churn, first/warm/mixed Search/BFS/PPR/Community, memory/allocations/writer delay, control/apply backlog and CDC fanout. Instrument zero per-data-RPC control network/journal/fsync. Predeclare budgets/repetitions and retain every raw failed run.\n\nPrior 51 plain/27 receipt cycle diagnostics and high shared-host load do not attribute a bottleneck to authentication or the writer. OFF/plaintext/non-Role-bound harness paths cannot qualify OIDC. The ended local Compose trial must not restart for this specification task. New final runtime/clock/quiet-host work requires a separately authorized task.\n\n## Historical fixed-writer merged-source status — 2026-10-04\n\nHistorical qualification-infrastructure source/CI: **1/1**. New #1672 policy source/CI: **0/1**. Final exact-source acceptance: **0/1**. Ordinary Google OIDC login / provider-neutral real Server/Admin ordinary management: **0/2**. Physical Android/iPhone: **0/2**. Affected publication: **0/9**. Google Security bundle is optional. Matrix criteria remain active except explicitly superseded blanket ordinary-management freshness; historical branch/simulator results do not complete new evidence.\n\n### Consolidated outstanding acceptance\n\n- [ ] From #1601: final combined native durability/capacity/recovery and lookup/compile/control persistence benchmarks, plus no per-data-RPC network/journal/fsync amplification; coordinate #1608.\n- [ ] From #1611: scoped evaluation and compilation/admission measurements for representative and configured-max Role/rule/key/batch sizes, cross-Role Deny and revision churn. Correctness tests are merged; do not weaken Deny for speed.\n- [ ] From #1615: added namespace/key bytes, allocations and exact/batch/query/replication mapping cost, with logical identity and independent-corpus/sys isolation intact.\n- [ ] From #1637: final scoped CDC/cache/rebootstrap and receipt host/performance/device matrix; independent parent/offline archives against the actual hosted parent after its publication. Paired-source override results are not hosted-archive acceptance. Coordinate #1613.\n- [ ] Resolve the Dart parent-tag/offline hosted-archive Gate cycle and stale hosted lock recorded in #1613. Parent source/isolated archive gates precede parent publication; offline hosted-parent qualification follows verified parent publication/byte equality and precedes offline publication. Cover parent/offline tags and manual-dispatch pending/qualified states without weakening independent archive/device gates. [Pinned-source review](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1610#issuecomment-5985899447).\n- [ ] Final #1612 whole-Server paired OFF/ON Search/BFS/PPR/Community first/warm/update-mixed time, allocations, retained/peak memory, writer delay, TTL/Delete/restore statistics; retain all component diagnostics and failed runs.\n- [ ] Complete #1606/#1607 actual Server/IdP browser flows and #1608/#1609 stable-clock native HA/failure/recovery/enforcement proof on one pinned source/image.\n\n### Publication dependency ledger (preparation only)\n\nAffected components are pb, Core, Go SDK, root Server/CLI, Node SDK, Admin image, Rust SDK, Dart online parent and Dart offline core: **0/9 published by this cohort**. Freeze reviewed versions and independent archive/tag contracts first. Publish pb before Go consumers; Core before its consumers; Go SDK before dependent root artifacts; Node before the packaged Admin build. Rust follows its independent owner/tag/archive gates. Dart releases are last: actual parent publication/archive equality before offline resolution/publication, with fresh exact-source Android/iPhone receipt/Head/CDC evidence. `offline_sqlite` remains unpublished. Evaluate an MCP rebuild against released pb/Go SDK if required; no MCP source change or publication is implied here. The #1613 workflow/hosted-lock repair is a prerequisite for this parent-first order; the current parent-tag Gate must not require the unpublished parent through offline hosted resolution. No tag, registry write, Release, deployment or binding/protection change is authorized by this ledger.\n\nThe final main image is not yet built/qualified. Declare numeric budgets, workload/floors, exact source/image, run order, repetitions and fresh-state/teardown requirements before expensive final families; preflight each boundary and preserve raw hashes. A prior invalid 64-consumer run and missed diagnostic Create target remain unqualified. Do not choose a passing sample or invent a fault cause.\n\nFinal whole-Server performance/HA/qualified-clock acceptance, ordinary real Google OIDC login, provider-neutral ordinary-management evidence, physical Android/iPhone and publication remain separate open exits in #1610. No deployment, tags, SDK publication, Google client/app creation or protection changes. Kubernetes/Helm is deferred; Dart releases remain last.\n\nMerged through #1661 at `93d537892f3025d4a1666dcab36c5748d5cfb9f6`; tree `fbd0278b5c400021b611e8bb0b4890668611ef9d` matches qualified head `064ef431cc5b8a3bb4d6a168c113522407c0529c`. All seven applicable exact-main workflows passed (29 successful checks, 11 expected skips), including [Go](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/actions/runs/37204816309), [CodeQL](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/actions/runs/37204816351), [Admin](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/actions/runs/37204816359), [Node](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/actions/runs/37204816338), [Rust](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/actions/runs/37204816326) and [Dart](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/actions/runs/37204816367). The 74-step mandatory local gate, 99 Admin browser contract cases and 26 CI contract tests passed; real Server Connect tests remain distinct from the browser fixture. Local manifest SHA-256: `1fdfb42c91eaeb803c7ce2a6e863b0a17459f9497b04a574b12f6b03281163d5`; exact-main completion proof SHA-256: `a29eb2c08c623641f9c151cc72285ec4685fac916811a13c620120f3e61d48c1`.\n\n\n## Historical ordinary-login and recent-auth split — #1658\n\nThe following records the delivered 2026-10-04 source contract. #1672 supersedes its ordinary-management five-minute requirement; login/evidence integrity and historical format evidence remain valid for that source. It is not the new target acceptance policy.\n\nThe user approved ordinary Code/PKCE login separately from recent-auth management on 2026-10-04. Missing auth_time is unknown; older signed evidence remains unchanged. Ordinary login/replacement does not request forced reauthentication or infer evidence from iat/callback/consent/account selection. Future or contradictory evidence is rejected. The Server-owned transaction saves step-up intent independently from ReplacesDigest. At that delivered baseline, step-up and important security changes required signed evidence within five minutes; failed step-up keeps the old session. Canonical state/replica/restart/restore and old-reader rejection must preserve this meaning (image v2, LNSEC03, native binding v2; no automatic migration or mixed-cohort rolling acceptance).\n\nOrdinary Google OIDC login and provider-neutral management qualification are separate. Google Security bundle, its optional additional claims and related Google app publication/verification are outside the baseline; they do not block implementation, acceptance, Issue closure or release. The prior plan qualified recent-authenticated management against a supporting Issuer; #1672 now replaces that requirement for ordinary management with valid OIDC and explicit Role authority. The requested ordinary Google login remains pending its standard operator Web client and human sign-in; that preparation does not require Security bundle. No provider/app-publication, merge, deploy or SDK-publication authorization is added; Dart releases remain last.\n\n## Superseding Edge policy decision — Head managed\n\nThe user adopted the Head-managed Edge model on 2026-10-04: base read is tail VertexRead AND head VertexRead; modification is tail VertexRead AND head VertexWrite. Explicit Deny/default deny remain. Independent directed prefix-pair Role configuration is withdrawn; #1626 now owns the concrete effect/response/compatibility matrix. Query/CDC/export/receipt capabilities remain independent. Head write does not grant VertexDelete or endpoint creation/update. HA new Create stays disabled.\n\nPrevious directed-policy candidate #1649 and its passing local results remain historical evidence, not qualification of this new policy. The user selected typed blind acceptance for head write-only callers. It acknowledges authorized request handling without claiming mutation success or disclosing weight/existence/collision/results. SDKs must explicitly represent unavailable details; batch/count/receipt/idempotency and current permission changes obey the same rule. Role/schema/ADR and runtime work proceed under this decision. Kubernetes/Helm remains deferred; native HA/security stays in scope.\n\n## Current task scope — Helm/Kubernetes excluded, reconfirmed 2026-10-05\n\nThe user explicitly excludes Helm/Kubernetes from this delivery because no real-cluster qualification is planned for the foreseeable future. Helm/chart implementation additions, Kubernetes setup/provisioning/deployment and cluster qualification are outside the current work. #1636 remains a separately deferred, open/Todo, P2, unscheduled backlog item; resume it only through a separately requested and scoped task.\n\nPreserve existing merged chart/security repairs and historical diagnostics. They are not real-cluster qualification. Unfinished Helm/Kubernetes work and #1636 are not prerequisites for the current source/CI, final acceptance, artifact publication or #1599 Epic completion. Existing repository CI is preserved; this scope decision adds no Helm/Kubernetes implementation or validation task.\n\nDocker/Compose/native Server HA/security/recovery validation remains in scope. Android/iPhone SDK physical-device evidence is a separate existing requirement and is unaffected by this Kubernetes decision. Runtime, provider, deployment and publication authorizations remain as already recorded.\n\nPart of #1599.\n\n**Current preparation:** may run in parallel. **Final target prerequisites:** practical #1669–#1671 repairs, #1672 policy source/CI and actual ordinary-management cases, approved #1608 S1–S5 and linked #1606/#1607/#1609/#1612/#1613 target criteria; #1614 is delivered baseline. #1668 and deferred #1636 are not blockers.\n\n## Outcome\n\nQualify #1599 on final merged source using the practical Role scenarios. Every preceding implementation PR already carries its own real-wire tests; this does not defer those tests.\n\n## Acceptance matrix\n\n- OFF no-env Server/Admin works without IdP/policy/session runtime; sys:/data: isolation still applies. Partial OIDC/native durability/authority failure cannot become OFF.\n- Namespace reader read/query only; editor/maintainer selected writes and lifecycle checks; cross-Role nested Deny; no direct user permissions; security-admin without data rights.\n- Both endpoint read / tail-read plus head-write modification, protected endpoint non-creation, OFF-only legacy endpoint creation, effective-weight/read denial, born-expired/shortened TTL, authorized expiry/cascades, mixed exact-batch rollback and authorized-subset prefix Delete/dry-run.\n- Hidden-only application mutations may change visible ranking through shared corpus statistics, but cannot disclose denied documents/nodes/edges, create traversable hidden bridges, or enter actual counts/degree aggregates/paging/CDC. Sys-only changes cannot alter business statistics. Cursor theft/Role edits/scope changes and node-local search affinity.\n- Identity CDC vs value CDC vs no CDC, mixed-scope events/hidden progress/gaps, policy-bound cursor failover/rebootstrap, no raw peer/receipt envelope, correct TTL-expiry limitations.\n- Scoped backup/export distinct from read and CDC. Receipt replay/status provenance, unknown IDs, recovery/retirement, original-result bytes and revoked-access refusal.\n- Admin Role editor and memberships, mode/scope/permission leases, stale cached page/CLI graph/autocomplete/late response and cross-tab invalidation.\n- Multi-origin HA with typed security bundles/session digests, eligible-node/per-attempt routes, selected-profile admission and revocation semantics, stale config/partition/restart and (for bounded freshness) qualified clock/suspend/certificate races; certified fenced recovery and uncertain restore refusal. The old owner/lease tests remain baseline evidence. No unproved takeover or zero-loss volume claim; business graph convergence after human revoke remains.\n- Real provider smoke records ordinary Google RP login separately from provider-neutral ordinary Server/Admin management and independently supported API access-token profiles. Missing/older auth_time succeeds for ordinary management; invalid/future/contradictory evidence fails credential validation. Test replacement without freshness inference, exact identity, permission/revocation and selected high-impact/machine cases. Optional retained step-up has separate evidence; MFA remains provider-owned. Google Security bundle is not required, and Google ID/Google-API access tokens do not qualify Lantern RFC 9068 Bearer support. Never publish secrets/PII.\n\n## Evidence and release readiness\n\n- [ ] Record final SHA, workflow IDs, raw evidence SHA-256 and sanitized verdicts in the Epic. All module/Admin/codegen gates pass.\n- [ ] Auth/prefix query/CDC/HA benchmark matrix declared before expensive runs; preserve workload/floors and exact image/fresh-state requirements.\n- [ ] Record merged-source, final acceptance, artifact publication and real-provider/device evidence independently.\n- [ ] Confirm pb-first/root/Node/Admin archive/tag/image requirements and whether other SDK runtime changes need independent releases/device evidence.\n- [ ] Verify #1609 operator guidance against the actual Admin/Server route and env contract: retired `LANTERN_PEERS` is not recommended, OFF/direct and OIDC/HTTPS same-origin proxy examples match implementation, and Helm/Kubernetes implementation, cluster qualification and deferred #1636 are excluded from the current source/acceptance/publication completion gates.\n- [ ] Only repeat costly runs after relevant changes, invalid evidence or predeclared stability checks; no passing-sample selection.\n- [ ] Document commit versus enforcement/admission/lease bounds, delivered-data limits, control availability, process versus volume durability, manual fencing and unsupported scoped families.\n## Delivery rules\n\nEvery changed external behavior needs same-PR real Connect integration coverage for a happy path and a failure/edge contract. Add hot-path benchmark scenarios where applicable. Preserve the module DAG, focused provider sub-configs, plural-canonical writes, and source/test pairing. Regenerate all affected consumers; never hand-edit generated code. Run the mandatory local gates before any push. Write all Issue content and updates in English.\n\n## Performance matrix\n\n- Paired baseline/final OFF and OFF/ON comparisons with matched logical data, workload/TLS/topology at unsaturated fixed offered load; separate sustained-capacity runs. Existing loose broad_rw floors and saturated p99 cannot demonstrate small auth overhead; preserve their floors.\n- Measure p50/p95/p99, per-producer throughput, CPU/request, allocations, prefix/key bytes, snapshot count and zero per-data-RPC authority/journal/fsync.\n- Local bearer/session admission, cold/warm JWKS/rotation/rejection floods, representative and configured-max Role/rule/key/batch sizes, deny batches and compilation/revision churn.\n- Fixed application corpus/graph plus hidden sys/data/Deny density: shared search DF/N/length totals and graph TF-IDF/BM25 weights, constrained top-k/paths, actual degree/scans/counts and graph-writer mutex/block latency. Compare first and reused scopes and representative write/delete mixtures; record allocations, retained-memory bounds, TTL maintenance and restore consistency. Different graph/index corpora stay separate; no permission-specific ranking-statistics cache is a standard path.\n- CDC 8/64/configured-max consumers, 100/10/1/0 percent visible events, slow consumers, consumer lag/starvation/filter CPU/queues/drops and producer latency.\n- Native system WAL/snapshot/apply cost, app write latency with durable sys enabled, checkpoint/compaction/session churn, multi-origin/control load scaling, selected-profile certification/revocation/expiry/recovery bursts and zero per-data-RPC coordination.\n- Declare representative cases/numeric budgets/run order/repetitions from comparable preliminary evidence before final runs; record exact source/image/topology/fresh state/CPU/raw hashes and all results. No invented slowdown percentage or correctness shortcuts.\n\n- Mixed OFF/OIDC public-serving peer, namespace-version/trust/generation mismatch, bootstrap before compatibility check and insecure rolling mode transition must reject without exposing protected data.\n\n- Sys-only durable restart with ephemeral graph state must not reuse graph origin/sequence identities; signed security continuity and transport deduplication remain distinct.\n\n\n## Final performance preflight and next prerequisite\n\nFinding from final #1661 acceptance preflight (#1610/#1612): Final performance preflight is held: the arm64 Docker VM has 16 CPUs and 8,216,018,944 B memory, while host load is 24.18/26.92/26.61 with six existing development containers. This does not meet the quiet-host prerequisite. No new final image/capacity run occurred and no existing container/job was changed. Private preflight manifest SHA-256: `a631af997f69ec4c66233f5c53eee59d302ddcf06791ab00a61734feccf520e4`; frozen exact-main source archive SHA-256: `337058a99264f0df8ac245e6c29de9d6e6883e0c9dcabb60b7a7700ae18dbb2f`. This is preparation, not a passing performance sample. Next: qualified quiet-host preflight, one pinned image and the predeclared unchanged final matrix.\n\nNo final-source performance exit is completed. Core diagnostics and all real-wire/source/CI proofs remain retained; Google/provider, physical-device and publication exits are separate, with Dart releases last.\n\nRemaining comparative-path prerequisite: current `testbed/bench/run.sh` creates `-mode off` for non-receipt scenarios; its ghz path is plaintext and unauthenticated. `shared_ranking.yaml` explicitly remains a diagnostic with a query prefix, not proof of OIDC admission. Before final #1610/#1612 OFF/ON acceptance, qualify an actual protected Role-bound comparative path with matched logical data, TLS/topology and offered load, first/warm/update-mixed Search/BFS/PPR/Community plus allocations/retained/peak memory and writer delay. Preserve diagnostic/release workloads and floors; a passing OFF scenario cannot qualify protected scope. Source: [native mode selection](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/blob/93d537892f3025d4a1666dcab36c5748d5cfb9f6/testbed/bench/run.sh#L275), [shared ranking scenario](https://github.com/anaregdesign/lantern/blob/93d537892f3025d4a1666dcab36c5748d5cfb9f6/testbed/bench/scenarios/shared_ranking.yaml). This prerequisite belongs to the existing final matrix, not a change to production auth or the frozen integration cohort.\n\n\n## Scope-alignment follow-up\n\n- [ ] Verify #1609 final wording and record ordinary OIDC management without blanket recent-auth, selected high-impact/machine policy and optional Google bundle. Preserve independent evidence buckets and non-freshness checks; historical five-minute gate tests do not prove #1672 acceptance.\n\n\n</details>\n\n\n</details>\n</details>\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1610/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/anaregdesign/lantern/issues/1610/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/lukstafi/ocannl-staging/issues/1871","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/lukstafi/ocannl-staging","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/lukstafi/ocannl-staging/issues/1871/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/lukstafi/ocannl-staging/issues/1871/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/lukstafi/ocannl-staging/issues/1871/events","html_url":"https://github.com/lukstafi/ocannl-staging/pull/1871","id":6310228917,"node_id":"PR_kwDOQyNS0s8AAAABOJNe3A","number":1871,"title":"slow-metal_gpu_hang_failure ends in a bounded time whether or not macOS kills its kernel","user":{"login":"lukstafi","id":3090721,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMwOTA3MjE=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3090721?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/lukstafi","html_url":"https://github.com/lukstafi","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lukstafi/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lukstafi/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lukstafi/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lukstafi/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lukstafi/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lukstafi/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lukstafi/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lukstafi/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lukstafi/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[],"state":"closed","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":1,"created_at":"2026-11-10T15:40:11Z","updated_at":"2026-11-10T16:29:56Z","closed_at":"2026-11-10T16:29:53Z","assignee":null,"author_association":"OWNER","active_lock_reason":null,"draft":false,"pull_request":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/lukstafi/ocannl-staging/pulls/1871","html_url":"https://github.com/lukstafi/ocannl-staging/pull/1871","diff_url":"https://github.com/lukstafi/ocannl-staging/pull/1871.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/lukstafi/ocannl-staging/pull/1871.patch","merged_at":"2026-11-10T16:29:53Z"},"body":"**`slow-metal_gpu_hang_failure` now ends in about 25 s on every Mac, with an honest verdict, instead of reaching its 300 s alarm on macOS 27.2.** The alarm was not a Metal failure the backend lost. The OS never killed the kernel. `sync` was correctly waiting for a kernel that was still running.\n\nCloses ahrefs/ocannl#2082\n\n## What the probes found\n\nA temporary raw-Metal probe, with no OCANNL in it, ran one cell per process on macbook-pro (Apple M4 Pro, macOS 27.2 26B123), in measurement legs `mbp-1` and `mbp-2`. The probe is dropped from this tree and kept on the branch `claude/w1110-2082-probe` (`test/metal_probe/run_cells.sh`). The full readings are on the issue.\n\n- A never-ending kernel, with 1 thread (OCANNL's all-serial dispatch) or 65536, was still `Scheduled` after 120 s.\n- The IORegistry `recoveryCount` stayed 0 throughout. No GPU recovery happened, so no failed status could have been lost.\n- Another process's trivial kernels kept completing beside it in under 0.1 s (116 of 116). A second queue in the same process did not.\n- Legitimate 10 s and 30 s kernels completed.\n- Exiting the process freed the GPU at once.\n- Wild writes, even 1 TiB past a buffer, and OOB writes under `MTL_SHADER_VALIDATION=1` completed with no error. So there is still no cheap way to fail a command buffer on purpose.\n\nOn 2026-11-07 the watchdog did kill this test's kernel about 3 s in, on mac-studio (M4 Max) under the same 27.2 (run `20261107T034514Z-70099`, \"Caused GPU Hang Error\"). Whether the OS kills the kernel is therefore the platform's call, under conditions not isolated here. It is not the backend's call. Cross-process contention is ruled out as the trigger. Elapsed time cannot classify a hang, which #2089 needs to know.\n\n## What changed\n\nAll in `test/operations/metal_gpu_hang_failure.ml`. No library code changed.\n\n- **The wait gets `kill_bound_s` = 20 s**, several times the ~3 s the observed kill took. Metal's `sync` waits in 100 ms slices of OCaml code, so a SIGALRM handler's exception ends `Context.sync` at the bound.\n- **Whether a kill happened is read from `recoveryCount`** (`ioreg -r -c IOAccelerator -l`), never from the backend under test. The five report claims form a `Verdict.sub_leg` that is open only where the count rose. Elsewhere they are skipped with `` `Environment `` aggregation, as `SKIPPED on a macOS that did not kill the never-ending kernel within the bound (gh-ocannl-2082)`. A backend that regresses where the OS does kill still fails those claims.\n- **New claim, evaluated on every Metal run:** \"the wait over the never-ending kernel never returned success\", meaning it raised or was still waiting at the bound. It cannot be skipped.\n- **A hard bound that needs no OCaml code to run** (from the local review): a `sh` watchdog process sends the default-action SIGTERM at 120 s, and `at_exit` retires it on a normal end. It covers a wait stuck in a native call, which defers the signal handler, and the steps after the wait. The earlier 300 s alarm used a default action and had the same property.\n- When the wait hit the bound, the read and the fresh-lineage steps are not attempted, since both would queue behind the running kernel.\n- Docs: `docs/agent-notes/backend-dialects-and-idents.md` records the platform finding and the gate. The comments on the 300 s alarm in `build-and-test.md`, `tools/per-test-cap.sh` and the dune stanza now describe the 20 s bound.\n\n## Verification\n\n| Run | Where | Result |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `mbp-3` leg, head `42233eea2` (before the review fix) | macbook-pro, Metal, `--expect-backend metal` | pass. The wait was still waiting after 20.1 s, recoveries did not rise, and the 5 report claims were SKIPPED (`~/.ocannl-test-runs/20261110T144742Z-96785` there) |\n| `mbp-3` control: Metal `sync` returns without waiting | macbook-pro | fails exactly \"the wait over the never-ending kernel never returned success\" (`20261110T144825Z-3552`) |\n| `mbp-4` leg, head `f637135da` (this head, rebased) | macbook-pro, Metal | pass (`20261110T153622Z-22199`) |\n| `mbp-4` control: Metal `sync` blocks in native `waitUntilCompleted` | macbook-pro | ended by the watchdog's SIGTERM (\"Command got signal TERM\"), run fails (`20261110T153701Z-27265`) |\n| `prepush --since origin/master` (36 aliases, cc) | mac-studio | pass (`20261110T145453Z-8747`) |\n| `dune build @check` + `@test/operations/scans` + the slow alias on cc (Metal leg skipped), rebased head | mac-studio | pass (`20261110T153328Z-10608`) |\n| `tools/test-harnesses.sh --affected` (14 harnesses; reached only through comment edits) | mac-studio | 13 passed. `test-test-run-digest.sh` failed one leg (\"a dune-refused invocation is INVOCATION REFUSED\": `wait last` saw a launch in flight), then passed on a rerun alone: a flake, unrelated to this change |\n| `tools/fmt-check.sh` | mac-studio | PASSED, gating each commit |\n| Rebased onto `9583da9a9`, after staging#1868 (#2089): `@check`, the slow alias on cc, `agent_notes_structure`, `fmt-check` | mac-studio | pass (`20261110T160335Z-47872`) |\n\n**The rebase onto #2089's merge** resolved one textual conflict, in the paragraph of `backend-dialects-and-idents.md` that both PRs extend; both texts are kept. #2089 makes `sync` raise a typed `Metal_failure.Command_buffer_failed` with the same rendered text, which the test reads through `Exn.to_string`. The Metal legs above ran before the rebase. On a Mac that does not kill the kernel, the test reaches only the `Still_waiting` path, which #2089 does not touch.\n\n**Declined:** #2089's worker suggested a claim pinning the classifier's `gpu_hang` verdict on the report (issue comment 6771441149). It would sit in the killed-kernel leg, which no current Mac opens, so no run here could evaluate it. It is left for the run that next sees a kill.\n\nThe slow alias's only `slow-` member is this test. It never ran on mac-studio, because a stalled GPU there would stall every sibling's Metal batch.\n\n## Decisions to review\n\n- **The verdict reads the OS, not the backend.** Gating the report claims on \"sync raised\" would read the value under test: a backend that stopped raising would skip instead of failing. `recoveryCount` is independent of `Metal_backend`. A recovery caused by another process during the 20 s would open the leg spuriously. That would fail loudly, not pass silently, and the test runs with the GPU to itself.\n- **20 s, not longer.** The only kill on record took ~3 s, and a run on a non-killing Mac pays the full bound. If a future macOS kills later than 20 s, the report claims skip and the never-returned-success claim still holds.\n- **No change to `Metal_backend.sync`.** A kernel nobody kills is a kernel still running, and the backend keeps waiting for it, as it does for a long legitimate one. A backend-side timeout cannot tell the two apart; the 30 s legitimate kernel shows why.\n- **The probe is not the test of record**, so it stays out of the tree.\n\n## Local Codex review\n\nOne finding, P2: the SIGALRM handler cannot run while the wait is stuck in native code, and the fallback alarm was armed only inside that handler. **Fixed** in the second commit with the separate watchdog process. The `mbp-4` control shows that watchdog ending a natively blocked `sync`.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/lukstafi/ocannl-staging/issues/1871/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":1,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/lukstafi/ocannl-staging/issues/1871/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pubky/paykit-rs/issues/254","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pubky/paykit-rs","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pubky/paykit-rs/issues/254/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pubky/paykit-rs/issues/254/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pubky/paykit-rs/issues/254/events","html_url":"https://github.com/pubky/paykit-rs/issues/254","id":6223282276,"node_id":"I_kwDOQZD5r88AAAABcu_AZA","number":254,"title":"[WIP] Draft spec: PIX payment endpoint (brl-pix-brcode) with payee-verified completion","user":{"login":"jvsena42","id":44164057,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ0MTY0MDU3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/44164057?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jvsena42","html_url":"https://github.com/jvsena42","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jvsena42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jvsena42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jvsena42/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jvsena42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jvsena42/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jvsena42/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jvsena42/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jvsena42/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jvsena42/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[],"state":"closed","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":3,"created_at":"2026-11-05T12:34:54Z","updated_at":"2026-11-10T16:28:04Z","closed_at":"2026-11-10T16:28:04Z","assignee":null,"author_association":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"> **WIP.** Draft for discussion. Nothing here is normative yet.\n\n## Problem / motivation\n\nPaykit names fiat rails in its identifier convention (`eur-sepa-iban`, `usd-revolut-tag`, `gbp-fps-sortcode`), but no fiat rail defines how a payment is made or verified. Brazilian sellers on the Market want to accept PIX, Brazil's instant payment system. Each seller would receive into their own bank account.\n\nPIX is a good first fiat rail for three reasons:\n\n- **One standard receiver API.** The Banco Central do Brasil publishes the API Pix ([bacen/pix-api](https://github.com/bacen/pix-api), v2.11.0-rc1), and banks implement it for their business customers.\n- **The payment reference rides inside the QR code.** A dynamic charge carries a `txid` the payer cannot edit, so the payee can match the payment to a request exactly.\n- **Every Brazilian bank app pays a BR Code.** The payer scans or pastes it, and their bank does not need to know about Paykit.\n\nLimits:\n\n- **Paykit wallets cannot send PIX.** The API Pix only covers receiving. Starting a payment from a third-party app needs an Open Finance initiator (ITP) licence.\n- **Only the payee can verify.** PIX has no public ledger, so no third party can check a payer-side proof. Only the payee's bank can confirm settlement.\n- **Static keys are excluded.** Payments to a plain PIX key or a static BR Code cannot be tied to a request, and Banco Inter's `GET /pix` does not list them. This draft covers dynamic charges only.\n\nRelated: #245, where the seller's side confirms receipt. DePix companion draft: #255.\n\n## Proposed solution\n\n### 1. Payment Endpoint Identifier\n\n`brl-pix-brcode`, following [payment-endpoint-identifier.md](https://github.com/pubky/paykit-rs/blob/main/specs/payment-endpoint-identifier.md): asset `brl` (ISO 4217), rail `pix`, format `brcode`.\n\nIt is used only as a request-specific Payment Endpoint in a Payment Request's `payment_endpoints` map. Payees SHOULD NOT publish it in public or Private Payment Lists, because each charge is single-use.\n\n### 2. Payment Endpoint Payload\n\n```json\n{\n  \"value\": \"00020101021226...br.gov.bcb.pix2554<psp-host>/qr/v2/<location-token>5204000053039865802BR5913<NAME>6008<CITY>62070503***6304ABCD\",\n  \"expires_at\": \"2026-11-06T12:00:00Z\"\n}\n```\n\n- `value` (required): the `pixCopiaECola` string returned by the payee's bank for a dynamic immediate charge (`cob`).\n  - It MUST be a dynamic BR Code: Point of Initiation Method `12`, with a location URL in merchant account information field 26, subfield 25.\n  - Payers MUST reject static codes.\n- `expires_at` (optional): the charge expiry (`calendario.criacao + calendario.expiracao`). It SHOULD be no later than the request's `payment_deadline` when one is set.\n- A typical dynamic BR Code is about 150 bytes, which fits the 1000-byte private message limit together with the rest of the request.\n\n### 3. Payment Request rules\n\n- **Request shape:**\n  - `amount.asset` SHOULD be `brl`, with at most 2 decimal places.\n  - Cross-asset pricing follows [payment-conversion.md](https://github.com/pubky/paykit-rs/blob/main/specs/payment-conversion.md).\n  - A `brl` request paid by `brl-pix-brcode` needs no rate.\n  - `required_app_id` MUST be non-null, as `payment_endpoints` already requires.\n- **Charge link:**\n  - The payee creates the charge with `PUT /cob/{txid}`, where `txid` is the `payment_request_id` with its hyphens removed.\n  - A UUID v4 without hyphens is 32 hex characters, which matches the API Pix `txid` pattern `[a-zA-Z0-9]{26,35}`. It links the charge to the request without any lookup table.\n- **Charge content:**\n  - `valor.original` MUST equal the requested amount after conversion.\n  - `valor.modalidadeAlteracao` MUST be `0`, so the payer cannot change the amount.\n  - `solicitacaoPagador`, the text shown in the payer's bank app, SHOULD be empty or a neutral reference. It MUST NOT carry Paykit identifiers or pubky keys.\n- **One-time only:** recurring requests are out of scope. Every Billing Period shares one `payment_endpoints` map, and a dynamic charge is single-use. Recurring PIX is Pix Automático, which runs inside the banks (see open questions).\n\n### 4. Payer behaviour\n\n1. Show `value` as a QR code and as copy-and-paste text.\n2. Do not edit or rebuild it.\n3. Do not fall back to other endpoints, as the existing `payment_endpoints` rules require.\n4. Optionally, open the device's handler for the payload.\n\nThe payer's app cannot verify settlement. It SHOULD show the request as \"awaiting payee confirmation\" until it receives a Receipt.\n\n### 5. Payee verification\n\nThe payee app or service verifies through its own bank, using the API Pix:\n\n1. Receive the `POST` callback registered with `PUT /webhook/{chave}`, or poll `GET /cob/{txid}` until the status is `CONCLUIDA`.\n2. Read the payment with `GET /pix/{endToEndId}`. Require:\n   - `txid` equals the request's derived `txid`;\n   - `valor` is at least the requested amount;\n   - `horario` is within the request's deadline and quote validity.\n3. Treat the `endToEndId` as the payment's identity. It MUST NOT complete another request.\n4. Complete the request by issuing a Receipt with `payment_request_id`. Proposed Receipt metadata:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"rail\": \"pix\",\n  \"end_to_end_id\": \"E00416968202611051200abcdefghijk\",\n  \"txid\": \"b7f9c2a16d434b0ea8d40fe2c712ab33\",\n  \"amount\": \"110.00\",\n  \"settled_at\": \"2026-11-05T12:00:03Z\"\n}\n```\n\nWebhook transport security is bank-specific and out of scope. For example, Banco Inter presents a client certificate signed by its own CA, and the receiving server checks it against Inter's `ca.crt`. Profiles MUST still require reconciliation by polling, because banks retry failed callbacks only a few times (Inter: 4 attempts over about 4 hours).\n\n### 6. Finality\n\nPIX settlement can be reversed:\n\n- The payee can refund with `PUT /pix/{e2eid}/devolucao/{id}`.\n- The payer's bank can claw funds back under MED, the BCB fraud-refund mechanism.\n\nThe spec SHOULD say that a Receipt records settlement, not finality, and that applications set their own dispute window. It SHOULD also define how a refund is signalled; see the open questions.\n\n## First implementer: Pubky Market (implementation note, not normative)\n\nThe first receiver is expected to be Pubky Market, using the components it already runs for Bitcoin. Sellers keep their own bank accounts and run no server.\n\n### Market's Bitcoin flow today\n\n1. The Market service calls `paykit-server` (`POST /marketplace/payment-requests/prepare`).\n2. `paykit-server` acts as the seller's receiving agent through a delegated key the seller grants once. It derives a per-invoice address from the seller's xpub, delivers the Paykit request to the buyer, watches the chain, and reports status to the Market.\n3. The Market also has seller-direct PayPal live. PayPal notifications are verified by the Market service (`POST /v0/paypal/ipn`), including refunds and reversals.\n\n### Proposed PIX flow on the same components\n\n1. **Seller setup.** The seller uploads their bank integration to `paykit-server`, next to the existing Paykit setup: for Inter, client ID, secret, `.crt` and `.key`. They are scoped to `cob.write cob.read pix.read webhook.write webhook.read`, so the credentials can create charges and read payments but cannot move money. This is the fiat analogue of the watch-only xpub.\n2. **Bind.** The Market asks `paykit-server` to prepare a `brl` request. `paykit-server` creates `PUT /cob/{txid}` on the seller's account and sends the request with `payment_endpoints: {\"brl-pix-brcode\": ...}`.\n3. **Buyer pays.** The web Shop shows the BR Code as a QR code and as copy-and-paste text. The buyer pays in any bank app. No Paykit-aware payer wallet is needed.\n4. **Verification.**\n   - `paykit-server` registers its own URL as the webhook for each seller's key. Inter signs all callbacks with one CA; the payload's `chave` identifies the seller.\n   - It also polls `GET /cob/{txid}` while a request is open, matching its Electrum observer.\n   - It then issues the Receipt (section 5) and reports status to the Market.\n5. **Reversals.** Refunds and MED claw-backs reuse the Market's PayPal reversal handling: record on the order, withhold digital delivery.\n\n### Market-side prerequisites\n\n- The Market currency registry has no `BRL` yet. Adding it needs exponent 2 and the `BTCBRL` feed, which Blocktank already serves (`https://api1.blocktank.to/api/fx/rates/btc`).\n- Locks-gated listings stay USD/BTC only. That is an existing Lock Server limit.\n\n### Risks for this deployment\n\n- **Seller eligibility.** A business account at a bank with an API is required. Inter's API is for business accounts. MEI eligibility is unconfirmed and should be checked first.\n- **Credential custody.** `paykit-server` holds each seller's limited-scope bank credentials. That needs encrypted storage and a security review before live use.\n- **Seller friction.** Sellers must renew the certificate yearly.\n- **One bank at launch.** Inter only; each further bank is an adapter behind the same interface.\n- **Relation to #245.** In #245's seller-confirmed direction, `paykit-server` would report what the bank said, and the seller's side would issue the Receipt.\n\n### Without a hosted service\n\nA seller's native app can do the same with no server: poll `GET /cob/{txid}` with the seller's own credentials, then issue the Receipt. Payments made while the app is closed are detected when it next opens. Browsers cannot present the bank's client certificate, so the web Shop needs the hosted path.\n\n## Protocol impact\n\n- New recommended identifier `brl-pix-brcode` and its payload convention. This is documentation only; `PaymentEndpointIdentifier` already accepts it.\n- New rule that `brl-pix-brcode` is valid only in a request's `payment_endpoints`, for one-time requests.\n- Completion is payee-verified through a Receipt, not a payer Payment Proof. This touches state derivation in [payment-requests.md](https://github.com/pubky/paykit-rs/blob/main/specs/payment-requests.md#state-derivation), because today the first valid Payment Proof completes a one-time request. See open question 1.\n- Proposed Receipt metadata fields for fiat settlement.\n\n## Downstream bindings\n\nNone for the identifier and payload. If open question 1 introduces a payee-side completion record or a payee proof kind, the SDK and its Swift/Kotlin bindings would expose it.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- **Static PIX key or static BR Code.** Rejected: payments cannot be tied to a request or verified.\n- **Payer-submitted proof carrying the `endToEndId`.** The payer would read it off their bank receipt and type it in, and no one but the payee could check it. It could be an optional hint at most.\n- **Open Finance payment initiation from the Paykit wallet.** Needs an ITP licence. Out of scope for the protocol.\n\n## Open questions\n\n1. **Completion without a payer proof.** Should a payee-issued Receipt complete a one-time request in SDK state? Or should there be a payee-side \"settled\" record, or a payee proof kind such as `pix-end-to-end-id` signed by the payee? This overlaps #245.\n2. **Refunds and MED.** How does a payee signal that a settled payment was refunded or clawed back? A corrective Receipt, a new event kind, or left to applications?\n3. **Recurring PIX.** Should Pix Automático (`/rec`, `/solicrec`, `/cobr`) map to Paykit subscriptions later, or stay out of scope? The payer's bank executes it, so Paykit allowances cannot apply.\n4. **Overpayment.** `modalidadeAlteracao = 0` prevents payers changing the amount, but should the profile say anything for banks that do not enforce it?\n5. **`txid` reuse.** If a charge expires unpaid, can the payee create a new charge for the same request? The derived `txid` would collide, so a suffix rule may be needed.\n\n## Additional context\n\n- BCB API Pix spec: https://github.com/bacen/pix-api, viewer at https://bacen.github.io/pix-api/\n- BR Code and initiation rules: BCB \"Manual de Padrões para Iniciação do Pix\", https://www.bcb.gov.br/estabilidadefinanceira/pix\n- Reference bank implementation, Banco Inter (business accounts):\n  - Docs: https://developers.inter.co. Production base URL `https://cdpj.partners.bancointer.com.br/pix/v2`, sandbox `https://cdpj-sandbox.partners.uatinter.co/pix/v2`.\n  - Auth: OAuth2 client credentials over mTLS. Scopes `cob.write cob.read pix.read webhook.write webhook.read`.\n  - The sandbox has `POST /cob/pagar/{txid}` to simulate a payment.\n  - Official SDKs: https://github.com/inter-co/pj-sdk-python (also `pj-sdk-java`, `pj-sdk-php`, `pj-sdk-csharp`).\n  - Webhook receiver example: https://github.com/inter-co/pj-webhook-server\n- Pubky Market:\n  - Shop: https://github.com/pubky/pubky-marketplace. Fiat rails design: `docs/ecommerce/fiat-rails-design.md`.\n  - Service: https://github.com/pubky/pubky-marketplace-service. Currency registry: `docs/multi-currency.md`. PayPal IPN: `docs/paypal-refund-ipn.md`.\n  - Receiving agent: https://github.com/pubky/paykit-server\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pubky/paykit-rs/issues/254/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pubky/paykit-rs/issues/254/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":"not_planned","pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NETIX-AI-OSS/netix-protocol-core/issues/13","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NETIX-AI-OSS/netix-protocol-core","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NETIX-AI-OSS/netix-protocol-core/issues/13/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NETIX-AI-OSS/netix-protocol-core/issues/13/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NETIX-AI-OSS/netix-protocol-core/issues/13/events","html_url":"https://github.com/NETIX-AI-OSS/netix-protocol-core/pull/13","id":6135630622,"node_id":"PR_kwDOS54zC88AAAABL9_uGA","number":13,"title":"fix(mqtt): replace custom vendor with maintained published MQTT client","user":{"login":"prafiles","id":1404592,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE0MDQ1OTI=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1404592?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/prafiles","html_url":"https://github.com/prafiles","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/prafiles/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/prafiles/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/prafiles/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/prafiles/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/prafiles/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/prafiles/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/prafiles/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/prafiles/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/prafiles/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[],"state":"closed","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":0,"created_at":"2026-10-31T13:58:55Z","updated_at":"2026-11-10T16:17:15Z","closed_at":"2026-10-31T17:28:15Z","assignee":null,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"draft":false,"pull_request":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NETIX-AI-OSS/netix-protocol-core/pulls/13","html_url":"https://github.com/NETIX-AI-OSS/netix-protocol-core/pull/13","diff_url":"https://github.com/NETIX-AI-OSS/netix-protocol-core/pull/13.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/NETIX-AI-OSS/netix-protocol-core/pull/13.patch","merged_at":"2026-10-31T17:28:15Z"},"body":"Replace the local rumqttc 0.25.1 vendor dependency with the published, maintained `rumqttc-v4-next` 0.34.0 client. The original upstream client still requires vulnerable rustls-webpki 0.102; this migration resolves rustls-webpki 0.103.16 from crates.io and removes the custom vendor tree.\n\nThe explicit v4 package preserves MQTT 3.1.1. Port construction and publish options to the documented public API while retaining bounded queues, QoS 1, retain, credentials, custom CA/mTLS configuration, reconnect counters and backpressure handling. Reject keepalive values exceeding the MQTT protocol limit with a configuration error.\n\nValidation:\n- `cargo fmt --all -- --check` passed.\n- `cargo test --locked -p republish-core --no-default-features` passed: 191 tests.\n- `cargo clippy --locked -p republish-core --no-default-features --all-targets -- -D warnings` passed.\n- Real loopback TLS regression tests confirm MQTT 3.1.1 CONNECT/auth, retained QoS 1 publishing and PUBACK accounting, and rejection of untrusted CAs and mismatched server names.\n- Inspected Cargo.lock: published v4-next/core-next 0.34.0, webpki 0.103.16, no rumqttc 0.25 or custom MQTT source.\n\nClient selection and API reference: [published v4-next 0.34.0](https://docs.rs/rumqttc-v4-next/0.34.0/rumqttc/) and [maintainer migration guide](https://github.com/thehouseisonfire/rumqtt/blob/main/MIGRATION.md). The default rumqttc-next facade is MQTT 5 and is deliberately not used here. Consumers can pin this PR's immutable commit until a separately authorized library release.\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NETIX-AI-OSS/netix-protocol-core/issues/13/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NETIX-AI-OSS/netix-protocol-core/issues/13/timeline","performed_via_github_app":{"id":1144995,"client_id":"Iv23liVemv8A9if9v0F2","slug":"chatgpt-codex-connector","node_id":"A_kwHOAOQ6Gs4AEXij","owner":{"login":"openai","id":14957082,"node_id":"MDEyOk9yZ2FuaXphdGlvbjE0OTU3MDgy","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/14957082?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai","html_url":"https://github.com/openai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/received_events","type":"Organization","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"name":"ChatGPT Codex Connector","description":"Bring ChatGPT and Codex to your GitHub repositories.","external_url":"https://www.chatgpt.com","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/chatgpt-codex-connector","created_at":"2025-02-14T01:37:05Z","updated_at":"2026-09-23T18:52:59Z","permissions":{"actions":"write","checks":"read","contents":"write","emails":"read","issues":"write","metadata":"read","pull_requests":"write","statuses":"read","workflows":"write"},"events":["check_run","check_suite","commit_comment","issues","issue_comment","pull_request","pull_request_review","pull_request_review_comment","pull_request_review_thread","repository","status","sub_issues"]},"state_reason":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NETIX-AI-OSS/bacnet-republisher/issues/9","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NETIX-AI-OSS/bacnet-republisher","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NETIX-AI-OSS/bacnet-republisher/issues/9/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NETIX-AI-OSS/bacnet-republisher/issues/9/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NETIX-AI-OSS/bacnet-republisher/issues/9/events","html_url":"https://github.com/NETIX-AI-OSS/bacnet-republisher/pull/9","id":6135296738,"node_id":"PR_kwDOSx-3dc8AAAABL9vPkQ","number":9,"title":"Migrate MQTT client to maintained release fixing webpki alerts","user":{"login":"prafiles","id":1404592,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE0MDQ1OTI=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1404592?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/prafiles","html_url":"https://github.com/prafiles","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/prafiles/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/prafiles/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/prafiles/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/prafiles/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/prafiles/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/prafiles/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/prafiles/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/prafiles/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/prafiles/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[],"state":"closed","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":0,"created_at":"2026-10-31T13:24:58Z","updated_at":"2026-11-10T16:16:45Z","closed_at":"2026-10-31T17:28:48Z","assignee":null,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"draft":false,"pull_request":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NETIX-AI-OSS/bacnet-republisher/pulls/9","html_url":"https://github.com/NETIX-AI-OSS/bacnet-republisher/pull/9","diff_url":"https://github.com/NETIX-AI-OSS/bacnet-republisher/pull/9.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/NETIX-AI-OSS/bacnet-republisher/pull/9.patch","merged_at":"2026-10-31T17:28:48Z"},"body":"Migrate the MQTT 3.1.1 publisher to the maintained, published `rumqttc-v4-next 0.34.0` client. Its registry dependency graph uses patched `rustls-webpki 0.103.16`; the NETIX custom rumqttc backport is removed. Port the documented client builder, keep-alive and publish APIs while preserving credentials, TLS roots/mTLS, QoS 1, retain and queue behavior. The existing BACnet transport patch is unchanged.\n\nValidation on migration commit `139a8231a34644b4b0e7b6e7a715692216736d4d`: [CI run 41208626501](https://github.com/NETIX-AI-OSS/bacnet-republisher/actions/runs/41208626501) passed formatting, strict all-target clippy and locked tests. Local `cargo test --all-targets` also passed (87 unit tests and four BACnet loopback integration tests; three existing simulator-dependent tests remain ignored). `git diff --check` passes. An independent audit of all four original alert ranges finds no vulnerable resolved versions.\n\nThe shared client migration has wire-level MQTT 3.1.1/auth/QoS1/retain/PUBACK and TLS unknown-CA/hostname rejection tests in [protocol-core #13](https://github.com/NETIX-AI-OSS/netix-protocol-core/pull/13). That supporting PR has all 19 CI checks passing on `81a822c`.\n\nUpstream package: https://docs.rs/rumqttc-v4-next/0.34.0/rumqttc/\nAlerts: https://github.com/NETIX-AI-OSS/bacnet-republisher/security/dependabot","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NETIX-AI-OSS/bacnet-republisher/issues/9/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NETIX-AI-OSS/bacnet-republisher/issues/9/timeline","performed_via_github_app":{"id":1144995,"client_id":"Iv23liVemv8A9if9v0F2","slug":"chatgpt-codex-connector","node_id":"A_kwHOAOQ6Gs4AEXij","owner":{"login":"openai","id":14957082,"node_id":"MDEyOk9yZ2FuaXphdGlvbjE0OTU3MDgy","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/14957082?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai","html_url":"https://github.com/openai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai/received_events","type":"Organization","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"name":"ChatGPT Codex Connector","description":"Bring ChatGPT and Codex to your GitHub repositories.","external_url":"https://www.chatgpt.com","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/chatgpt-codex-connector","created_at":"2025-02-14T01:37:05Z","updated_at":"2026-09-23T18:52:59Z","permissions":{"actions":"write","checks":"read","contents":"write","emails":"read","issues":"write","metadata":"read","pull_requests":"write","statuses":"read","workflows":"write"},"events":["check_run","check_suite","commit_comment","issues","issue_comment","pull_request","pull_request_review","pull_request_review_comment","pull_request_review_thread","repository","status","sub_issues"]},"state_reason":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ceph/ceph/issues/72081","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ceph/ceph","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ceph/ceph/issues/72081/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ceph/ceph/issues/72081/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ceph/ceph/issues/72081/events","html_url":"https://github.com/ceph/ceph/pull/72081","id":5581141828,"node_id":"PR_kwDOACNBX88AAAABFFzMfA","number":72081,"title":"squid: rgw/kafka: add mTLS client certificate auth support for Kafka notifications","user":{"login":"jrse","id":17491894,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE3NDkxODk0","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/17491894?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jrse","html_url":"https://github.com/jrse","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jrse/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jrse/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jrse/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jrse/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jrse/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jrse/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jrse/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jrse/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jrse/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":141186344,"node_id":"MDU6TGFiZWwxNDExODYzNDQ=","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ceph/ceph/labels/documentation","name":"documentation","color":"fef2c0","default":true,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":144329618,"node_id":"MDU6TGFiZWwxNDQzMjk2MTg=","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ceph/ceph/labels/rgw","name":"rgw","color":"00ffe4","default":false,"description":null,"archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":10833005364,"node_id":"LA_kwDOACNBX88AAAAChbKLNA","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ceph/ceph/labels/releng-audit-fail","name":"releng-audit-fail","color":"db0e11","default":false,"description":"Release engineering: failed backport verification audit.","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":11919631940,"node_id":"LA_kwDOACNBX88AAAACxncmRA","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ceph/ceph/labels/ci-approved","name":"ci-approved","color":"3EB2A5","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ceph/ceph/milestones/20","html_url":"https://github.com/ceph/ceph/milestone/20","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ceph/ceph/milestones/20/labels","id":10526400,"node_id":"MI_kwDOACNBX84AoJ7A","number":20,"title":"squid","description":null,"creator":{"login":"cbodley","id":1928002,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5MjgwMDI=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1928002?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cbodley","html_url":"https://github.com/cbodley","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cbodley/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cbodley/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cbodley/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cbodley/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cbodley/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cbodley/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cbodley/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cbodley/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cbodley/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"open_issues":125,"closed_issues":1597,"state":"open","created_at":"2024-02-07T15:47:09Z","updated_at":"2026-11-10T17:53:03Z","due_on":null,"closed_at":null},"comments":3,"created_at":"2026-09-25T09:49:50Z","updated_at":"2026-11-10T16:15:47Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"draft":false,"pull_request":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ceph/ceph/pulls/72081","html_url":"https://github.com/ceph/ceph/pull/72081","diff_url":"https://github.com/ceph/ceph/pull/72081.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/ceph/ceph/pull/72081.patch","merged_at":null},"body":"QA tests (test_bn.py, kafka-security.sh, README.rst) were dropped from this backport per request; only code and documentation are included.\r\n\r\nfeat(rgw/kafka): add mTLS client certificate authentication for Kafka notifications\r\n\r\nAdd support for mutual TLS (mTLS) client certificate authentication when publishing bucket notifications to Kafka brokers. RGW can now present a client certificate and private key to authenticate with brokers that require ssl.client.auth=required.\r\nChanges:\r\n- Add ssl-certificate-location, ssl-key-location, and ssl-key-password topic attributes for configuring client certificates\r\n- Validate that ssl_certificate and ssl_key are provided together\r\n- Add kafka-security.sh script for generating broker and client TLS certs\r\n- Include ssl_key_password in connection identity (hash/equality)\r\n- Add mTLS test (test_notification_kafka_security_ssl_mtls) using use_mtls=True flag on the existing SSL security path\r\n- Update RGW notifications documentation with mTLS parameters\r\n\r\nFixes: http://tracker.ceph.com/issues/67427\r\n\r\n(cherry picked from commit 227d40105bb62aaefb3bdaefed893de493f08810)\r\n\r\nConflicts:\r\n\tsrc/rgw/rgw_kafka.cc\r\n - squid's Manager::connect() signature used different indentation and a trailing space on the URL parameter; resolved to the incoming version","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ceph/ceph/issues/72081/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/ceph/ceph/issues/72081/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/atedeg/mdm/issues/21","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/atedeg/mdm","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/atedeg/mdm/issues/21/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/atedeg/mdm/issues/21/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/atedeg/mdm/issues/21/events","html_url":"https://github.com/atedeg/mdm/issues/21","id":1303521160,"node_id":"I_kwDOHppZ585NsieI","number":21,"title":"Dependency Dashboard","user":{"login":"renovate[bot]","id":29139614,"node_id":"MDM6Qm90MjkxMzk2MTQ=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/2740?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/renovate%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/renovate","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/renovate%5Bbot%5D/followers","follo